{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 612, "key": "leaderboard_decomposed (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-11: Built on stale notebook badges. The 0.966 hack-family scores are frozen pre-rescore badges, not live; those authors sit at 0.881-0.939. Rank 1's 0.970 is a LIVE post-patch score, so no fraudulent headroom exists on the board.)", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The hack cannot raise adjusted_edge_jaccard - it is neutral-to-slightly-negative there.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 69, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 621, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "DECISIVE. The hack raises ONLY the division term. Decomposing the board: honest 0.947 stack implies adj_edge ~0.945; the 0.966 hack family implies ~0.871; rank 1 (0.970) implies ~0.875. The leaders' real EDGE tracking is markedly WORSE than the honest stack's - they trade quality for a fraudulent +0.095. An honest pipeline at edge 0.945 needs only divJ 0.25 to TIE rank 1 and 0.335 to reach 0.980, both under the measured post-processing-only ceiling of 0.589."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 613, "key": "patch_risk_is_our_asymmetry (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-11: The patch already happened on 2026-07-17, rescore complete 2026-07-23. There is no pending patch to benefit from and no exploited band left to collapse.)", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "# This is a METRIC EXPLOIT ... Public score; does NOT commit to final-2. Patchable.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 69, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 621, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "If the host patches this before the private rerun - it already patched the weakly-connected-component exploit on 2026-07-18 and rescored - the whole 0.95-0.97 public band collapses to its true edge term ~0.87, while an honest 0.945 + divJ 0.335 = 0.9785 is untouched. The exploit is the incumbents' risk and our asymmetry."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 614, "key": "host_patched_it_in_july", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The patch (now public in the repo) requires divisions to be genuine strongly-connected parent-to-two-daughter structures matched within the 7 um distance radius. The far-away synthetic forks now register as false positives instead of true positives", "review_after": null, "source_id": 70, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric-exploit", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "HOST, 2026-07-18 (topic 727154): the division-Jaccard exploit was found, patched, and ALL submissions rescored. Pre-patch, matching relied on weakly-connected-component structure and did not require a genuine parent-two-daughter fork near a real GT division. The patch requires divisions to be genuine STRONGLY-connected parent->two-daughter structures matched within the 7um radius, so far-away synthetic forks now register as FALSE POSITIVES rather than true positives."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 615, "key": "exploit_described_by_a_competitor", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "(My current third place exploits the metric hack as I tested it last night.) ... a single \"hub\" node placed at coordinates far outside the data ... and dropping the real forks from the tracks.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 70, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric-exploit", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A competitor who was then 3rd described the exploit publicly: a hub node far outside the volume (e.g. -10000,-10000,-10000) edged to the root of every track component, merging the prediction into one weakly-connected component; synthetic fork chains at the same far coordinates; AND dropping the real forks from the tracks. Pushed division Jaccard from ~0 to ~1.0."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "medium", "id": 616, "key": "post_patch_variant_is_live (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-11: Resolved: no residual variant survives. A 2026-09-03 re-run of the hub at 2x scale against the patched scorer scored 0.939, below the clean plateau.)", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Validated offline on host_metric replica: VAL 0.8388 -> 0.9203 (+0.0815).", "review_after": null, "source_id": 70, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 621, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric-exploit", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "OPEN CONTRADICTION to resolve. The host patched on 2026-07-18, yet notebooks pulled 2026-09-11 still inject the hub and score 0.950-0.966, with an author's own note claiming +0.0815 measured against the PATCHED scorer replica. The residual mechanism per the teardown is component merging: the hub makes real division evidence pass _has_stage_coverage, and far-away forks still contribute zero FP because FP counting requires a matched node. VERIFY against support/division_metrics.py before relying on either reading."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 617, "key": "base_rates_kill_distance_gates", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A gate wide enough to admit the median division admits hundreds of continuations for every division it catches. The failure is a base rate, not an overlap you can tune away.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 70, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED FROM LABELS (topic 740573, reproducible without a model): 73 files, 36 divisions, 25,661 continuation edges. Continuation step median 1.724um (IQR 0.908-2.188, p99 6.906); division step median 4.569um (IQR 3.300-6.214, p90 7.081); sister separation median 8.849um (IQR 7.196-10.235, max 14.653). The division IQR sits ENTIRELY INSIDE the ordinary continuation range. Continuations above each threshold vs divisions: >5um 722:30 (24:1), >7um 241:8 (30:1), >9um 82:3 (27:1), >10um 38:1 (38:1). A distance gate wide enough to admit the median division admits hundreds of continuations per division caught."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "medium", "id": 618, "key": "appearance_is_a_real_but_weak_signal", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Peak intensity separates them at AUC 0.73 for the parent at t, then 0.68, 0.70, 0.70 for t+1 to t+3 ... elongation measure does not separate at all: 0.34, 0.48, 0.58, 0.56, which is chance.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 70, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Division APPEARANCE separates measurably but weakly: peak intensity gives AUC 0.73 for the parent at the split frame, then 0.68/0.70/0.70 at t+1..t+3; local contrast 0.55/0.65/0.69/0.64. An intensity-weighted ELONGATION measure does NOT separate at all (0.34-0.58, i.e. chance) at 1.625um z spacing. Small n - 8 parents, 16 daughters - so treat as a first look."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 619, "key": "unannotated_divisions_exist", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "bright thin lines at time t are new cell divisions that happen before t ... i think many cell divisions are not annotated in the Kaggle data.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 70, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A strong competitor (hengck23) argues many real divisions are NOT annotated in the Kaggle labels, and that a bright thin line at time t marks a division a few frames earlier - visible in the ultrack paper's external data. Also warns the sparse annotation may record a 'long parent' as short. If true, the labelled 151 divisions understate the true rate and a detector trained only on them is trained on a biased subset."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 620, "key": "focus3d_pretrain_and_adapt", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "1) Just pretrain with Focus3D annotation 2) find matches (hit) of Focus3D and Kaggle zyx to compute diff dzyx 3) have two heads ... Kagge zyx = Focus3D +dzyx", "review_after": null, "source_id": 70, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FOCUS-3D (HuggingFace: Qinghua-thu/FOCUS-3D) is a 3D cell segmentation model the forum rates highly, with a stated adaptation recipe: pretrain on Focus3D annotation, match Focus3D and Kaggle zyx to compute diff dzyx, then use TWO HEADS - one predicting Focus3D zyx, one predicting dzyx in micrometres - so Kaggle zyx = Focus3D + dzyx. Classical OOD domain-calibration trick for few-data regimes. Also supports human-in-the-loop point annotation, so it can hand-label divisions to train a division detector."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 621, "key": "exploit_is_DEAD_since_2026_07_23", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "outwrest LB rank 2235 score 0.881, verified in lb_snapshot 2026-09-11, against a notebook badge of 0.950", "review_after": null, "source_id": 71, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric-exploit", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE EXPLOIT IS DEAD. Patched in commit aa65e90 on 2026-07-17 23:47 UTC, ~8h after it was reported, and ALL submissions rescored by 2026-07-23 01:16 UTC. Verified independently against the live leaderboard: outwrest, author of the original hack notebook whose badge still reads 0.950, sits at rank 2235 with 0.881. A competitor re-ran the hub against the PATCHED scorer on 2026-09-03 at 2x scale (3000 roots, 20 forks) and scored 0.939 - BELOW the 0.9465 clean plateau, so the hub now contributes nothing. Rescore screenshots: hack 0.950->0.877 (-0.073), four clean submissions 0.903->0.902 (-0.001)."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 622, "key": "patch_had_an_undocumented_second_defence (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-11: no out-degree cap exists; I verified against every version of the source. The real drops are non-consecutive-frame edges and merge duplicates.)", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "any node with >2 outgoing edges has all but two dropped", "review_after": null, "source_id": 71, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 633, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric-exploit", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The patch has TWO defences. Beyond the documented 'merely sharing a weakly connected component is not sufficient', it adds an OUT-DEGREE CAP the forum never mentioned: any node with more than 2 outgoing edges has all but two dropped, which structurally destroys a many-root hub before the division logic runs. DIRECTLY RELEVANT TO OUR DESIGN - a legitimate fork must have exactly 2 outgoing edges or it is silently truncated."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 623, "key": "kaggle_evaluation_page_is_STALE", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "the predicted graph is checked for a connected component that covers the pre-split stage", "review_after": null, "source_id": 71, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "ACTIONABLE: the competition's Evaluation page still describes the PRE-PATCH rule - 'the predicted graph is checked for a connected component that covers the pre-split stage' - which aa65e90 removed on 2026-07-17. Design to metrics.md and the vendored source, NEVER to the Kaggle Evaluation page, or you are designing against the exploitable metric."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 624, "key": "edge_term_has_no_ceiling_and_is_sanctioned", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Host: max score is 1.1; >1.0 is expected behaviour", "review_after": null, "source_id": 71, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The one genuinely unpatched asymmetry is NOT the division term but adj_edge_jaccard: floored at 0, no ceiling, so under-predicting nodes multiplies the score above 1 (worked example 1.0102). SANCTIONED, not an exploit - the host confirmed on 2026-07-14 that max score is 1.1 and >1.0 is expected."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "medium", "id": 625, "key": "no_disqualification_statement_exists", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "no host post on disqualification exists across all 102 topics", "review_after": null, "source_id": 71, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "rules", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "NEGATIVE FINDING, censused across all 102 topics: the host has made NO statement about disqualifying exploiting submissions, and NONE about which metric version the private rerun uses. The entire remedy was the patch plus rescore. The repo's last commit is the 2026-07-17 patch, so absent an unannounced change, public and private use the same version."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 626, "key": "the_real_lever_is_linker_capacity_not_a_detector", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "so every fork the ILP produced is destroyed there", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE PLAN CHANGES. Divisions are not missed for want of a division model - they are DESTROYED downstream. Evidence: a competitor measured parent+both daughters detected 7/7, both edges present 0/7, parent out-degree 1 every time. The public 0.938+ family rebuilds its edge list from a Hungarian assignment BEFORE safe_div runs, so every fork the ILP produced is destroyed there. And fork PRICING makes a fork non-optimal: Ultrack's reference configs price a division IDENTICALLY to a disappearance (-0.1/-0.1, -0.001/-0.001) while the public stack prices 1.2 against a disappearance of 1.5, under which a fork can never win. The lever is out-degree capacity, fork pricing and detector localisation - NOT a division detector, NOT wider gates, NOT an appearance feature."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 627, "key": "gates_are_already_correct_on_true_geometry", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "99.1% of divisions have both arcs inside 7.0 um; 99.9% inside 9.0 um", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "REFUTES the widen-the-gates plan. Measured on a DENSE lineage (zebrahub ZSNS003: 4,057,611 nodes, 15,868 real divisions, 105x the Kaggle labelled sample, same lab, same organism): 99.1% of divisions have BOTH arcs inside 7.0um and 99.9% inside 9.0um; scaled to Kaggle cadence ~9.2um reaches ~99%. The public stack's 9.0um parent / 14.0um sister gates are already about right. The earlier 2-of-7-at-7um figure measured DETECTOR LOCALISATION ERROR, not division geometry."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 628, "key": "base_rates_are_far_worse_than_the_forum_measured", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Continuation:division above 7 um is 203:1, above 9 um 875:1, above 10 um 929:1", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "On dense data the base rate is catastrophic for distance gates: continuation:division above 7um is 203:1, above 9um 875:1, above 10um 929:1 - against the forum's 30:1/27:1/38:1 from sparse labels. A LONG parent step is evidence AGAINST a division. Also: parent motion history is chance - the parent's step is flat at 1.315um for 8 frames before the split, dipping only to 1.242um on the final step against a population median of 1.317um. Kill that feature."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 629, "key": "false_positive_forks_are_mostly_FREE", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Otherwise, unmatched, structurally valid forks with no local GT evidence are ignored.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FP is far narrower than the forum believes. fp_forks = (considered | evaluable_forks | invalid_forks) - tp_forks, and evaluable_forks requires the fork to match a GT node within 7um WHOSE GT NODE STILL HAS AN OUTGOING EDGE. With only ~2.8% of nuclei annotated, a fork on an unannotated cell costs NOTHING. Only four things cost you: forking on an annotated continuing node, near-missing a real GT division window, bridging two different annotated GT components, or a merged branch. This reframes the fear of high fork volume - and means predicting the unannotated divisions is free."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 630, "key": "unannotated_divisions_quantified", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "one division edge in 124 links dense vs one in 853 in the Kaggle labels", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "hengck23's claim that many divisions are unannotated now has a number: one division edge in 124 links on dense zebrahub against one in 853 in the Kaggle labels - a ~6.9x deficit, and the finer dense cadence biases AGAINST that direction, so the true deficit is likely larger. A detector trained on the 151 labelled divisions is trained on annotator-selected easy cases."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 631, "key": "only_one_external_set_matches_voxel_scale", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "all six zebrahub single-objective acquisitions are 1.24 x 0.439 x 0.439 um", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "All six zebrahub single-objective acquisitions are 1.24 x 0.439 x 0.439 um - NONE match Kaggle. Only royerlab/ultrack/zebrafish_embryo.ome.zarr matches (1.625 x 0.40625 x 0.40625), the Ultrack paper's 'Sparse zebrafish'. FOCUS-3D is a Mask2Former/MAE-3D instance segmenter with a one-shot upload-a-TIFF Space and has NO human-in-the-loop point annotation - it is not the hand-labelling route the forum described, though it could supply instance masks for Ultrack/HOCT."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 632, "key": "support_dir_was_the_PRE_PATCH_scorer", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "support/division_metrics.py weak=2 strong=0 lines=450; reference copy weak=0 strong=2 lines=574", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "LANDMINE, fixed 2026-09-11. support/metrics.py and support/division_metrics.py were byte-identical to commit 7396b7e (2026-07-08), the PRE-PATCH scorer - _weakly_connected_components, 450 lines, no _is_strongly_connected_division. Anything validated against them is calibrated to the CLOSED exploit: they score a far-away synthetic fork as a TP where the live metric scores it as an FP. Replaced with the patched versions; originals kept under support/PRE-PATCH-DO-NOT-USE/ for forensics. Detect with: grep -c _is_strongly_connected_division (patched 2, pre-patch 0)."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 633, "key": "patch_drops_nonconsecutive_and_merge_dup_edges", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Drop edges that do not connect consecutive frames ... Keep only the edge with the lowest EDGE_ID per matched GT edge and discard the rest with a warning.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric-exploit", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CORRECTION of my own earlier claim. There is NO out-degree cap in the patch - I grepped every version and the only out_degree usage is >=2 as the definition of a predicted fork, not a cap that drops edges. What the patch DOES drop: (1) edges that do not connect consecutive frames, i.e. t_target - t_source != 1, removing backward and frame-skipping edges; (2) merge duplicates - when several predicted nodes match the same GT node, only the edge with the lowest EDGE_ID per matched GT edge is kept."}
