{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 644, "key": "div/48eaff3a: hengck23 could not get good division results from appearance alone and is looking for another signal, after ma", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "My first instinct is to find the upper bound of achievable division Jaccard. I would use the human correct rate as a proxy or oracle. From my observations, just by appearance above alone, i don't get good results. I need to think of something else.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "hengck23 could not get good division results from appearance alone and is looking for another signal, after manually inspecting crop series along trajectories."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 645, "key": "div/5dc64261: The acquisition frame rate is too coarse to see a cell slowly split: a division appears as a cell suddenly bec", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "the frame rate seems to be too large for us to observe the slow splitting of a cell. It is like the cell \"suddenly becomes two (and far apart)\"", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The acquisition frame rate is too coarse to see a cell slowly split: a division appears as a cell suddenly becoming two objects already far apart, which is why continuous-deformation cues fail."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 646, "key": "div/07616ecc: hengck23's only reliable human cue for a division is exclusion from context: confidence in the neighbouring tr", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "i can only be very sure it is a division when the neighbour track is very obvious, so that since the parent cell cannot disappear, it must be split. This means that IPL can only make a correct division only if the neighbour tracks has very high confidence?", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "hengck23's only reliable human cue for a division is exclusion from context: confidence in the neighbouring tracks, since the parent cannot simply disappear - implying divisions are only recoverable where surrounding links are already high-confidence."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 647, "key": "div/6f8328b9: Even for a human, a division can be hard to distinguish from a false positive link: hengck23 describes a divis", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "For division, I can sometimes see 2 \"different series being concatenated together\". In fact, it may be difficult for humans to tell if it is FP or division?", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Even for a human, a division can be hard to distinguish from a false positive link: hengck23 describes a division as looking like two different crop series concatenated together."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 648, "key": "div/0a360334: Measured on 8 labelled parents and 299 ordinary nodes from 5 films: peak intensity separates divisions at AUC ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Peak intensity separates them at AUC 0.73 for the parent at t, then 0.68, 0.70, 0.70 for t+1 to t+3. Local contrast 0.55, 0.65, 0.69, 0.64. An intensity weighted elongation measure does not separate at all: 0.34, 0.48, 0.58, 0.56, which is chance.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Measured on 8 labelled parents and 299 ordinary nodes from 5 films: peak intensity separates divisions at AUC 0.73 at the split frame (0.68-0.70 for daughters t+1..t+3), local contrast 0.55-0.69, and elongation is at chance (0.34-0.58)."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 649, "key": "div/6da4a2b7: Labelled divisions do NOT cluster in time within a film: across 25 films with labelled divisions, division tim", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Synchrony, labels only: 25 films have labelled divisions, 8 have two or more. Within one film the labelled division times spread by a median of 32 frames (8 to 38) out of 100. So the labelled divisions do not cluster.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Labelled divisions do NOT cluster in time within a film: across 25 films with labelled divisions, division times spread by a median of 32 frames out of 100 - evidence against hengck23's 'find one and you find many' synchrony claim, on the labelled subset."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 650, "key": "div/23510a96: Motion statistics calibrated on the real lineage edges: 1.86 um/frame median step, +0.30 lag-1 directional per", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Motion is calibrated on the real lineage edges: 1.86 µm/frame median step, +0.30 lag-1 directional persistence, 7.24 µm sister separation.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Motion statistics calibrated on the real lineage edges: 1.86 um/frame median step, +0.30 lag-1 directional persistence, 7.24 um sister separation - the persistence term is a multi-frame signal available before any fork is proposed."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 651, "key": "div/0ea26e93: Broken continuations sit at the same separation as real sister pairs (orphan median 8.5-10 um vs 7.24 um calib", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Jose calibrated sister separation at 7.24 um from the real lineage edges; my orphan median is 8.5-10 um. A distance gate cannot separate the two populations. Whatever decides daughter-vs-broken-track has to use something else", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Broken continuations sit at the same separation as real sister pairs (orphan median 8.5-10 um vs 7.24 um calibrated sister separation), so no distance gate can separate daughters from linker failures - the decision must use appearance, motion history, or better localisation."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 652, "key": "div/0ce8857c: Both Ultrack zebrafish reference configs use max_distance 10.0 and max_neighbors 5 with small balanced event p", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Both zebrafish configs in the supplementary repo use max_distance 10.0 and max_neighbors 5, and the event penalties are small and balanced: large_zebrafish has appear 0.002, disappear 0.001, division 0.001; sparse_zebrafish has all three at 0.1.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Both Ultrack zebrafish reference configs use max_distance 10.0 and max_neighbors 5 with small balanced event penalties (division 0.001 or 0.1), whereas the public Kaggle lineage fork sets division 1.2 / disappearance 1.5 / appearance 0, under which a fork can never be optimal."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 653, "key": "div/b99da7cc: In the public Kaggle lineage pipeline the division penalty is set so high relative to disappearance that a for", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The public lineage I forked runs division at 1.2 and disappearance at 1.5 with appearance at 0. I had already worked out on my own solver's objective that a fork can never be optimal at those values", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "In the public Kaggle lineage pipeline the division penalty is set so high relative to disappearance that a fork can never be the optimal solution of the objective."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 654, "key": "div/e26371b2: An asymmetric gate is needed: allowing out-degree 2 is necessary but not sufficient, because a continuation-tu", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Allowing out-degree 2 was necessary and not sufficient, because a gate tuned for continuations rejects the second arc before capacity ever gets consulted. What the numbers say is that the second arc needs a different gate from the first: tight for a continuation", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "An asymmetric gate is needed: allowing out-degree 2 is necessary but not sufficient, because a continuation-tuned distance gate rejects the second arc before capacity is consulted; the second arc needs roughly double the first arc's radius."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 655, "key": "div/4e5b76bf: Measured reachability of both division arcs versus gate radius on 7 labelled divisions: 2/7 inside 7.0 um, 5/7", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "both steps inside a 7.0 um gate 2/7 (29%) both steps inside a 9.9 um gate 5/7 (71%) both steps inside a 12.0 um gate 6/7 (86%) both steps inside a 15.0 um gate 7/7 (100%)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Measured reachability of both division arcs versus gate radius on 7 labelled divisions: 2/7 inside 7.0 um, 5/7 inside 9.9 um, 6/7 inside 12.0 um, 7/7 inside 15.0 um (predicted-node positions, so detector error is included)."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 656, "key": "div/a78804b8: Argument for solving divisions inside the optimisation rather than post hoc: a wide gate inside a competitive ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "If division and non-division hypotheses compete inside one optimisation, the wide gate only gets used where the alternative explanation is worse, so you do not pay for it everywhere. Doing it afterwards, the way my pipeline does, the gate is global and every widening buys false positives", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Argument for solving divisions inside the optimisation rather than post hoc: a wide gate inside a competitive objective is only used where the non-division explanation is worse, whereas a post-hoc gate is global and every widening buys false positives everywhere."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 657, "key": "div/99d1860c: hengck23's planned division design is late-stage tracklet association, not per-frame fork proposal: get detect", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "my current strategy is to have good cell detection and tracking. Then infer division from long track ( stage 3 ). I assume death/birth can only happen at image boundary, so maybe we can detect splits from long tracks, ie i treat division as tracklet association problem, tracklet as node association", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "hengck23's planned division design is late-stage tracklet association, not per-frame fork proposal: get detection and tracking right first, assume birth/death only at the image boundary, then infer splits from long tracks with tracklets as nodes."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 658, "key": "div/873e5fad: The tracklet-association framing tested negative: the interior-orphan pool is ~400x larger than the events it ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Those four films hold 3 labelled divisions; the whole train set holds 151 across 87 of the 199 label files. So the pool is roughly 400x larger than the events it is meant to contain.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The tracklet-association framing tested negative: the interior-orphan pool is ~400x larger than the events it should contain (1,289 interior orphans on four films holding 3 labelled divisions)."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 659, "key": "div/5e904864: In the public 0.938 pipeline family the motion-relink stage rebuilds the edge list from a one-to-one Hungarian", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "In the public 0.938 pipeline family the motion-relink stage rebuilds the edge list from a one-to-one Hungarian assignment before safe-div runs, so every fork the ILP produced is destroyed there. If you inherit that post-processing, any division work upstream of it is invisible.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "In the public 0.938 pipeline family the motion-relink stage rebuilds the edge list from a one-to-one Hungarian assignment before safe-div runs, destroying every fork the ILP produced - so any upstream division work is invisible."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 660, "key": "div/c50a8c55: A Hungarian one-to-one linker forfeits the entire division term by construction - no node can have two outgoin", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A Hungarian linker forfeits the whole division term by construction. One successor per cell means no node ever has two outgoing edges, so division Jaccard is exactly 0.000 — in my test, 40 FN out of 40 — and 0.1 of the available 1.1 is gone before the tracker sees an image.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A Hungarian one-to-one linker forfeits the entire division term by construction - no node can have two outgoing edges, so division_jaccard is exactly 0.000 (40 FN of 40 in the test), leaving 0.1 of the available 1.1 unreachable."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 661, "key": "div/b375db6e: The host's own HOCT (general_v0) division-aware tracker, fed synthetic ball masks from point detections, over-", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "On my train movies it under-performed a tuned ILP on the edge term (it over-links), produced spurious forks rather than recovering real divisions, and ran ~45 min/movie on the open-source solver — so it's not submission-viable as-is with this input.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The host's own HOCT (general_v0) division-aware tracker, fed synthetic ball masks from point detections, over-linked, produced spurious forks rather than recovering real divisions, and ran ~45 min/movie - not submission-viable."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 662, "key": "div/d889f62c: hengck23's proposed route to a division detector: inspect ROI crops t-10..t+10 around Kaggle parent nodes in n", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "it is not difficult to use focus3d framework to hand-label these because it supports human-in-the-loop annotation (basically click a point like SAM). then you can train a division detector", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "hengck23's proposed route to a division detector: inspect ROI crops t-10..t+10 around Kaggle parent nodes in napari to confirm the appearance signature, then hand-label with focus3d's human-in-the-loop click annotation and train a detector on those labels."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 663, "key": "div/8dd774fa: hengck23 asserts many real divisions are not annotated in the Kaggle ground truth, and that they are not hard ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "i think many cell divisions are not annotated in the Kaggle data. It is not so difficult to identify them (and i think when you find one, you are likely to find many others. it is rare only one cell splits).", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "hengck23 asserts many real divisions are not annotated in the Kaggle ground truth, and that they are not hard to identify because divisions rarely occur singly."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 664, "key": "div/3f0b20b7: hengck23 warns that because the annotation is sparse, an apparently long parent-to-daughter step may be record", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "note that the annotation is sparse. it is possible that the \"long parent\" is annotated short.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "hengck23 warns that because the annotation is sparse, an apparently long parent-to-daughter step may be recorded as a short one, biasing any geometry measured on labels."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 665, "key": "div/c26c06af: The labels cover track SEGMENTS, not whole lineages: of 572 labelled tracks, 351 begin after frame 0 and 391 e", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Of 572 labelled tracks, 351 begin after frame 0 and 391 end before the last frame, median length 35 frames, while all 25,733 labelled edges span exactly one timepoint. So the 36 divisions are a labelled subset, not an unbiased sample of every division in the movies.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The labels cover track SEGMENTS, not whole lineages: of 572 labelled tracks, 351 begin after frame 0 and 391 end before the last frame, median length 35 frames - so the 36/151 labelled divisions are a biased subset, not an unbiased sample of all divisions."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 666, "key": "div/05e6d527: Only ~2.8% of nuclei are annotated in the training ground truth and there are ~304 division events across all ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The training ground truth is sparse — roughly 2.8% of nuclei are annotated, and across all 199 videos there are about 304 division events.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Only ~2.8% of nuclei are annotated in the training ground truth and there are ~304 division events across all 199 videos - a count roughly double the 151 fork figure others report, suggesting the two counts differ by the parent-vs-daughter unit."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 667, "key": "div/d283dd4c: The real GT division rate is ~0.26% of nodes counted as mitotic parents; the synthetic set inflates it to 4.07", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "the 4.07% figure is in that same unit — mitotic parents divided by all nodes, i.e. 165,267 / 4,056,226. So it's directly comparable to the ~0.26% real rate I quoted; both are \"fraction of nodes that are a dividing parent.\"", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The real GT division rate is ~0.26% of nodes counted as mitotic parents; the synthetic set inflates it to 4.07%, a 15.7x ratio for positive-class reweighting."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 668, "key": "div/18125fff: The 0.91-0.94 real-nuclei detectability reference comes from sparse annotations and annotators tend to label u", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "the 0.91–0.94 real reference comes from the sparse annotations (~2.8% of nuclei), and annotators tend to label unambiguous cells — so that reference is probably optimistic, and I haven't measured how real recall varies with density.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 0.91-0.94 real-nuclei detectability reference comes from sparse annotations and annotators tend to label unambiguous cells, so that reference is probably optimistic - direct support for annotation selection bias toward easy cases."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 669, "key": "div/441999db: The real training set comes from only 2 actual embryos, which differ enormously: annotation sparsity ~1% vs ~9", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "our real training set turns out to come from only 2 actual embryos, and they differ a lot annotation sparsity of roughly 1% vs 9%, and division counts of 26 vs 125 across their samples.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The real training set comes from only 2 actual embryos, which differ enormously: annotation sparsity ~1% vs ~9% and division counts 26 vs 125 across their samples."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 670, "key": "div/aab58df8: The ground truth follows only ~22 tracks per movie rather than every cell, so an unannotated blob is not a mis", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The ground truth follows ~22 tracks per movie, not every cell, but 100% of its edges span exactly one frame — no gap-closing to learn, and an unannotated blob is not a missed detection.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The ground truth follows only ~22 tracks per movie rather than every cell, so an unannotated blob is not a missed detection - the metric never sees unlabelled cells as edge false positives."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 671, "key": "div/ea99139c: The patched division metric requires genuine strongly-connected parent->two-daughter structure matched within ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The patch (now public in the repo) requires divisions to be genuine strongly-connected parent→two-daughter structures matched within the 7 µm distance radius. The far-away synthetic forks now register as false positives instead of true positives", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The patched division metric requires genuine strongly-connected parent->two-daughter structure matched within a 7 um distance radius; forks that do not match a real GT division register as false positives."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 672, "key": "div/4fea0f1b: Pre-patch the division match relied on weakly-connected-component structure and did not require a fork to sit ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "In the pre-patch version, that matching relied on weakly-connected-component structure and did not strictly require each matched division to be a genuine parent→two-daughter fork sitting near a real GT division.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Pre-patch the division match relied on weakly-connected-component structure and did not require a fork to sit near a real GT division, which is what the hub-node exploit abused to push division_jaccard from ~0 to ~1.0."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 673, "key": "div/3633610b: The host confirmed a division-Jaccard metric exploit, published a patch in royerlab/kaggle-cell-tracking-compe", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A few of you might be aware that an exploit of our metrics was found, specifically in the division Jaccard score. We have made a patch and will re-score all submissions as soon as possible (likely Monday). Submissions that did not actively exploit this will not have their scores affected.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The host confirmed a division-Jaccard metric exploit, published a patch in royerlab/kaggle-cell-tracking-competition, and re-scored all submissions; non-exploiting submissions were unaffected."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 674, "key": "div/52f17e37: The 17 July patch (commit aa65e90) closed three holes at once: divisions now need directed local topology inst", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The metric was patched on 17 July (commit aa65e90), closing three holes at once: divisions now require directed local topology rather than shared weak connectivity, edges spanning more than one frame are dropped, and merged edges are collapsed.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 17 July patch (commit aa65e90) closed three holes at once: divisions now need directed local topology instead of shared weak connectivity, edges spanning more than one frame are dropped, and merged edges are collapsed; pre-July local CV numbers are not comparable."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 675, "key": "div/194da82c: Measured on the same graphs, the hub-and-fake-fork exploit moved a 1:1 linker 0.9839 -> 1.0639 under the old m", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "appending a hub node and 40 fake forks moved a one-to-one linker 0.9839 → 1.0639 under the old metric and 0.9839 → 0.9794 under the new one", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Measured on the same graphs, the hub-and-fake-fork exploit moved a 1:1 linker 0.9839 -> 1.0639 under the old metric but 0.9839 -> 0.9794 under the patched one, i.e. it now loses score."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 676, "key": "div/e315c1c4: Over-detection is nearly free under the metric (a predicted node matching no GT node is never a false positive", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A predicted node that matches no ground-truth node is never a false positive; the whole cost is the adjusted-Jaccard term. The line is exactly 1 − 0.1 × over-prediction, so 10% more nodes has to buy only a 1% relative gain in edge Jaccard to break even.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Over-detection is nearly free under the metric (a predicted node matching no GT node is never a false positive; the line is exactly 1 - 0.1 x over-prediction), so detection recall is worth far more than precision."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 677, "key": "div/5e1b5180: Duplicate detections cost ~9% and buy nothing: node matching is one-to-one bipartite, so a twin one voxel away", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Duplicating a detection costs ~9% and buys nothing. Node matching is a one-to-one bipartite assignment, so a twin one voxel away matches nothing: edge Jaccard did not move at all, only the node count.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Duplicate detections cost ~9% and buy nothing: node matching is one-to-one bipartite, so a twin one voxel away matches nothing and only inflates the node count - taking a union of two models' detections without a merge pass pays this in full."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 678, "key": "div/de3ccc8f: Counter-argument to prioritising divisions: post-patch the top was 0.914-0.929 while division_jaccard was ~0 f", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Yet the post-patch top is 0.914–0.929, so the separation comes from somewhere. For those above ~0.914 — not asking for methods — is your gain on the edge term (a genuinely better/retrained model pushing adj_edge past ~0.91), or on divisions", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Counter-argument to prioritising divisions: post-patch the top was 0.914-0.929 while division_jaccard was ~0 for essentially everyone, so the observed frontier separation came from the edge term, not divisions."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 679, "key": "div/ebf2bd42: Hypothesis that the two score terms are not independent: a cleaner edge model would let the ILP form divisions", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "My hunch is that a cleaner edge model would let the ILP form divisions natively (divisions riding on long-range link recall) — i.e. the two terms aren't independent.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Hypothesis that the two score terms are not independent: a cleaner edge model would let the ILP form divisions natively, with divisions riding on long-range link recall rather than needing a dedicated division effort."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 680, "key": "div/f8dd54ff: Base rate against divisions: 151 divisions across all 199 training movies, one link in 853, and 112 of the 199", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Divisions: 151 in total, i.e. one link in 853, and 112 of the 199 movies contain none at all.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Base rate against divisions: 151 divisions across all 199 training movies, one link in 853, and 112 of the 199 movies contain none at all."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 681, "key": "div/14a3c331: The dominant bottleneck on division score is argued to be the rare-event base rate - a few hundred events amon", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "it suggests the rare-event nature of divisions (a few hundred events among hundreds of thousands of annotated nodes) is probably the dominant bottleneck for most people's division score, more than model architecture per se", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The dominant bottleneck on division score is argued to be the rare-event base rate - a few hundred events among hundreds of thousands of nodes - rather than model architecture."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 682, "key": "div/bf739b83: Weight-tuning offers only two bad options - suppress everything for div_j=0, or let a few real forks through b", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "It can only choose between \"suppress everything, div_j=0\" (weight 1.0) or \"let a few real ones through, buried under thousands of fake ones, div_j still ≈0\" (weight 0.5–0.65). Neither path gets me a meaningful positive division score.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Weight-tuning offers only two bad options - suppress everything for div_j=0, or let a few real forks through buried under thousands of false ones with div_j still ~0 - so no meaningful positive division score is reachable that way."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 683, "key": "div/3dea1016: Missed divisions are not random: they concentrate in crowded frames with ~3x the node count and closer neighbo", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "theyre almost all in crowded frames. like 3x the node count of the ones i get right and closer neighbours. and when i checked why each one failed basically none of them fail for js one reason. they fail 2 or 3 checks at once. so loosening any single threshold recovers nothing", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Missed divisions are not random: they concentrate in crowded frames with ~3x the node count and closer neighbours, and each failure trips 2-3 checks at once, so loosening any single threshold recovers nothing and only adds false positives."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 684, "key": "div/a0154a94: The public Ultrack zebrafish_embryo.ome.zarr reports voxel scale 1.625 x 0.40625 x 0.40625 um and shape (522, ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "'scale': [1.0, 1.0, 1.625, 0.40625, 0.40625], 'type': 'scale'}], 'path': '0'}, level=0 shape=(522, 1, 505, 2217, 2170)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The public Ultrack zebrafish_embryo.ome.zarr reports voxel scale 1.625 x 0.40625 x 0.40625 um and shape (522, 1, 505, 2217, 2170), matching the Kaggle voxel size exactly - so the Ultrack paper's sparse-zebrafish Fig.6 dataset is the same acquisition family as the competition data."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 685, "key": "div/729170a8: A free CC0 synthetic dataset provides 165,267 labelled mitotic-parent divisions across 4,056,226 nodes (~540x ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "165,267 labelled divisions across 4,056,226 nodes That is roughly 540× more mitosis supervision than the competition ground truth provides.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A free CC0 synthetic dataset provides 165,267 labelled mitotic-parent divisions across 4,056,226 nodes (~540x the competition's mitosis supervision), generated by a physical model with the evaluator's exact stride-4 XY downsampling."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 686, "key": "div/af569a87: In the synthetic dataset both daughters inherit the parent's track_id, so nodes[:,4] is a lineage/clone id rat", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "both daughters inherit the parent's track_id. So nodes[:, 4] is a lineage/clone id, not a unique track — after a division, two distinct tracks share it. The unambiguous lineage lives in edges (and a node with two outgoing edges is exactly a mitosis).", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "In the synthetic dataset both daughters inherit the parent's track_id, so nodes[:,4] is a lineage/clone id rather than a unique track - building a candidate graph off track_id makes cells appear to teleport."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 687, "key": "div/830caf83: CRITICAL: support/division_metrics.py on disk is the PRE-PATCH (exploitable) division scorer using weak connec", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "support/: 'All matched prediction nodes in a single weakly-connected component of the prediction graph.'  reference/: 'Merely sharing a weakly connected component is not sufficient.'", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CRITICAL: support/division_metrics.py on disk is the PRE-PATCH (exploitable) division scorer using weak connectivity; the current patched scorer is reference/royerlab-baseline/src/tracking_cellmot/division_metrics.py, which uses _is_strongly_connected_division. Tuning against support/ calibrates to the closed exploit."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 688, "key": "div/d2c49cef: A predicted fork that matches no GT node, is not a local candidate at any GT division window, and has matched ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Otherwise, unmatched, structurally valid forks with no local GT evidence are ignored.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A predicted fork that matches no GT node, is not a local candidate at any GT division window, and has matched evidence on at most one child branch is scored as NEITHER TP NOR FP - it is completely free under the patched metric."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 689, "key": "div/4d94ee68: The free-fork rule is confirmed by the baseline's own unit test: a fork whose parent is unmatched and where on", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "\"\"\"One branch with no matched child or grandchild provides no evidence.\"\"\" ... assert evaluate_divisions(pred, gt, max_distance=1.0) == DivisionCounts(tp=0, fn=0, fp=0,)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The free-fork rule is confirmed by the baseline's own unit test: a fork whose parent is unmatched and where only one of two branches has matched GT evidence yields DivisionCounts(tp=0, fn=0, fp=0)."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 690, "key": "div/5489c7d6: Exactly four things make a predicted fork a division FALSE POSITIVE: (a) the fork node itself matches an annot", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "fp_forks = (considered | evaluable_forks | invalid_forks) - tp_forks", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Exactly four things make a predicted fork a division FALSE POSITIVE: (a) the fork node itself matches an annotated GT node with out-degree>=1; (b) it was a local candidate at a GT division window but failed topology or lost the bipartite pairing; (c) two distinct child branches have nearest matched evidence in different GT weakly-connected components; (d) its local branches merge."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 691, "key": "div/5e2e449f: Only forks landing on ANNOTATED GT nodes are 'evaluable' as FPs: the fork must match a GT node within 7 um who", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "evaluable_forks = {pred_id for pred_id in pred_forks if pred_id in pred_to_gt and gt_graph.out_degree(pred_to_gt[pred_id]) >= 1}", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Only forks landing on ANNOTATED GT nodes are 'evaluable' as FPs: the fork must match a GT node within 7 um whose GT node still has an outgoing edge. With ~2.8% of nuclei annotated, the great majority of speculative forks never enter the FP tally at all."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 692, "key": "div/2d97ce17: A fork on a matched GT node whose annotation ENDS there (out-degree 0) is deliberately excluded from the FP ta", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A matched GT node with no children marks the end of the annotation - we can't tell whether the cell actually divided there, so such predicted divisions are excluded from the count (and therefore from the FP tally).", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A fork on a matched GT node whose annotation ENDS there (out-degree 0) is deliberately excluded from the FP tally, because the scorer cannot tell whether the cell really divided - so forking at the end of an annotated segment is also free."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 693, "key": "div/7e9aace6: The most dangerous fork is the one that bridges two different annotated GT tracks: if two direct-child branche", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "for pred_id in pred_forks: ... else: if len(set(branch_evidence)) >= 2: cross_component_forks.add(pred_id)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The most dangerous fork is the one that bridges two different annotated GT tracks: if two direct-child branches have nearest matched evidence in distinct GT weakly-connected components the fork is an unconditional FP, matched or not."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 694, "key": "div/a2842d85: Near-misses at real divisions are penalised where distant speculation is not: any fork sitting on a matched pa", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "local_nodes = parent_ids | {successor for parent_id in parent_ids for successor in matched_pred.successors(parent_id)} local_forks = local_nodes & pred_div_nodes considered |= local_forks", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Near-misses at real divisions are penalised where distant speculation is not: any fork sitting on a matched parent-side node of a GT division window (or its immediate successor) enters 'considered' and becomes an FP if it fails the topology test or loses the pairing."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 695, "key": "div/0f4dcf59: The division matching window is exactly four timepoints: grandparent -> dividing parent -> children -> grandch", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "grandparent -> dividing parent -> children -> grandchildren\n```\nThis window permits a predicted fork one timepoint before or after the GT split without using graph-wide reachability.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The division matching window is exactly four timepoints: grandparent -> dividing parent -> children -> grandchildren, extracted per GT division; predicted nodes are matched independently against that window at 7 um, so the fork may be one frame early or late."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 696, "key": "div/4957428c: The TP topology test is local and directed: the fork must be, or be the immediate successor of, a node matched", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "pred_parent_ids = {pred_div, *pred_graph.predecessors(pred_div)} if pred_parent_ids.isdisjoint(parent_ids): return False", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The TP topology test is local and directed: the fork must be, or be the immediate successor of, a node matched to the GT divider or its predecessor, and the fork's two child-lineages ({child} u successors(child)) must cover two distinct GT daughter lineages under a bipartite matching."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 697, "key": "div/f49d63f2: Daughter evidence may arrive at different timepoints and a grandchild can stand in for an unmatched direct chi", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A bipartite matching must associate the two GT daughter lineages with two different predicted branches. The two supporting matches may occur at different timepoints within the window.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Daughter evidence may arrive at different timepoints and a grandchild can stand in for an unmatched direct child, so a division can still score TP when only one daughter is detected at t+1 and the other only at t+2."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 698, "key": "div/fa9adb9d: A fork is rejected as malformed if a child used by the fork has any other parent, or if a fallback grandchild ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A direct child used by the fork must have that fork as its sole parent. When grandchild fallback is needed, each grandchild must belong to that child alone. A locally merged or shared branch is rejected.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A fork is rejected as malformed if a child used by the fork has any other parent, or if a fallback grandchild is shared - so merge-then-split structures cannot score and are charged as FPs."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 699, "key": "div/5fb46b66: division_jaccard is micro-averaged: TP/FP/FN are summed across every video in the split before the Jaccard is ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "has_divisions = (div_tp + div_fp + div_fn) > 0 division_jaccard = _jaccard(div_tp, div_fp, div_fn) if has_divisions else float(\"nan\")", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "division_jaccard is micro-averaged: TP/FP/FN are summed across every video in the split before the Jaccard is taken, so a handful of FPs on one movie dilutes TPs won on another; and if no divisions exist anywhere the term is dropped entirely."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 700, "key": "div/90619920: One predicted fork can be credited to at most one GT division and one GT division to at most one fork (maximum", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A maximum-cardinality bipartite matching is then computed so each pred fork serves at most one GT division, and each GT division is paired with at most one pred fork.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "One predicted fork can be credited to at most one GT division and one GT division to at most one fork (maximum-cardinality bipartite pairing), which is what closed the hub-node exploit - so duplicating forks around a real division buys nothing and costs FPs."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 701, "key": "div/f0bf1c9e: Predicted nodes that match no GT node are never edge false positives either - the sparse ground truth makes ov", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Because the ground truth is sparse, a correct prediction will inevitably include nodes and edges the ground truth doesn't cover. Predicted nodes that do not match a ground-truth node are not counted as false positives.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Predicted nodes that match no GT node are never edge false positives either - the sparse ground truth makes over-detection structurally cheap, penalised only through the adjusted-Jaccard node-count term with a=0.1."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 702, "key": "div/21ec2904: A dense, unbiased zebrafish lineage from the same lab (ZSNS003, 4,057,611 nodes) yields 15,868 real divisions ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "continuation n= 3888503 median= 1.317 IQR=0.878-1.861 p90= 2.842 p99= 6.361 | division step n= 31736 median= 3.070 IQR=2.322-3.922 p99= 6.459 | sister separation n= 15868 median= 5.854 IQR=4.747-7.145 p99= 10.862", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A dense, unbiased zebrafish lineage from the same lab (ZSNS003, 4,057,611 nodes) yields 15,868 real divisions - 105x the Kaggle labelled sample - with division step median 3.070 um (IQR 2.322-3.922) against continuation median 1.317 um, and sister separation median 5.854 um (IQR 4.747-7.145)."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 703, "key": "div/aa2c2a85: DECISIVE against long-step gating: on 15,868 dense divisions the continuation:division ratio above 7 um is 203", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "thr continuations division_steps ratio | 5 87926 2597 33.9:1 | 7 29088 143 203.4:1 | 9 15755 18 875.3:1 | 10 12081 13 929.3:1 | 12 6561 5 1312.2:1", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "DECISIVE against long-step gating: on 15,868 dense divisions the continuation:division ratio above 7 um is 203:1, above 9 um 875:1 and above 10 um 929:1 - far worse than the 30:1 / 27:1 / 38:1 measured on the 36 Kaggle labels. A long parent step is evidence AGAINST a division, not for one."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 704, "key": "div/8590e08b: CONTRADICTS the asymmetric-gate prescription: on true positions 99.1% of divisions have BOTH parent->daughter ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "both arcs inside 7.0 um: 15728/15868 (99.1%) | both arcs inside 9.0 um: 15850/15868 (99.9%) | both arcs inside 9.9 um: 15855/15868 (99.9%) | both arcs inside 12.0 um: 15863/15868 (100.0%)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CONTRADICTS the asymmetric-gate prescription: on true positions 99.1% of divisions have BOTH parent->daughter arcs inside 7.0 um and 99.9% inside 9.0 um. The earlier 2/7-at-7um / 7/7-at-15um reachability curve measured detector localisation error, not cell motion - the fix is localisation, not a wider fork gate."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 705, "key": "div/cc40687a: NEGATIVE RESULT on parent motion history: the parent's step length is flat at 1.315 um for 8 frames before the", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "parent step length (um) k frames before the split: k-1 n= 15131 median= 1.242 | k-2 n= 15131 median= 1.315 | k-3 median= 1.315 | k-4 median= 1.315 | k-8 n= 12244 median= 1.315", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "NEGATIVE RESULT on parent motion history: the parent's step length is flat at 1.315 um for 8 frames before the split and only dips to 1.242 um on the final step into the fork, against a population continuation median of 1.317 um. A motion-history feature on the parent is essentially chance."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 706, "key": "div/59a1747b: Sisters are already ~4.4x a continuation step apart at birth and then separate only slowly (5.854 um at the sp", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "t+ 0 n= 15868 median= 5.854 | t+ 1 median= 6.203 | t+ 2 median= 6.459 | t+ 3 median= 6.753 | t+ 5 median= 7.249 | t+10 n= 4437 median= 8.057", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Sisters are already ~4.4x a continuation step apart at birth and then separate only slowly (5.854 um at the split rising to 8.057 um ten frames later, ~0.22 um/frame), so waiting extra frames to confirm a sister pair by separation buys little - +15% by t+3."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 707, "key": "div/cad37dc3: Every one of the 15,868 forks in the dense lineage has exactly two daughters - zero cases of any other multipl", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "forks with exactly 2 daughters 15868 other multiplicities 0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Every one of the 15,868 forks in the dense lineage has exactly two daughters - zero cases of any other multiplicity - confirming the strict binary fork model the metric assumes."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 708, "key": "div/09228f15: EVIDENCE FOR UNANNOTATED DIVISIONS, with a magnitude: the dense lineage carries one division edge in 124 links", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "continuation steps 3888503 track starts with parent 31736 ... forks with exactly 2 daughters 15868", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EVIDENCE FOR UNANNOTATED DIVISIONS, with a magnitude: the dense lineage carries one division edge in 124 links (31,736 of 3,920,239), against the Kaggle sparse labels' one in 853 - roughly a 6.9x deficit. Since ZSNS003's finer cadence should bias it toward FEWER divisions per link, the true deficit is if anything larger."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 709, "key": "div/4f86c1a4: The dense-vs-sparse cadence differs: ZSNS003's continuation median is 1.317 um against the Kaggle labels' 1.72", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "continuation n= 3888503 median= 1.317 ... division step n= 31736 median= 3.070 ... sister separation n= 15868 median= 5.854", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The dense-vs-sparse cadence differs: ZSNS003's continuation median is 1.317 um against the Kaggle labels' 1.724 um, so ZSNS003 geometry should be scaled by ~1.31 to compare. That puts the Kaggle-equivalent division step at ~4.0 um (labels say 4.57) and sister separation at ~7.7 um (labels say 8.85) - the sparse labels run slightly LONG, consistent with easy-case selection."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 710, "key": "div/d519c303: Scaled to Kaggle cadence the dense sister-separation p99 is ~14.2 um and the 99.9% both-arcs radius ~11.8 um, ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "sister separation n= 15868 median= 5.854 IQR=4.747-7.145 p90= 8.337 p99= 10.862 max= 20.457", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Scaled to Kaggle cadence the dense sister-separation p99 is ~14.2 um and the 99.9% both-arcs radius ~11.8 um, so the public 0.947 stack's parent<=9.0 um / sister<=14.0 um gates already sit at about the right place on true geometry - widening them is not where the division score is."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 711, "key": "div/36f2667e: Correction to the forum's reading of the Ultrack configs: the event weights are NEGATIVE, and division_weight ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "sparse_zebrafish [tracking] appear_weight = -0.1 bias = 0 disappear_weight = -0.1 division_weight = -0.1 ; large_zebrafish [tracking] appear_weight = -0.002 disappear_weight = -0.001 division_weight = -0.001", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Correction to the forum's reading of the Ultrack configs: the event weights are NEGATIVE, and division_weight equals disappear_weight EXACTLY in both zebrafish configs (-0.1/-0.1 sparse, -0.001/-0.001 large) - the reference tracker prices a division identically to a disappearance."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 712, "key": "div/deaae96b: The Ultrack sparse-zebrafish config scores links with distance_weight = 0.0 - distance contributes NOTHING to ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "[linking] distance_weight = 0.0 max_distance = 10.0 max_neighbors = 5 n_workers = 1 z_score_threshold = 5.0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The Ultrack sparse-zebrafish config scores links with distance_weight = 0.0 - distance contributes NOTHING to the link cost, which is overlap/IoU-driven - alongside max_distance 10.0, max_neighbors 5, z_score_threshold 5.0 and a 25-frame solver window."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 713, "key": "div/4554a9b1: CONFIRMED: none of the six zebrahub single-objective acquisitions match the Kaggle voxel size - all report 1.2", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "ZSNS001 :: [{'scale': [1.0, 1.0, 1.24, 0.439, 0.439], 'type': 'scale'}] ... ZSNS005 :: [{'scale': [1.0, 1.0, 1.24, 0.439, 0.439], 'type': 'scale'}]", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CONFIRMED: none of the six zebrahub single-objective acquisitions match the Kaggle voxel size - all report 1.24 x 0.439 x 0.439 um. Only public.czbiohub.org/royerlab/ultrack/zebrafish_embryo.ome.zarr matches Kaggle's 1.625 x 0.40625 x 0.40625."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 714, "key": "div/c56a0991: The voxel-size mismatch does NOT block using zebrahub for division geometry: the track CSVs carry explicit id/", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "track_id,t,z,y,x,id,parent_track_id,parent_id 1,1,92.0,439.0,437.0,2015514,-1,-1 1,2,92.0,440.0,435.0,3013903,-1,2015514", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The voxel-size mismatch does NOT block using zebrahub for division geometry: the track CSVs carry explicit id/parent_id lineage and coordinates convert to micrometres, so division statistics in um transfer even though the image grid differs. Only appearance/PSF transfer is affected."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 715, "key": "div/1caa6946: FOCUS-3D as published is a 3D instance SEGMENTATION model (Mask2Former on an MAE-3D backbone), and its online ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Upload one 3D TIFF volume (<100 MB), specify imaging parameters, run FOCUS-3D, inspect segmentation across Z slices, and download the instance-label TIFF.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 72, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FOCUS-3D as published is a 3D instance SEGMENTATION model (Mask2Former on an MAE-3D backbone), and its online Space is a one-shot upload-a-TIFF-get-labels demo with no human-in-the-loop point annotation and no lineage - so it is not the division hand-labelling route hengck23 suggested, though it could supply the instance masks Ultrack/HOCT need."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 716, "key": "frozen_frames_are_THE_gt_defect", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "947 byte-identical frame pairs across 114 of 128 6bba videos (7.47% of pairs) and ZERO in all 71 44b6 videos", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE GT DEFECT TO DEFEND AGAINST. 947 byte-identical consecutive frame pairs across 114 of 128 6bba videos (7.47% of pairs) and ZERO across all 71 44b6 videos, with identical freeze schedules shared across distinct crops. In 6bba_fc516dc6 t=81 and t=82 are byte-identical yet the GT edge moves 8.90um - BEYOND the 7um matching radius - almost all of it in Z (23->18 voxels = 8.125um), i.e. annotation wobble on the 4x-anisotropic axis, with both GT points on the same blob. The naive defence (copy the detection across a frozen frame) is GUARANTEED to miss. And because it is 89% concentrated in one embryo, any freeze heuristic tuned on train is tuned on 6bba alone - and test is embryo-disjoint."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 717, "key": "annotation_bias_measured", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "within 8 voxels of the XY border are 6.4% vs a uniform expectation of 12.1%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Annotation is NOT a random subset, measured by decoding 30 .geff files (7,584 nodes, all 44b6): nodes within 8 voxels of the XY border are 6.4% against a uniform expectation of 12.1%; within 4 voxels of the Z border, 6.0% vs 12.5% - roughly HALF density at every border in all three axes. The central two octiles hold 46-49% against 25% expected. Time is flat except a dip in the last ~12 frames. Caveat: 30/199 files, all 44b6, and a centred tissue shell is a partial confound."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 718, "key": "coordinates_verified_clean", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "t 0-99, z 0-63, y 0-255, x 0-255 against a (100,64,256,256) volume", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Settled by direct decoding: geff coordinates are int64 with ranges EXACTLY t 0-99, z 0-63, y 0-255, x 0-255 against a (100,64,256,256) volume. No off-by-one, no axis swap, no origin offset."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 719, "key": "zebrahub_explicitly_allowed_by_host", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Yes you are free to use the data and all resources in Zebrahub for this competition! There is no overlap with the test set.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "rules", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "HOST RULING, 2026-08-13, Thibgolds: Zebrahub is allowed and has no test-set overlap. Publicly downloadable at public.czbiohub.org/royerlab/zebrahub/imaging/single-objective/ (~2.2GB of Ultrack track CSVs + 6 OME-Zarr volumes). BUT its tracks are Ultrack PSEUDO-LABELS, not curated, and NO Zebrahub volume matches the Kaggle voxel scale: single-objective is (1.24,0.439,0.439) at 2.82:1 anisotropy, multi-view (1.97,0.485,0.485) at 4.06:1 but ~20% coarser everywhere. It is also Zarr v2 5D TCZYX against the competition's v3 4D TZYX."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 720, "key": "hand_labelling_train_is_permitted", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "the rules bar hand labelling only of the validation dataset or test data records", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "rules", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The rules bar hand labelling only of 'the validation dataset or test data records' - hand-labelling TRAINING data is permitted. Given only 151 annotated divisions exist and ~6.9x more are estimated to be unannotated, hand-labelling divisions on train is both legal and possibly the highest-value data action available."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 721, "key": "cell_tracking_challenge_is_a_trap", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Cloning of our datasets or their parts, including reference annotations, is strictly forbidden", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "rules", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CTC is a RULES TRAP: the permission cited is a PRIVATE EMAIL to one participant, and CTC term 5 states 'Cloning of our datasets or their parts, including reference annotations, is strictly forbidden' - which blocks re-hosting it as a Kaggle dataset, and an internet-disabled rerun requires exactly that. SSBD Keller zebrafish is also useless: CC BY-NC-SA (non-commercial, fails the rules) and ships 0 bytes of images - 48.2GB of nuclear positions only, isotropic 1um, 90s interval."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 722, "key": "synthetic_division_set_is_real_but_weak", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "DS_SEQ_LEN=6 ... the 1,539 native-resolution 256x256 static volumes carry centroids only, no graph", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 18.5GB synthetic set is REAL and CC0 (clears the rules bar) but is NOT a Kaggle Dataset - the slug 404s; it ships as the NOTEBOOK OUTPUT of josefreitasalvesneto/biohub-synthetic-dataset, attached via Add Data -> Notebook Output. Four defects: (1) ALL lineage supervision is in 6-frame 64^3 crops (DS_SEQ_LEN=6), and the 1,539 native-resolution 256x256 volumes carry CENTROIDS ONLY, no graph - so no long-range linking can be learned from it; (2) the '540x' headline mixes units by 2x - 165,267 is mitotic PARENTS against the corpus's 151 fork-parents, not 304, so anyone using the 15.7x reweighting factor is off by 2x; (3) texture and contrast are the author's own admitted weakest axes, which is exactly what a detector trains on; (4) zero reported transfer results in six weeks."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 723, "key": "royerlab_pretrained_weights_are_free", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "unet-simview.pt (84 MiB) and unet-daxi.pt (20 MiB), BSD-3-Clause, both HTTP 200", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "UNDER-USED AND FREE: royerlab publish torchscript UNet weights - unet-simview.pt (84 MiB) and unet-daxi.pt (20 MiB), BSD-3-Clause, both HTTP 200. Their published zebrafish configs gate linking at max_distance = 10.0um, WIDER than the competition's 7um matching radius."}
