{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 772, "key": "data/d81e5de4: GEFF node coordinates index the Zarr array directly as image[t,z,y,x] with NO axis swap, flip or origin offset", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The GEFF coordinates map directly to the corresponding Zarr array as: image = zarr.open_group(zarr_path, mode=\"r\")[\"0\"] voxel = image[t, z, y, x] There is no additional axis swap, flip, or origin transformation. The metadata uses (T, Z, Y, X)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "GEFF node coordinates index the Zarr array directly as image[t,z,y,x] with NO axis swap, flip or origin offset; the um scaling applies only to physical distances, never to array indexing."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 773, "key": "data/9754ea76: VERIFIED BY MEASUREMENT: there is no off-by-one between geff and volume. Across 30 train .geff files the int64", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "30 complete geffs; dtypes={'t': 'int64', 'z': 'int64', 'y': 'int64', 'x': 'int64'} | t: min=0 max=99 | z: min=0 max=63 | y: min=0 max=255 | x: min=0 max=255", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "VERIFIED BY MEASUREMENT: there is no off-by-one between geff and volume. Across 30 train .geff files the int64 coordinate ranges are exactly t 0-99, z 0-63, y 0-255, x 0-255, matching the (100,64,256,256) volume shape."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 774, "key": "data/26c93b4b: The .geff root zarr.json is geff_version 1.1 and carries per-file axis metadata including the um scale (z 1.62", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "\"geff_version\": \"1.1\", \"directed\": true, \"axes\": [{\"name\":\"t\",...,\"min\":0.0,\"max\":75.0,\"scale\":1.0}, {\"name\":\"z\",...,\"min\":1.0,\"max\":63.0,\"scale\":1.625}, {\"name\":\"y\",...,\"scale\":0.40625}, {\"name\":\"x\",...,\"scale\":0.40625}]", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The .geff root zarr.json is geff_version 1.1 and carries per-file axis metadata including the um scale (z 1.625, y/x 0.40625) and the per-file coordinate min/max, so the scale need not be hardcoded."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 775, "key": "data/8e7f98de: CHUNKING TRAP: chunks are one full timepoint (1,64,256,256), so a time-major read touches one chunk while read", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Zarr decompresses a whole chunk regardless of how little you asked for, so reading one full timepoint touches 1 chunk while reading a single Z-slice across all time touches 100 chunks - 100x the I/O for 64x less data. Time-major access is nearly free", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CHUNKING TRAP: chunks are one full timepoint (1,64,256,256), so a time-major read touches one chunk while reading one Z-slice across all time touches 100 chunks - 100x the I/O for 64x less data."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 776, "key": "data/d09cb6d8: INTENSITY TRAP: the shipped quantiles are 0.0->15, 0.1->75, 0.9->497, 0.99->1478, 1.0->4319, so naive min-max ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "0.0 -> 15, 0.1 -> 75, 0.9 -> 497, 0.99 -> 1478, 1.0 -> 4319 The range is 15 to 4319, but 90% of voxels sit at or below 497, which is 11.2% of that range. Min-max scaling to [0,1] therefore puts 90% of your data below 0.112.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "INTENSITY TRAP: the shipped quantiles are 0.0->15, 0.1->75, 0.9->497, 0.99->1478, 1.0->4319, so naive min-max scaling puts 90% of voxels below 0.112 of the range; clip at the 99th percentile first."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 777, "key": "data/849208df: ANISOTROPY TRAP quantified: Z/Y is exactly 4.0, so two detections 4 voxels apart in Z are 6.5 um apart while 4", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "So Z / Y = exactly 4.0 - one voxel step in Z covers four times the physical distance of one step in Y or X. ... Two detections 4 voxels apart in Z are 6.5 micrometers apart; 4 voxels apart in Y are 1.63.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "ANISOTROPY TRAP quantified: Z/Y is exactly 4.0, so two detections 4 voxels apart in Z are 6.5 um apart while 4 voxels apart in Y are 1.63 um - any gating radius computed on raw voxel indices understates Z by 4x."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 778, "key": "data/49fcfd32: Zarr cannot be pip-installed during a scored rerun (internet disabled); the working route is uploading the .wh", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "But uploading a dataset containing the .whl files required for its install, run them, install Zarr and then import.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Zarr cannot be pip-installed during a scored rerun (internet disabled); the working route is uploading the .whl files as a Kaggle dataset and installing from it offline."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 779, "key": "data/fb0125bf: Division geometry measured on real labelled events: the parent->daughter step median is 8.47 um and sister sep", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "longest parent to daughter step median 8.47 um range 5.69 to 13.65 sister separation at prediction median 10.22 um range 7.87 to 13.12 ... both steps inside a 7.0 um gate 2/7 (29%) both steps inside a 9.9 um gate 5/7 (71%) both steps inside a 12.0 um gate 6/7 (86%) both steps inside a 15.0 um gat...", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Division geometry measured on real labelled events: the parent->daughter step median is 8.47 um and sister separation at prediction 10.22 um, so a 7 um gate tuned for ~3 um continuations admits only 2 of 7 divisions; 15 um admits all 7."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 780, "key": "data/539afff3: Divisions are not lost to detection: across 7 labelled divisions the parent and both daughters were detected 7", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "parent and both daughters detected 7/7 (100%) both parent->daughter edges in my graph 0/7 exactly one edge present 7/7 predicted parent out-degree 1, every single time", "review_after": null, "source_id": 73, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Divisions are not lost to detection: across 7 labelled divisions the parent and both daughters were detected 7/7 within the 7 um radius, yet exactly one of the two parent->daughter edges was produced every time."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 781, "key": "focus3d_pseudolabels_beat_sparse_annotation", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "edge_recall_end_to_end 0.84-0.99, mean ~0.93 ... trains 200 epochs without overfitting", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "BEST METHOD WE ARE NOT DOING. hengck23 trained a full pipeline with ZERO Kaggle sparse annotation - nodes from FOCUS-3D dense centroids, edges from synthetic pairs made by augmenting frame t into t+1 - and validated on 11 held-out Kaggle-annotated samples over full 100-frame sequences: edge_recall_end_to_end 0.84-0.99, mean ~0.93; on 6bba_337b1b3a specifically 0.9644 edge recall at mean_edge_prob 0.9515. Critically he trains 200 EPOCHS WITHOUT OVERFITTING, against the loss-blowup-after-10-epochs that three separate people report on sparse annotation. FOCUS-3D is a 3D Mask2Former on a 3D-MAE ViT-L backbone (EMBED_DIM 1008, DEPTH 24, 300 queries) - far too slow in-notebook, so its only viable role is OFFLINE label generation."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 782, "key": "PU_detection_loss_is_on_disk_unused", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "w_pos=12.0 on labelled centres, w_bg=1.0 only below the 40th intensity percentile, w_ignore=0.05 on bright unlabelled tissue", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A positive-unlabelled detection loss is ALREADY ON DISK and unused: evidence/datasets/support-pack/source_scripts/train_full_frame_center_detector.py uses a Gaussian heatmap target (sigma=1.0), w_pos=12.0 on labelled centres, w_bg=1.0 ONLY below the 40th intensity percentile, and w_ignore=0.05 on bright unlabelled tissue. The deployed BCEWithLogits on single hard voxels with det_neg_weight=1e-2 teaches every unannotated REAL cell to be a hard negative - the most likely cause of the widely-reported training instability."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 783, "key": "division_upweight_is_dead_code", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "weight = torch.ones_like(loss); weight[div_rows] = 1.0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FREE LEVER: in the organisers' compute_loss the division-upweighting hook is dead code - `weight = torch.ones_like(loss); weight[div_rows] = 1.0`. Divisions are 10% of the metric and have NEVER been upweighted in any public lineage."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 784, "key": "train_inference_threshold_mismatch", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Training selects peaks with det_logits > 0.3 on the raw logit (p~0.574); inference uses sigmoid(logits) > det_threshold with CLI default 0.99", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "TRAIN/INFERENCE MISMATCH. Training selects peaks with det_logits > 0.3 on the RAW LOGIT (p~0.574); inference uses sigmoid(logits) > det_threshold with a CLI default of 0.99. The linker was therefore never trained on the sparse high-precision candidate set it is scored on. Training also matches detections to GT at 5.0um, stricter than the metric's 7um."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 785, "key": "gap_recovery_means_a_NODE_not_an_edge", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "all 7,998 edges span exactly 1 frame", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "REFRAMES GAP RECOVERY. Measured on the 31 local train geffs: the GT contains NO skip-frame edges at all - all 7,998 edges span exactly 1 frame. So gap recovery can never mean emitting a t->t+2 edge; the scorer will never match one. It must mean RECOVERING THE MISSING INTERMEDIATE NODE and then two 1-frame edges. The support pack's unshipped full_frame_center rescue prior was built for exactly this, with a ready calibration sweep at 7um over thresholds 0.10-0.80."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 786, "key": "downsample_destroys_nothing", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "across 7,584 GT nodes in 30 datasets, zero collapsed", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The (1,4,4) downsample destroys nothing: the training code warns GT nodes 'collapse to duplicate voxels ... undetectable', but across 7,584 GT nodes in 30 datasets ZERO collapsed. Chasing finer resolution is not justified. GT coordinates are int64 voxel indices, so there is no subpixel signal to recover either. Caveat: measured on ANNOTATED nodes only."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 787, "key": "measured_negatives_to_avoid", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "cutting nodes 17% cost ~0.18 edge Jaccard", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED NEGATIVES. Ultrack as a detector/segmenter: two independent participants found it ineffective versus their own detector, oversegmentation unusable, centroids matching GT only on isolated cells. CoTracker: fails through division (morphology change loses pixel correspondence), 2D-MIP only, positional encoding breaks in 3D, not birth/death/division aware. More post-processing: four independent plateau reports (0.942, 0.916, 'local goes up LB goes down', 'hit a wall'). Raising the detection threshold to fix node count: cutting nodes 17% cost ~0.18 edge Jaccard. Against GNNs: the GT is 4,435 disjoint trees of which at most 151 branch, so >=96.6% are pure linear chains."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 788, "key": "two_hours_per_epoch_is_a_T4_artefact", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "T4 has very bad fp32, basically 25x slower", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "hardware", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The widely-quoted 2h/epoch is a Kaggle T4 FP32 artefact - 'T4 has very bad fp32, basically 25x slower'. The same author got it to 50 minutes; another reports 4h for 50 epochs (~5 min/epoch) on their own GPU. The public checkpoint is named 400ep, window_size=2, otherwise the organisers' untuned defaults. This materially changes the compute budget: the job is far smaller than the forum number implies."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 789, "key": "meth/5db1cb6d: A UNet3D point detector distilled from FOCUS-3D dense centroids plus Kaggle annotation runs 6 s per volume on ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "experiment on training a unet3d with dense cell centroids from focus3d + kaggle annotation:\n\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/code/hengck23/cell-point-detector\n\n(6 sec per volume on one T4 gpu)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A UNet3D point detector distilled from FOCUS-3D dense centroids plus Kaggle annotation runs 6 s per volume on a single T4 and is published as a Kaggle notebook."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 790, "key": "meth/1292a21b: That FOCUS-3D-distilled detector reaches node recall 0.992-1.000 on Kaggle annotations at prob threshold 0.20 ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "prob threshold=0.20 | sample_id truth_est_num_nodes predict_num_nodes num_nodes_ratio gt hit miss recall | 44b6_0113de3b 25755 28431 1.103902155 52 52 0 1.000 | 44b6_0b24845f 32795 77446 2.361518524 51 51 0 1.000", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "That FOCUS-3D-distilled detector reaches node recall 0.992-1.000 on Kaggle annotations at prob threshold 0.20 across 11 train samples, but over-predicts nodes by 1.09x to 2.36x the estimated true count."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 791, "key": "meth/0f8afaa3: Raising that detector's threshold from 0.20 to 0.50 cuts the node-count ratio only modestly (e.g. 2.36x to 2.0", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "prob threshold=0.50 | 44b6_0b24845f 32795 66171 2.017716115 51 51 0 1.000 | 6bba_05b6850b 6362 8744 1.374410563 861 813 48 0.944", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Raising that detector's threshold from 0.20 to 0.50 cuts the node-count ratio only modestly (e.g. 2.36x to 2.02x) while starting to cost recall (0.944 on one sample), so threshold alone does not fix over-detection."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 792, "key": "meth/5469bb63: The stated detector objective in this lane is not maximum recall but minimum predicted nodes at near-100% reca", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Getting the point is usually not the issue.  But we want to minimise no of predicted nodes with near 100% recall rate", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The stated detector objective in this lane is not maximum recall but minimum predicted nodes at near-100% recall, because the adjusted-edge-Jaccard node-count term penalises over-prediction."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 793, "key": "meth/59ea691d: A full detector+linker pipeline trained entirely on FOCUS-3D dense pseudo-labels with ZERO Kaggle sparse annot", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Training: - node: focus3D dense annotation - edge: dense pair, synthetic created by t1 = augmentation of t0 - use 0,10,20, ..90 of kaggle data (10% of all data). train 200 epoches. No kaggle sparse annotation is used at all !!!", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A full detector+linker pipeline trained entirely on FOCUS-3D dense pseudo-labels with ZERO Kaggle sparse annotation reaches end-to-end edge recall 0.84-0.99 (mean ~0.93) on 11 held-out Kaggle-annotated samples over full 100-frame sequences."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 794, "key": "meth/a3d8ed26: Dense FOCUS-3D node+edge pseudo-labels allow 200 training epochs without overfitting, versus the instability o", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "training usually dense FOCUS3d annotation + frame/augmented frame actually works.\n\nwith dense node and edge (pairing) annotation, i can train up to 200 epochs without overfitting.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Dense FOCUS-3D node+edge pseudo-labels allow 200 training epochs without overfitting, versus the instability others report training on Kaggle's sparse annotation."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 795, "key": "meth/9e902296: The reported best linker in this lane is a SuperGlue-style transformer alternating per-frame self-attention an", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "I design my own transformer following the SuperGlue framework for keypoint matching: alternating self-frame attention and cross-frame attention. [...] 'edge_recall_end_to_end': 0.9644334160463193, 'mean_edge_rank': 0.060891938250428816, 'mean_edge_prob': 0.9515399047913187", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The reported best linker in this lane is a SuperGlue-style transformer alternating per-frame self-attention and cross-frame attention, validated at edge_recall_end_to_end 0.9644 and mean_edge_prob 0.9515 on unseen sample 6bba_337b1b3a with divisions excluded."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 796, "key": "meth/9eb33373: That link transformer is trained with three losses on the pair head - forward and backward softmax matching in", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "forward_match_loss = softmax ( pair candiates, death ) backward_match_loss = softmax ( pair candiates, birth) smiliarity_loss = sigmoid ( pair candiates) [...] We do not cosider cell division, which is handled at higher level. you need longer tracks to predict lineage", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "That link transformer is trained with three losses on the pair head - forward and backward softmax matching including explicit death/birth slots, plus a sigmoid similarity loss - and deliberately has no division head, division being deferred to a higher level."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 797, "key": "meth/b8d84f6c: Because the winning recipe needs no Kaggle annotation, the author argues the linker could in principle be trai", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The implication is that you do not need kaggle annotation to train. So you can do online training on hidden data in theory", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Because the winning recipe needs no Kaggle annotation, the author argues the linker could in principle be trained online on the hidden test volumes."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 798, "key": "meth/8c656fc5: The proposed FOCUS-3D domain-adaptation recipe is a shared backbone with two heads - a FOCUS-3D node-logit hea", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "1) Just pretrain with Focus3D annotation\n2) find matches (hit) of Focus3D and Kaggle zyx to compute diff dzyx\n3) have two heads: one to predict Focus3D zyx, another head dzyx, then Kagge zyx = Focus3D +dzyx\nhint: might as well let diff head predict values in um", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The proposed FOCUS-3D domain-adaptation recipe is a shared backbone with two heads - a FOCUS-3D node-logit head and a Kaggle refinement head predicting a residual offset in microns plus a residual logit - so Kaggle coords = FOCUS-3D coords + dzyx."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 799, "key": "meth/3aba8e2a: The two-head recipe is explicitly a residual-logit formulation: l_K = l_F + delta_l and p_K = p_F + dzyx_um, s", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "p_K = p_F + delta_p_um ; l_K = l_F + delta_l [...] I especially like the residual-logit formulation instead of predicting a completely independent Kaggle probability. It tells the second head: \"preserve what Focus3D already knows; only learn how Kaggle's annotation distribution differs.\"", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The two-head recipe is explicitly a residual-logit formulation: l_K = l_F + delta_l and p_K = p_F + dzyx_um, so the Kaggle head preserves what FOCUS-3D knows and only learns how the Kaggle annotation distribution differs."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 800, "key": "meth/d684d4c7: MEASURED NEGATIVE: Ultrack segmentation was ineffective compared with a purpose-trained detector; ultrack-td c", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Ultrack segmentation proved ineffective for me as well compared to just using our own detector. What Ultrack did seem to do well for me was generating dense tracks, using ultrack-td contours from FOCUS3D segmentations. I have not been able to acheive any meaningful result yet from any of these", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED NEGATIVE: Ultrack segmentation was ineffective compared with a purpose-trained detector; ultrack-td contours from FOCUS-3D segmentations gave dense tracks but still no meaningful result."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 801, "key": "meth/812795a6: MEASURED NEGATIVE: Ultrack centroids only match Kaggle ground truth when a cell is isolated and no oversegment", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "It seems that i cannot get Ultrack oversegment to work.\n\nWhen the cell is isolated and no oversegment is required, Ultrack centroid is close to Kaggle ground truth", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED NEGATIVE: Ultrack centroids only match Kaggle ground truth when a cell is isolated and no oversegmentation is needed; the oversegmentation mode could not be made to work."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 802, "key": "meth/c2841b32: MEASURED NEGATIVE: using FOCUS-3D directly as the detector inside a submission notebook times out; it is only ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "I tried to use focus-3d to segment cells, but it timed out when submitting. Are you doing dense segmentation first when tracking? Or is it just using focus-3d to generate a dense annotation set for training tracking?", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED NEGATIVE: using FOCUS-3D directly as the detector inside a submission notebook times out; it is only viable offline as a pseudo-label generator."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 803, "key": "meth/1abe573c: FOCUS-3D instance segmentation fed into HOCT (Higher-Order Cell Tracking Transformer, royerlab) produces track", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "FOCUS-3D (instance segmentation) --> HOCT (tracking)\n\nhttps://github.com/royerlab/hoct/tree/main\n\nHigher-Order Cell Tracking Transformer\n\nTHICK BLUE: kaggle annotation\n\nOTHER THIN: nearest track from HOCT", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FOCUS-3D instance segmentation fed into HOCT (Higher-Order Cell Tracking Transformer, royerlab) produces tracks whose nearest neighbour to a Kaggle GT track sits within ~1.05-1.49 um, but HOCT tracks run far longer than the sparse GT."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 804, "key": "meth/8ecb14b6: CoTracker works on 2D maximum-intensity projections and tracked cells through long direction shifts where LSA,", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "I found cotracker really good when I converted the 3d to 2d(mip along z) It is able to track the visible cells properly where the classical methods (LSA, motion, ILP) failed. Mostly during the long shifts in direction.\nBut it won't work on 3d and we can't finetune it", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CoTracker works on 2D maximum-intensity projections and tracked cells through long direction shifts where LSA, motion models and ILP failed, but cannot be finetuned to 3D because its positional awareness breaks."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 805, "key": "meth/58518405: MEASURED NEGATIVE: CoTracker applied directly does not account for the morphological change at division and lo", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Cotracker may not help here I tried it, since it does not take into account the morphological changes that happen during division and the pixel to pixel mapping may be lost so you ,ay have to edit a bit of original architecture,", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED NEGATIVE: CoTracker applied directly does not account for the morphological change at division and loses the pixel-to-pixel mapping."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 806, "key": "meth/689a84f5: An error decomposition argues CoTracker-style long-trajectory coherence targets the wrong failure mode: once n", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Once node recall is high, nearest-neighbour on physical distance alone already resolves the large majority of links, and the residual errors concentrate in a small subset where two candidates sit within roughly one cell radius of each other.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "An error decomposition argues CoTracker-style long-trajectory coherence targets the wrong failure mode: once node recall is high, nearest-neighbour distance already resolves most links and residual errors are short-range ambiguities within one cell radius."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 807, "key": "meth/43534698: Full CoTracker-style all-to-all attention over 5 frames x 1280 nodes is judged computationally infeasible; the", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "if we adopt cotracker3 frame work, it is all-to-all attention: frame1 attents to frames 2,3,4,5. this is useful for missing, occluded point tracking and prevent gaps. But this is impossible due to huge computation [...] But we are also given 94% accurate pairwise link", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Full CoTracker-style all-to-all attention over 5 frames x 1280 nodes is judged computationally infeasible; the proposed fix uses the ~94%-accurate pairwise linker to carve 'moving subregions' and runs all-to-all attention only inside them."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 808, "key": "meth/34b15226: Gap recovery is explicitly unsolved in this lane: the author's current method does not help when the gap excee", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "still feel that it is not good enough (does not help if gap is more than one missing frame) … need to dream about it", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Gap recovery is explicitly unsolved in this lane: the author's current method does not help when the gap exceeds one missing frame."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 809, "key": "meth/8580899c: The proposed gap fix is a 5-frame link transformer that self-attends inside each frame then cross-attends to a", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "t1 t2 t3 t4 t5 / A -> A' -> weak/missing -> A''' -> A'''' is conditioned on context from all five frames: p(e_2,3 | 0,1,2,3,4) [...] # still predict ordinary consecutive edge: edge01 = pair_head(h[0], h[1])", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The proposed gap fix is a 5-frame link transformer that self-attends inside each frame then cross-attends to adjacent frames, so a weak or missing detection at t3 is conditioned on all five frames, while still emitting only consecutive-frame edges."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "low", "id": 810, "key": "meth/c8dada7a: An alternative gap strategy is to initialise with the pairwise model and then iteratively re-link with separat", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "brute force idea [...] initalise with pairwise model [...] iterate and update / link t,t+1 attnetion transformer / link t,t+2 / link t,t+3 / link t,t+4", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "An alternative gap strategy is to initialise with the pairwise model and then iteratively re-link with separate t,t+1 / t,t+2 / t,t+3 / t,t+4 attention transformers."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 811, "key": "meth/5c047c40: A proposed detector change is to make detection contextual - cell score at p_i = f(local image, nearby detecte", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "cell score at p_i = f(local image, nearby detected cells, temporal neighbors) [...] UNet / candidate cells + features / node attention / transformer / refined node confidence / link prediction", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A proposed detector change is to make detection contextual - cell score at p_i = f(local image, nearby detected cells, temporal neighbours) - so faint cells survive because neighbouring tracks continue consistently."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 812, "key": "meth/df855166: A concrete alternative to BCE-on-hard-voxels for sparse annotation: reformulate detection as learnable local p", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "instead of classification binary problem, we reformulate it as learnable peak detection. at each pixel location after unet logit head, loss = softmax of pixel over his neighbour.  those pixel without annotation are not computed in loss at all", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A concrete alternative to BCE-on-hard-voxels for sparse annotation: reformulate detection as learnable local peak detection with a softmax over each annotated centre's neighbourhood, so unannotated voxels contribute no loss at all."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 813, "key": "meth/6b77910b: The local-softmax detection loss is L(c) = -log( exp(s(c)/tau) / sum_{v in N(c)} exp(s(v)/tau) ) over the P^3 ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Let the network output a single scalar peak score s(v) for every voxel. For an annotated center c, collect the scores inside its P x P x P neighborhood [...] The annotated center should win the local softmax", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The local-softmax detection loss is L(c) = -log( exp(s(c)/tau) / sum_{v in N(c)} exp(s(v)/tau) ) over the P^3 neighbourhood of each annotated centre, with a margin variant suggested."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 814, "key": "meth/11eb3873: The affinity-field proposal is to port OpenPose Part Affinity Fields to cell linking: predict a local vector f", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "\"Temporal Affinity Fields for 3D Cell Lineage Reconstruction\"\n\n - predicting a local vector field that tells the graph optimizer how cells should connect\n\nHint: create flow GT for supervision using optical flow and sparse annotation tracks", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The affinity-field proposal is to port OpenPose Part Affinity Fields to cell linking: predict a local vector field telling the graph optimizer how cells connect, supervised by optical flow plus sparse tracks. No score was ever reported."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 815, "key": "meth/a8453a67: Measured link displacement statistics over 63,751 GT edges at scale (1,4,4): 99th percentile |dz|=4, |dy|=3, |", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "(63751, 10)\n[1. 2. 2. 4.]\n[0.5  1.25 1.75 3.  ]\n[0.5  1.5  2.   3.75]\nmin       -37.000000    -13.000000     -9.750000  ###???\nmax        35.000000     12.500000      5.750000 ###???", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Measured link displacement statistics over 63,751 GT edges at scale (1,4,4): 99th percentile |dz|=4, |dy|=3, |dx|=3.75 sub-voxels, with extreme outliers reaching -37/+35 in z."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 816, "key": "meth/cb55dc6e: Visualising the host baseline (temporal unet + link transformer) max-probability links shows long links are al", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Visualisation results from host repo baseline code (temporal unet + link transformer). i show max prob link.\n\n - long links are almost wrong (easy to filter such results)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Visualising the host baseline (temporal unet + link transformer) max-probability links shows long links are almost always wrong, making link length a cheap filter."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "low", "id": 817, "key": "meth/9e459024: An alternative linking formulation: render detected points into one volume colour-coded by t and run an ORDINA", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Once you get detection point, you can plot everything once in zxy and color code using t. Hence it become 3d conv ( instead of 4d conv)you can think of recolor the points from t to linkage colors ( s,l,d,e ). Temporal unet —> ordinary unet ( like detecting blood vessel)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "An alternative linking formulation: render detected points into one volume colour-coded by t and run an ORDINARY 3D U-Net to segment the motion line joining zyx0 and zyx1, labelling voxels start/inside/divide/end like blood-vessel segmentation."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 818, "key": "meth/bd2f6142: The recommended diagnostic protocol is edge recall ~= node recall^2 x conditional linking accuracy, holding de", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Edge recall ≈ node recall² × conditional linking accuracy [...] Then keep the detector fixed while comparing linkers, and keep the linker fixed while comparing detectors. Otherwise, post-processing can appear to improve locally by changing the candidate set and graph simultaneously.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The recommended diagnostic protocol is edge recall ~= node recall^2 x conditional linking accuracy, holding detector fixed while comparing linkers and vice versa, since post-processing can appear to help by changing candidate set and graph at once."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 819, "key": "meth/d962d94e: A participant running systematic experiments concludes the public UNet/node-transformer is still a very strong", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "From my experiments, Pilkwang's UNet/node-transformer is still a very strong backbone. If conditional linking is already high, I would focus on detector calibration/precision and division ranking before spending much more time on a new linker.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A participant running systematic experiments concludes the public UNet/node-transformer is still a very strong backbone and that detector calibration/precision plus division ranking beat a new linker as a place to spend effort."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 820, "key": "meth/cb89cc99: Multiple participants report the public checkpoint plus post-processing has hit a hard ceiling and that retrai", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "I'd recommend retraining the model instead of just using the public ckpt.\nunet+transformer is a strong approach, but there are still things you can improve, like preprocessing, model architecture\nthe current ckpt has kind of hit a wall, it's hard to get more gain from post-processing alone.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Multiple participants report the public checkpoint plus post-processing has hit a hard ceiling and that retraining, not more post-processing, is the required move."}
