{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 841, "key": "meth/2c0f8285: Submission timeouts are NOT scoring cost - local scoring over all train data runs in just over a minute - they", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "this is not caused by scoring; local scoring on all train data runs just over 1 minute. All the other time goes into constructing your prediction. Time how long your code takes over test set of 4 videos, scale it up to approximate full test set", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Submission timeouts are NOT scoring cost - local scoring over all train data runs in just over a minute - they are prediction construction time."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 842, "key": "meth/861cd5e7: MEASURED NEGATIVE for a classical Otsu/watershed + NN + graph-repair pipeline: raising the detection threshold", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "raising the detection threshold to cut nodes 17% costs ~0.18 edge Jaccard (there are no junk detections to remove; the node count reflects real cell density). Not a viable lever.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED NEGATIVE for a classical Otsu/watershed + NN + graph-repair pipeline: raising the detection threshold to cut nodes by 17% costs about 0.18 edge Jaccard, because that node count reflects real cell density rather than junk."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 843, "key": "meth/3aacf239: DATA: the microscope acquired multiple views per volume which were fused, with small inter-view intensity devi", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The microscope acquired multiple views of each volume, which were then fused.\nThere may be small intensity deviations between views, so they were linearly scaled to match a reference view.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "DATA: the microscope acquired multiple views per volume which were fused, with small inter-view intensity deviations linearly rescaled to a reference view."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 844, "key": "meth/d9330c19: A held-out motion-field study on 7,368 unique ordinary-persistence edges reports 0.570 um per-coordinate error", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "on 7,368 unique held-out ordinary-persistence edges, the selected motion field achieved approximately 0.570 µm error per coordinate versus 1.035 µm for a zero-motion baseline, a 44.9% reduction.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A held-out motion-field study on 7,368 unique ordinary-persistence edges reports 0.570 um per-coordinate error versus 1.035 um for a zero-motion baseline, a 44.9% reduction."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "low", "id": 845, "key": "meth/b136b5eb: Unmeasured suggestion: V-JEPA 2.1 as unsupervised pretraining, on the argument that it behaves like unsupervis", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Tracking needs dense, spatially precise features. The newer V-JEPA 2.1 specifically targets dense, spatially structured and temporally consistent features ….\n\nV-JEPA 2.1 behaves like unsupervised optical flow in feature space,", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Unmeasured suggestion: V-JEPA 2.1 as unsupervised pretraining, on the argument that it behaves like unsupervised optical flow in feature space; no result was ever reported."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 846, "key": "meth/f0de0745: MEASURED NEGATIVE for classical flow trackers: Ultrack and byotrack disagree wherever predicted movement is la", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "results of ultrack and byotrack. They tend to disagree when there is \"large predicted movement\". this is because the optimization cost forces it to \"always\" link to something\n\n\"link cost < termination cost + new-track cost\"", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED NEGATIVE for classical flow trackers: Ultrack and byotrack disagree wherever predicted movement is large, because the optimisation cost forces a link whenever link cost < termination cost + new-track cost."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 847, "key": "meth/ecb30345: RULES: training a model offline and loading it as a private Kaggle Dataset in the submission notebook is permi", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "That's permitted, but you should be able to reproduce it in case you win, see https://www.kaggle.com/WinningModelDocumentationGuidelines", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "RULES: training a model offline and loading it as a private Kaggle Dataset in the submission notebook is permitted, subject to reproducibility on a win."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 848, "key": "meth/0913d5fa: Ensemble-of-open-source-trackers strategy: use consistency across 5 open-source trackers to manufacture dense ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Imagine if i use 5 open source tracker, and using consistency, i can have more short tracks (currently we have less than 1% link labelled and the rest are unlabelled)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Ensemble-of-open-source-trackers strategy: use consistency across 5 open-source trackers to manufacture dense short-track pseudo-labels, since currently less than 1% of links are labelled."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 849, "key": "meth/349d0cd2: The 0.947-tier support pack ships the organisers' TemporalUNet3D and SimpleNodeTransformer BYTE-IDENTICAL to r", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "$ diff -u reference/royerlab-baseline/src/tracking_cellmot/models/temporal_unet.py evidence/datasets/support-pack/repo/src/biohub_tracking/models/temporal_unet.py  ->  (no output; likewise for simple_node_transformer.py)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 0.947-tier support pack ships the organisers' TemporalUNet3D and SimpleNodeTransformer BYTE-IDENTICAL to reference/royerlab-baseline - the public tier has not changed the architecture at all, only training length, hyperparameters and post-processing."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 850, "key": "meth/573b8834: The public 0.947 checkpoint was trained for 400 EPOCHS with window_size=2, not the 50 epochs the training scri", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "\"artifact_name\": \"biohub-tracking-support-pack-400ep-snapshot-v1\" [...] \"config\": {\"downsample\": [1,4,4], \"pool_kernel_um\": 5.0, \"unet_layers\": [32,64,128], \"unet_out_channels\": 32, \"window_size\": 2}", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The public 0.947 checkpoint was trained for 400 EPOCHS with window_size=2, not the 50 epochs the training script documents as its example - so reported 50-epoch training costs understate the real budget 8x."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 851, "key": "meth/e2125a2a: TemporalUNet3D DISABLES temporal attention at the full-resolution stage by default (skip_fullres_temporal=True", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "If True (default), replace the temporal-attention block at the full-resolution (first) encoder stage with an Identity. Per-voxel attention at full res dominates both memory and runtime; skipping it gives ~3x speedup and ~30% less memory with negligible quality loss in practice.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "TemporalUNet3D DISABLES temporal attention at the full-resolution stage by default (skip_fullres_temporal=True), so per-voxel time attention runs only at the 1/2 and 1/4 stages; the organisers claim ~3x speedup and ~30% less memory for negligible quality loss. Re-enabling it is an untested axis."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 852, "key": "meth/54b93747: SimpleNodeTransformer has NO intra-frame self-attention - only bidirectional cross-attention between t and t+1", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "# Bi-directional cross-attention: t attends to t+1 and vice versa.\n        for block in self.blocks:\n            ...\n                q = grad_ckpt(_q_fn, q, k, mask_t1, use_reentrant=False)\n                k = grad_ckpt(_k_fn, k, q, mask_t, use_reentrant=False)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "SimpleNodeTransformer has NO intra-frame self-attention - only bidirectional cross-attention between t and t+1 - which is exactly the component the SuperGlue-style proposal in topic 738217 adds, making that a genuine architectural delta rather than a restatement."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 853, "key": "meth/57edb1e6: BUG / FREE LEVER: the edge loss contains a division-upweighting hook that is a no-op - weight is initialised t", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "div_rows = target.sum(dim=1) > 1\n    weight = torch.ones_like(loss)\n    weight[div_rows] = 1.0\n\n    return (loss * weight)[mask].mean()", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "BUG / FREE LEVER: the edge loss contains a division-upweighting hook that is a no-op - weight is initialised to ones and division rows are then set to 1.0 - so divisions, worth 0.1 of the metric, have never actually been upweighted in the public training recipe."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 854, "key": "meth/a75dd271: The baseline edge loss is a FOCAL (gamma=2) BCE applied to a COLUMN-softmax over sources, masked to rows or co", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "probs = torch.softmax(logits, dim=0)  # dim=0 intentional: divisions allowed, merges aren't\n    bce = F.binary_cross_entropy(probs, target, reduction=\"none\")\n    p_t = probs * target + (1 - probs) * (1 - target)\n    loss = ((1 - p_t) ** 2) * bce", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The baseline edge loss is a FOCAL (gamma=2) BCE applied to a COLUMN-softmax over sources, masked to rows or columns that carry sparse annotation - not a plain sigmoid BCE; the dim=0 softmax is a deliberate structural prior that divisions are allowed but merges are not."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 855, "key": "meth/da4f67a0: TRAIN/INFERENCE MISMATCH: at training the linker sees peaks selected by RAW LOGIT > 0.3 (prob ~0.574), but at ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "train: det_threshold: float = 0.3 ... is_peak = (det_logits == pooled) & (det_logits > det_threshold)\npredict: is_peak = (logits == pooled) & (torch.sigmoid(logits) > det_threshold)\n--det-threshold ... default=0.99", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "TRAIN/INFERENCE MISMATCH: at training the linker sees peaks selected by RAW LOGIT > 0.3 (prob ~0.574), but at inference peaks are selected by SIGMOID(logit) > det_threshold with a CLI default of 0.99 - so the learned linker is never trained on the sparse high-precision candidate set it is scored on."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 856, "key": "meth/6e55852e: The training-time detection-to-GT association radius is max_match_distance=5.0 um, stricter than the metric's ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "    det_threshold: float = 0.3,\n    pool_kernel_um: float = 5.0,\n    max_match_distance: float = 5.0,", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The training-time detection-to-GT association radius is max_match_distance=5.0 um, stricter than the metric's 7 um matching radius, so the linker's supervision discards detections the scorer would have counted as hits."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 857, "key": "meth/e90e8ec1: DETECTION CEILING: the (1,4,4) downsample provably destroys ground-truth nodes - the training code emits an ex", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "f\"Sample {b}: {n_gt - n_unique}/{n_gt} GT nodes collapsed to \"\n                f\"duplicate voxels after downsampling — these are undetectable.\",", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "DETECTION CEILING: the (1,4,4) downsample provably destroys ground-truth nodes - the training code emits an explicit warning that GT nodes collapse to duplicate voxels after downsampling and are thereby undetectable."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 858, "key": "meth/b47e8283: The detection loss is a per-sample renormalised weighted BCE - weight_pos = 1/n_pos and weight_neg = neg_weigh", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "# Per-sample normalisation: weight_pos = 1/n_pos, weight_neg = neg_weight/n_neg.\n    n_pos = target.reshape(B, -1).sum(dim=1).clamp(min=1)\n    n_neg = (target.numel() // B - n_pos).clamp(min=1)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The detection loss is a per-sample renormalised weighted BCE - weight_pos = 1/n_pos and weight_neg = neg_weight/n_neg - so det_neg_weight=1e-2 is a ratio applied after count normalisation, not a raw class weight."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 859, "key": "meth/cce2f89e: A DIFFERENT and directly usable detector formulation exists on disk: a full-frame 3D U-Net center-heatmap dete", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The loss is positive-unlabelled: labelled centers are strong positives, dark background is a normal negative, and bright unlabelled voxels receive a small weight so sparse labels do not become hard false negatives.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A DIFFERENT and directly usable detector formulation exists on disk: a full-frame 3D U-Net center-heatmap detector with a POSITIVE-UNLABELLED loss, where bright unlabelled voxels get a small weight instead of becoming hard false negatives - the exact failure mode that destabilises sparse-annotation training."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 860, "key": "meth/b602894f: The PU detector's concrete weights are w_pos=12.0 for labelled centres, w_bg=1.0 for voxels below the 40th int", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "weights = np.full(heatmap.shape, cfg.w_ignore, dtype=np.float32)\n    bg_cutoff = float(np.quantile(image, cfg.bg_quantile))\n    weights[image < bg_cutoff] = cfg.w_bg\n    weights[heatmap > cfg.pos_thresh] = cfg.w_pos", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The PU detector's concrete weights are w_pos=12.0 for labelled centres, w_bg=1.0 for voxels below the 40th intensity percentile, and w_ignore=0.05 for everything else (bright unlabelled tissue), on a Gaussian heatmap target with sigma=1.0 rather than single hard voxels."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 861, "key": "meth/0fc60653: The PU detector also normalises intensities by percentile (50th to 99.5th, clipped -0.5 to 6.0) rather than us", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "gauss_sigma: float = 1.0\n    pos_thresh: float = 0.05\n    bg_quantile: float = 0.40\n    w_pos: float = 12.0\n    w_bg: float = 1.0\n    w_ignore: float = 0.05\n\n    norm_lo_pct: float = 50.0\n    norm_hi_pct: float = 99.5\n    norm_clip_lo: float = -0.5\n    norm_clip_hi: float = 6.0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The PU detector also normalises intensities by percentile (50th to 99.5th, clipped -0.5 to 6.0) rather than using raw values, which matters because the hosts confirmed multi-view fusion with per-view linear rescaling."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 862, "key": "meth/7f3ce1e7: GAP RECOVERY, documented design: the full-frame centre detector is intended purely as a conservative node-resc", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "\"recommended_use\": \"Use only as a high-confidence rescue prior near graph gaps, short components, or unmatched motion endpoints. Avoid adding all peaks directly to the node set.\", \"role\": \"auxiliary_center_prior\"", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "GAP RECOVERY, documented design: the full-frame centre detector is intended purely as a conservative node-rescue prior near graph gaps, short components and unmatched motion endpoints - with an explicit warning against adding all its peaks to the node set."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 863, "key": "meth/f1b12a25: The rescue gate is calibrated by sweeping peak thresholds 0.10 to 0.80 against a 7 um match radius on 240 eval", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "--gate-thresholds \"${BIOHUB_FULL_FRAME_GATE_THRESHOLDS:-0.10,0.15,0.20,0.25,0.30,0.40,0.50,0.60,0.70,0.80}\"\n  --gate-match-radius-um \"${BIOHUB_FULL_FRAME_GATE_MATCH_RADIUS_UM:-7.0}\"\n  --gate-eval-frames \"${BIOHUB_FULL_FRAME_GATE_EVAL_FRAMES:-240}\"", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The rescue gate is calibrated by sweeping peak thresholds 0.10 to 0.80 against a 7 um match radius on 240 evaluation frames, producing gate_summary.json and gate_threshold_metrics.csv - a ready-made calibration protocol for node rescue."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 864, "key": "meth/975cf16d: The gate diagnostics carry an explicit caveat that sparse-label precision and recall are calibration features,", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "\"warning\": \"Sparse-label precision/recall are calibration features, not complete-cell metrics.\"", "review_after": null, "source_id": 74, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The gate diagnostics carry an explicit caveat that sparse-label precision and recall are calibration features, not complete-cell metrics - so a rescue threshold tuned on them cannot be read as a detection quality number."}
