{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 920, "key": "meta/17b7c2b7: An offline-installable numpy+scipy reimplementation of the official scorer exists and was verified against the", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "It is verified, not assumed: against the official implementation on real ground truth with 14 randomised perturbation families (jitter, dropped nodes, duplicate detections, gap edges, backward edges, rewired links, out-degree 3, far-away noise) across 6 datasets — 84 cases, 0 mismatches on TP/FP/FN.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "An offline-installable numpy+scipy reimplementation of the official scorer exists and was verified against the official implementation on real GT with 14 randomised perturbation families across 6 datasets: 84 cases, 0 mismatches on TP/FP/FN."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 921, "key": "meta/40dc73e3: The official node matching MAXIMISES the sum of 1/(1+d) after a hard 7 um threshold; it does NOT minimise tota", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "matching is threshold first, then maximum-weight bipartite matching on `1/(1+d)` — *not* minimum total distance. Different objective, different result in dense regions.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The official node matching MAXIMISES the sum of 1/(1+d) after a hard 7 um threshold; it does NOT minimise total distance, and the two objectives differ in dense regions."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 922, "key": "meta/f9c15ff7: A predicted edge is graded only when it touches annotated evidence: source matched a GT node with out-degree>0", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "An edge is graded when it touches annotated evidence: its source matched a GT node that HAS an outgoing edge, or its target matched one that HAS an incoming edge. Everything else is invisible ... predicting links between unannotated cells is free", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A predicted edge is graded only when it touches annotated evidence: source matched a GT node with out-degree>0, OR target matched a GT node with in-degree>0. Everything else is invisible, so links between unannotated cells are free."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 923, "key": "meta/c6b79fe2: Predicted edges with dt != 1 (gaps, backward, same-frame, self) are silently DROPPED by the scorer before coun", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The organisers' `metrics.md` says such an edge \"is counted as a false positive\", and their own `test_skip_connection_scores_zero` docstring reasons that way too — but both readings score 0 in that particular test, so the test cannot tell them apart. The code drops the edge.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Predicted edges with dt != 1 (gaps, backward, same-frame, self) are silently DROPPED by the scorer before counting - neither TP nor FP nor FN - contradicting the official metrics.md and the organisers' own test docstring, which say they count as false positives."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 924, "key": "meta/82864730: The only way to recover a missed detection is to INTERPOLATE THE NODE and emit two dt=1 edges: a gap edge scor", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Drawing the gap edge scores exactly the same as drawing nothing (0.000 both). Inserting an interpolated node inside the radius restores the full 1.000. Miss the radius and you pay 2 FPs instead. With median step 1.8 um against a 7 um radius, linear interpolation between t-1 and t+1 lands inside", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The only way to recover a missed detection is to INTERPOLATE THE NODE and emit two dt=1 edges: a gap edge scores identically to predicting nothing (0.000 vs 0.000), while an interpolated node inside the radius restores J=1.000."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 925, "key": "meta/09404d6a: A single duplicate detection 0.4 um from a real cell HALVES the edge Jaccard (1.000 -> 0.500): only one twin c", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "a duplicate detection halves the score. Only one of the two twins can win the one-to-one match; the loser's edges still touch annotated evidence, so they are graded as FPs. 1.000 -> 0.500 from one extra peak 0.4 um away.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A single duplicate detection 0.4 um from a real cell HALVES the edge Jaccard (1.000 -> 0.500): only one twin can win the one-to-one match and the loser's edges are still graded, so they become FPs. NMS quality outranks every other lever."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 926, "key": "meta/76333390: Centroid precision has a measured cliff at sigma ~2 um, far below the 7 um match radius: 1.5 um is free, 2.5 u", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The cliff is at sigma ~= 2 um — about 1.2 voxels in z and 5 voxels in xy. Below 1.5 um it is free; at 2.5 um you have lost 16 %; at 3 um, 41 %; at 4 um, 74 %. The curve is essentially identical for both embryos ... The failure is driven by inter-cell spacing, not cell size.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Centroid precision has a measured cliff at sigma ~2 um, far below the 7 um match radius: 1.5 um is free, 2.5 um costs 16%, 3 um costs 41%, 4 um costs 74% - driven by ~9-10 um inter-cell spacing, not cell size, and identical in both embryos."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 927, "key": "meta/ce171eac: Ground truth annotation density over all 199 train files is 2.82%: 133,318 annotated nodes against 4,725,117 e", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Ground truth is 2.82 % dense (133,318 annotated nodes vs. an estimated 4,725,117 real cells). A dense heatmap loss over the whole volume trains the model to call real cells background.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Ground truth annotation density over all 199 train files is 2.82%: 133,318 annotated nodes against 4,725,117 estimated real cells, so a dense heatmap loss trains the model to call real cells background."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 928, "key": "meta/e646552c: The GT graph is unusually clean: 100% of edges span exactly one frame, zero gaps, zero merges, zero out-degree", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The GT graph is unusually clean: 100 % of edges span exactly one frame, zero gaps, zero merges, zero out-degree > 2, and only 151 divisions.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The GT graph is unusually clean: 100% of edges span exactly one frame, zero gaps, zero merges, zero out-degree>2, and only 151 divisions across the entire training corpus."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 929, "key": "meta/81cde522: The 199 'samples' are overlapping crops of two movies: decoding GEFF node ids as (t+offset)*BASE+label shows 6", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "decoding the GEFF node ids shows the 199 samples are overlapping crops of two movies — 6,206 of the 8,128 `6bba` file pairs share annotated cells, up to 29 % of the smaller file. A random per-video split leaks at the *cell* level.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 199 'samples' are overlapping crops of two movies: decoding GEFF node ids as (t+offset)*BASE+label shows 6,206 of 8,128 6bba file pairs share annotated cells, up to 29% of the smaller file - so a random per-video split leaks at the CELL level, not merely by correlation."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 930, "key": "meta/ab42eb06: GEFF node ids decode as (t + offset) * BASE + label with BASE=1e6 for 6bba and 1e9 for 44b6, verified on all 1", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The ids look arbitrary, but they are `(t + offset) * BASE + label`: `BASE` is 1e6 for `6bba` and 1e9 for `44b6` — a per-embryo convention. Verified on all 199 training files with zero exceptions. `offset - 1` is the crop's start frame in the source movie", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "GEFF node ids decode as (t + offset) * BASE + label with BASE=1e6 for 6bba and 1e9 for 44b6, verified on all 199 training files with zero exceptions; offset-1 is the crop's start frame in the source movie."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 931, "key": "meta/4cce17c4: Leave-one-embryo-out gives only TWO folds and they are lopsided: 6bba carries 113k annotated nodes against 44b", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The honest local estimate is leave-one-embryo-out, which gives you exactly two folds, and they are lopsided: 6bba carries 113k annotated nodes against 44b6's 20k", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Leave-one-embryo-out gives only TWO folds and they are lopsided: 6bba carries 113k annotated nodes against 44b6's 20k, so per-sample weighting lets the larger embryo dominate any CV number."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 932, "key": "meta/749a0598: Recall beats the node-count bonus analytically: edge TP requires BOTH endpoints to match so J ~ r^2 in recall ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Don't chase the bonus: edge TP needs BOTH endpoints, so J ~ r^2 in recall r, and d/dr[r^2 * (1.1 - 0.1r)] > 0 on [0, 1]. Recall always wins.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Recall beats the node-count bonus analytically: edge TP requires BOTH endpoints to match so J ~ r^2 in recall r, and d/dr[r^2*(1.1-0.1r)] > 0 on [0,1] - under-predicting never pays."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 933, "key": "meta/94471e05: Measured Kaggle T4x2 calibration: 104 ms per volume to read and decompress and 210 ms per volume for a full-re", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "a Kaggle T4 x2 session measured 104 ms per volume to read and decompress, and 210 ms per volume for a full-resolution 64-channel fp16 3D conv stack — about 4.8 % each of the 12 h budget for ~19,900 volumes. GPU time is not the constraint; the 4 vCPUs are.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Measured Kaggle T4x2 calibration: 104 ms per volume to read and decompress and 210 ms per volume for a full-res 64-channel fp16 3D conv stack, about 4.8% each of the 12h budget for ~19,900 volumes - the 4 vCPUs, not the GPU, are the constraint."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 934, "key": "meta/35702e4d: Each visible test/ volume is byte-identical to a train volume down to the sha256 of every chunk and ships no .", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "each one is byte-identical to a train volume down to the sha256 of every chunk, and none of them ships a `.geff` of its own. They are there to show the submission format ... the visible `test/` volumes are useless as validation — you already have their labels", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Each visible test/ volume is byte-identical to a train volume down to the sha256 of every chunk and ships no .geff of its own, so the visible test set is useless as validation and is only a format example."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 935, "key": "meta/68ce8ddd: Normalisation should use the per-dataset image_statistics quantiles shipped in zarr.json (0.001/0.999), never ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "NEVER scale by /65535 -- that would squash every cell into the bottom ... clip and scale with the 0.001/0.999 pair. Deriving them yourself means decompressing", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Normalisation should use the per-dataset image_statistics quantiles shipped in zarr.json (0.001/0.999), never /65535: the global max is only a small fraction of the uint16 range."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 936, "key": "meta/37faa6e1: Node-count tuning is a measured DEAD END with three LB datapoints: offline validator said +0.013 from min-trac", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "| baseline fork | 0.9368 | 0.938 |\n| min length 10 + det 0.98 + per-type | 0.9495 | 0.934 |\n| my own pipeline, same policy | 0.9523 | 0.865 |\n\nOffline said plus 0.013. The leaderboard said minus 0.004.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Node-count tuning is a measured DEAD END with three LB datapoints: offline validator said +0.013 from min-track-length and detection-threshold filtering, the public LB said -0.004 (0.938 -> 0.934), and on their own pipeline 0.9523 offline became 0.865 on LB."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 937, "key": "meta/5042c997: A metric term that rewards absence cannot be validated on sparse labels: deleting a track only registers a cos", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A metric term that rewards absence is the hardest kind to validate on sparse labels. The labels can confirm the reward. They cannot confirm the price.\n\nIf you're testing anything that reduces output volume, check what fraction of what you removed was even labelled, before you believe the score.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A metric term that rewards absence cannot be validated on sparse labels: deleting a track only registers a cost if that track happened to be annotated, so the labels confirm the reward but never the price."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 938, "key": "meta/7188dde7: An offline ruler agreeing with the LB at the baseline says nothing about whether it RANKS configurations corre", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "an offline ruler agreeing with your leaderboard score at the baseline tells you nothing about whether it *ranks* things correctly. Mine matched at 0.9368 against 0.938, and then pointed the wrong way.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "An offline ruler agreeing with the LB at the baseline says nothing about whether it RANKS configurations correctly: 0.9368 offline matched 0.938 on LB, then pointed the wrong way for four submissions."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 939, "key": "meta/f787dba7: The two embryo types have wildly different label density - one 0.2 to 1.3 percent of nodes labelled, the other", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The two embryo types have very different label density. One has 0.2 to 1.3 percent of its nodes labelled. The other has 13 to 15. So throwing away short tracks is nearly free on the sparse one and expensive on the dense one.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The two embryo types have wildly different label density - one 0.2 to 1.3 percent of nodes labelled, the other 13 to 15 percent - so any volume-reducing policy is nearly free on the sparse one and expensive on the dense one, which makes per-type tuning look falsely good."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 940, "key": "meta/132dc386: Adoptable diagnostic: split_score(J, n_pred, n_est) decomposes adjusted edge Jaccard into the raw Jaccard and ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "def split_score(J, n_pred, n_est, k=0.1):\n    mult = 1.0 - k * (n_pred - n_est) / n_est\n    return {\"J\": J, \"multiplier\": mult, \"adj\": max(0.0, J * mult}\n ... flag = \"  <-- almost all multiplier, treat as unproven\" if share > 0.7", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Adoptable diagnostic: split_score(J, n_pred, n_est) decomposes adjusted edge Jaccard into the raw Jaccard and the node-count multiplier, flagging any change where >70% of the delta came from the multiplier as unproven."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 941, "key": "meta/40b88465: The split_score replica formula matches the official scorer exactly - support/metrics.py computes adj_edge_jac", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "adj_edge_jaccard = max(\n            0.0, edge_jaccard * (1 - ADJUSTMENT_ALPHA * total_node_ratio),\n        )", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The split_score replica formula matches the official scorer exactly - support/metrics.py computes adj_edge_jaccard = max(0.0, edge_jaccard * (1 - ADJUSTMENT_ALPHA * total_node_ratio)) with ALPHA=0.1 and no upper clip - so the offline/LB divergence is a label-sparsity effect, not a scorer mismatch."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 942, "key": "meta/79072158: A reusable 8-case mock-test suite (A-H) exercises every metric behaviour against the OFFICIAL scorer on tiny h", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The evaluation code (`tracking_cellmot.metrics`) depends only on `tracksdata` and `polars` — not on PyTorch, CUDA, or zarr. So this tutorial stays light", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A reusable 8-case mock-test suite (A-H) exercises every metric behaviour against the OFFICIAL scorer on tiny hand-built graphs, needs no GPU, no zarr and no competition data - only tracksdata and polars - making it a cheap regression harness for any local scorer wrapper."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 943, "key": "meta/27f34122: An extra wrong edge creates a PHANTOM DIVISION: adding A0->A2 alongside correct edges gives one edge FP and al", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A0 now has out-degree 2, so the metric also sees a *spurious division* ⇒ a division FP appears too. Great illustration of how edge mistakes can create phantom divisions.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "An extra wrong edge creates a PHANTOM DIVISION: adding A0->A2 alongside correct edges gives one edge FP and also one division FP, because the metric detects divisions purely as out-degree 2."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 944, "key": "meta/c7a53057: Unmatched predicted nodes are NOT punished as false positives - the metric assumes they are unannotated real c", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A predicted node with no GT node within 7 µm stays unmatched (and, crucially, is *not* punished as an FP — the GT is sparse, so unmatched predictions are usually just unannotated real cells).", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Unmatched predicted nodes are NOT punished as false positives - the metric assumes they are unannotated real cells - so over-detection only costs via the node-count penalty, never via the raw Jaccard."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 945, "key": "meta/ed70b564: The 7 um match radius is anisotropic in voxels: 4.3 voxels in z but 17.2 voxels in x/y, because z is 4x coarse", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "the 7 um matching radius is 4.3 voxels in z but 17.2 voxels in x/y. Distances have to be computed in micrometres -- hence `zyx * scale`", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 7 um match radius is anisotropic in voxels: 4.3 voxels in z but 17.2 voxels in x/y, because z is 4x coarser (1.625 vs 0.40625 um/voxel), so the same voxel error costs 4x more in z."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 946, "key": "meta/ae0018e9: Frame-to-frame displacement measured across all 199 training movies and 128,883 GT links: median 1.82 um, p95 ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Frame-to-frame displacement: median 1.82 µm, 95th percentile 5.34 µm, 99th percentile 8.38 µm. A nearest-neighbour linker with a radius of 8.4 µm can reach 99% of the true links. In voxels that is about 21 in x/y and 5 in z", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Frame-to-frame displacement measured across all 199 training movies and 128,883 GT links: median 1.82 um, p95 5.34 um, p99 8.38 um, so an 8.4 um linking radius reaches 99% of true links and 5.3 um reaches 95%."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 947, "key": "meta/b6194f87: UNTAKEN IDEA: the public 0.908 pipelines inherit the metric's 7.0 um node-matching gate as their LINKING gate,", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The public 0.908 pipelines use 7.0 µm as their linking gate as well. It reads like a recommendation. It is not — it is the tolerance for *node position error*, and using it as a *motion* gate throws away every true link longer than 7 µm.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "UNTAKEN IDEA: the public 0.908 pipelines inherit the metric's 7.0 um node-matching gate as their LINKING gate, but the two are unrelated - the scorer never checks predicted edge length - so a 7 um motion gate discards ~2% of true links for free."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 948, "key": "meta/68597177: UNTAKEN IDEA: the 7 um motion-gate loss is per-movie, not per-link, so a per-movie gate set from that movie's ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A fifth of the movies lose nothing at 7 µm; a third lose more than 2%, and the worst loses one link in seven. A global gate tuned on the pooled distribution is tuned for the median movie and wrong for exactly the movies where it matters.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "UNTAKEN IDEA: the 7 um motion-gate loss is per-movie, not per-link, so a per-movie gate set from that movie's own p99 candidate distance beats any global constant."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 949, "key": "meta/da73f981: Of the true links a 7 um gate discards, about two-thirds are fast in-plane motion and one-third are z-dominate", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Of the links a 7 µm gate discards, about two-thirds are cells moving fast across the plane and one-third are dominated by z — a median of three z-voxels, which is exactly the kind of step a search neighbourhood expressed in \"voxels in every direction\" would fail to reach.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Of the true links a 7 um gate discards, about two-thirds are fast in-plane motion and one-third are z-dominated with a median of three z-voxels, so a neighbourhood expressed in 'voxels in every direction' fails on the tall minority."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 950, "key": "meta/d79ed414: Divisions are 151 events across all 199 movies against ~130,000 links - one link in 853 - and most movies cont", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Divisions: 151 across all 199 movies, against ~130,000 links. One link in 853 is a division. A tracker that hypothesises divisions eagerly will spend all of its error budget on them.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Divisions are 151 events across all 199 movies against ~130,000 links - one link in 853 - and most movies contain no division at all."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 951, "key": "meta/1ffe0168: The ground truth follows only ~22 tracks per movie averaging ~30 of 100 frames and never skips a frame within ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The ground truth follows ~22 tracks per movie, not every cell, but never skips a frame within a track. An unannotated blob is not a missed detection.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 76, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "meta-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The ground truth follows only ~22 tracks per movie averaging ~30 of 100 frames and never skips a frame within a track, so there is no gap-closing to learn and an unannotated blob is not evidence of a missed detection."}
