{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1035, "key": "node_count_multiplier_measured_at_scale", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "121,782 nodes -> 0.954. 67,292 nodes (-44.7%) -> 0.954 ... the hosts are aware... its the expected behavior", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED AT SCALE by a competitor (topic 739686): an identical pipeline, two submissions differing ONLY in a track-deletion step. 121,782 nodes -> 0.954. 67,292 nodes, a 44.7% cut -> 0.954, IDENTICAL. Their weighted adjustment factor went 1.0009 -> 1.0500, exactly cancelling the lost edge TPs. 0.908 x 1.05 = 0.953 reproduces it. Crucially T_true is NOT hidden: it is estimated_number_of_nodes in the GEFF metadata shipping with the input, READABLE AT INFERENCE TIME. A competitor reports the hosts are aware and call it expected behaviour, and the Evaluation page says scores above 1.0 are expected."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1036, "key": "CONTRADICTION_node_pruning (RESOLVED 2026-09-11 by tools/prune_experiment.py - see RESOLVED_node_pruning)", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "node-budget pruning: optimum = no pruning (pooled-OOF) vs 121,782->67,292 nodes scoring identically", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1189, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "OPEN CONTRADICTION, do not resolve silently. The measurement above says a 44.7% node cut is score-NEUTRAL because the multiplier compensates. But our own monolith measured 'node-budget pruning: optimum was NO pruning at all' (pooled-OOF), and zhincez measured node-count tuning at offline +0.013 against LB -0.004 over three datapoints. Most likely reconciliation: WHICH nodes are removed. The 739686 result deletes whole TRACKS (removing nodes whose edges were already contributing little), whereas threshold pruning removes nodes scattered across surviving tracks and destroys edge TPs on both sides. TEST LOCALLY with evaluate_datasets() before betting a submission on either reading."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1037, "key": "binding_constraint_is_absolute_fork_COUNT", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "one competitor's ILP emitted 1 true division alongside 3,260 false ones", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The binding constraint on division_jaccard is ABSOLUTE FORK COUNT, not precision. False positives enter the denominator 1:1 against only ~151 GT divisions, so reaching divJ 0.22 needs a whole-corpus fork budget in the LOW THOUSANDS. One competitor's ILP emitted 1 true division alongside 3,260 false ones. Consequence: weight-tuning an existing ILP provably cannot get there. Every published division_jaccard: 0.000 (any 1:1 Hungarian linker, structurally - 40 FN of 40), 0.0004, 0.03, 0.12, ~0.2, 0.22."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1038, "key": "sister_geometry_figures_were_RETRACTED", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The failure is a base rate, not an overlap you can tune away.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 8.47um / 10.22um sister-geometry figures circulating in the forum were RETRACTED by their own author: they were DETECTOR LOCALISATION ERROR, not division geometry. The re-measurement on labels gives continuation median 1.724um (p99 6.906), division step 4.569um, sister separation 8.849um, and at a 7um gate a continuation:division ratio of 30:1."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1039, "key": "zebrahub_tracks_are_ultrack_pseudolabels", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "its *_tracks.csv are Ultrack pseudo-labels carrying systematic errors especially around divisions", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CAUTION on Zebrahub, which the host explicitly permitted: its *_tracks.csv are ULTRACK PSEUDO-LABELS, not curated ground truth, and they carry systematic errors ESPECIALLY AROUND DIVISIONS. Training a division model on them means learning Ultrack's division failures."}
