{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1041, "key": "possible_surviving_seam_in_evaluate", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "evaluate() applies the one-timepoint filter and out-degree cap only inside the edge computation", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A possible surviving seam, derived from CODE not a thread: evaluate() applies the one-timepoint filter and the out-degree cap only inside the EDGE computation, then hands the RAW graph to evaluate_divisions(). Combined with pred_lineages = [{child, *successors(child)}], a second daughter arc could be routed through an intermediate node. Unlike the dead hub hack this needs REAL MATCHED DAUGHTERS, so it is a routing trick rather than fabricated signal. UNVERIFIED - test locally before relying on it."}
