{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1043, "key": "swp/91a0cafd: WHAT WAS PATCHED (2026-07-18): the exploit was a weakly-connected-component hole in the division Jaccard. Pre-", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A few of you might be aware that an exploit of our metrics was found, specifically in the division Jaccard score. We have made a patch and will re-score all submissions as soon as possible (likely Monday). Submissions that did not actively exploit this will not have their scores affected.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "WHAT WAS PATCHED (2026-07-18): the exploit was a weakly-connected-component hole in the division Jaccard. Pre-patch, a GT division counted as a TP whenever the matched predicted nodes merely shared ONE connected component that contained ANY predicted fork anywhere. The fix requires directed local topology instead."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1044, "key": "swp/aed33a95: The patch commit message names the hole exactly, and the patched metrics.md spells out the new requirement: sh", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "updating metric to patch weakly connected component exploit  ///  Directed local topology. The parent anchor must be the predicted fork or its immediate predecessor, and the daughter matches must lie downstream of the fork on their assigned branches.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The patch commit message names the hole exactly, and the patched metrics.md spells out the new requirement: sharing a weakly connected component is no longer sufficient."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1045, "key": "swp/d73c8e84: THE EXPLOIT WAS THE HUB NODE, AND IT IS DEAD. The pre-patch exploit was a public notebook (outwrest/metric-hac", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Adds one synthetic \"hub\" node at sentinel coordinates (t=-1000, z=y=x=-10000 — clearly outside any real acquisition volume/time range) connected to the roots of those trees. Appends a chain of 5 synthetic cell-division events (divider → child, continuation), also at sentinel coordinates", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE EXPLOIT WAS THE HUB NODE, AND IT IS DEAD. The pre-patch exploit was a public notebook (outwrest/metric-hack-minimal-baseline-tta-2gpu) that post-processed only the submitted CSV: it added one synthetic hub node at t=-1000, z=y=x=-10000 joined to the roots of up to 1400 largest trees, plus a chain of 5 synthetic division events at sentinel coordinates. It was reported to the organisers on 2026-07-17, the same day as the patch commit."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1046, "key": "swp/bb0480ed: DIRECTLY CONTRADICTS THE BRIEF I WAS GIVEN: under the PATCHED metric the hub-node trick LOSES score. Measured ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Same graphs, both versions: appending a hub node and 40 fake forks moved a one-to-one linker 0.9839 → 1.0639 under the old metric and 0.9839 → 0.9794 under the new one. ... that is the reason I wrote this up rather than the exploit, which is closed and now loses score.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "DIRECTLY CONTRADICTS THE BRIEF I WAS GIVEN: under the PATCHED metric the hub-node trick LOSES score. Measured on identical hand-built graphs, appending a hub node and 40 fake forks moved a one-to-one linker 0.9839 -> 1.0639 under the old metric but 0.9839 -> 0.9794 under the new one. Anyone claiming a current 0.950-0.966 comes from hub-node forks should re-check against the patched scorer."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1047, "key": "swp/78614064: WHY THE 0.950-0.966 NUMBERS STILL EXIST: public NOTEBOOK scores were never recalculated after the patch, so pr", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The metric bug was fixed and the leaderboard was recalculated, but some public notebooks still display their old, inflated scores from the pre-fix metric. Since \"Highest Score\" is the natural way to find strong baselines, these notebooks are still appearing above better solutions.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "WHY THE 0.950-0.966 NUMBERS STILL EXIST: public NOTEBOOK scores were never recalculated after the patch, so pre-fix inflated scores are still displayed and still sort to the top of 'Highest Score'. The competition leaderboard was rescored; the notebook rankings were not. A 0.950-0.966 public notebook is a stale pre-patch ghost, not a live score."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1048, "key": "swp/1f37c1be: The 0.950 cluster was openly understood at the time as the copied metric hack, and it predates the rescore.", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Why are there still so many people submitting public solutions to exploit the 0.950 rule vulnerability? ... its mainly a metric hack and its published on the public code and everyone copied it , theres a rescoring on monday so lets wait", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 0.950 cluster was openly understood at the time as the copied metric hack, and it predates the rescore."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1049, "key": "swp/3b645b62: THE REAL MECHANISM BEHIND A LIVE 0.954, AND THE SINGLE MOST EXPLOITABLE GAP I FOUND: the node-count adjustment", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "full      121,782    117,187     0.954\n  reduced    67,292     65,651     0.954\n\nThe second deletes 44.7% of the predicted nodes and 44.0% of the edges. The public score does not move to three decimals. ... the weighted factor goes from 1.0009 to 1.0500.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE REAL MECHANISM BEHIND A LIVE 0.954, AND THE SINGLE MOST EXPLOITABLE GAP I FOUND: the node-count adjustment is two-sided. Two submissions from one team, identical pipeline, differing only in a step that DELETES tracks: 121,782 nodes scored 0.954 and 67,292 nodes (-44.7%) also scored 0.954. At T_pred/T_true ~= -0.5 the multiplier is 1.05, and the weighted factor moved 1.0009 -> 1.0500."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1050, "key": "swp/53772687: The node-count bonus is officially sanctioned behaviour, not a bug the host intends to close: a competitor rep", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "About the unbounded score, the hosts are aware. They've commented in other posts ready, its the expected behavior  ///  Due to the nature of the metric, it is possible for scores to exceed 1.0.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The node-count bonus is officially sanctioned behaviour, not a bug the host intends to close: a competitor reports the hosts are aware and call it expected, and the Evaluation page itself says scores above 1.0 are possible."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1051, "key": "swp/ca6700ca: The formula has a floor but no ceiling: adjusted_jaccard = max(0, jaccard * (1 - 0.1*(T_pred - T_true)/T_true)", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The max(0.0, ...) puts a floor on it, but there's no corresponding ceiling. So when a submission predicts fewer nodes than estimated_number_of_nodes, the ratio goes negative and the multiplier ends up greater than 1.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The formula has a floor but no ceiling: adjusted_jaccard = max(0, jaccard * (1 - 0.1*(T_pred - T_true)/T_true)). T_pred below T_true multiplies the score UP, to a hard maximum of 1.1x as T_pred approaches zero. A worked example on train clip 44b6_0b24845f: edge Jaccard 0.98, node ratio -0.29, adjusted 1.0102."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1052, "key": "swp/cb58a0e7: T_true is NOT hidden - it is read from the GEFF metadata key estimated_number_of_nodes which ships with the da", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "with N_true read from estimated_number_of_nodes in the ground truth metadata. ... a faithful prediction has fewer nodes than the estimate, the ratio goes negative, and the factor comes out above 1.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "T_true is NOT hidden - it is read from the GEFF metadata key estimated_number_of_nodes which ships with the data, so a submission can read its own target node count at inference time and steer T_pred to the bonus side."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1053, "key": "swp/7028584e: The strongest public competitor states the node-count lever as strategy outright: aim for N_est or fewer nodes", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "yes. N_pred is a metric hack. From competition point of view: \"it is better to detect just enough annotated nodes (up to N_est or less)\" rather than all nodes\". This change the way on how you treated unlabelled data, apart from sparse labelled.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The strongest public competitor states the node-count lever as strategy outright: aim for N_est or fewer nodes, and treat that as changing how unlabelled data is handled."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1054, "key": "swp/bfead009: The same competitor plans a dedicated density head to hit the estimated node count, so the lever is known but ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "(4) maybe a head to predict node density so that i can predict kaggle estimate node count (metric hack)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The same competitor plans a dedicated density head to hit the estimated node count, so the lever is known but under-exploited."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1055, "key": "swp/f0439e19: COROLLARY THE FIELD IS GETTING BACKWARDS: over-detection is nearly free, so detection thresholds are probably ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Over-detection is almost free. A predicted node that matches no ground-truth node is never a false positive; the whole cost is the adjusted-Jaccard term. The line is exactly 1 - 0.1 x over-prediction, so 10% more nodes has to buy only a 1% relative gain in edge Jaccard to break even.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "COROLLARY THE FIELD IS GETTING BACKWARDS: over-detection is nearly free, so detection thresholds are probably too high across the field. The break-even line is exactly 1 - 0.1*over-prediction, so 10% more nodes needs only a 1% relative gain in edge Jaccard to pay for itself."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1056, "key": "swp/c3320886: But duplicate detections are pure cost: node matching is one-to-one bipartite, so a twin one voxel away matche", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Duplicating a detection costs ~9% and buys nothing. Node matching is a one-to-one bipartite assignment, so a twin one voxel away matches nothing: edge Jaccard did not move at all, only the node count. If you take the union of two models' detections without a merge pass, you pay this in full.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "But duplicate detections are pure cost: node matching is one-to-one bipartite, so a twin one voxel away matches nothing and moves edge Jaccard not at all while costing ~9% of the node budget. Naive union-ensembling of two detectors without a merge pass pays this in full."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1057, "key": "swp/12566c52: The patch quietly added three more submission-shaping rules to the EDGE metric that are not on the Evaluation ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Keep the two edges with the lowest EDGE_ID per source and drop the rest with a warning.  ///  [733877] divisions now require directed local topology rather than shared weak connectivity, edges spanning more than one frame are dropped, and merged edges are collapsed.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The patch quietly added three more submission-shaping rules to the EDGE metric that are not on the Evaluation page: edges not spanning exactly one timepoint are DROPPED (so gap-closing t+2 links contribute nothing), duplicate edges collapsing onto the same GT edge are deduplicated, and out-degree is capped at 2 by keeping the TWO LOWEST EDGE IDS - so CSV row order decides which two arcs of an over-linked node survive."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1058, "key": "swp/16cb7807: CODE-DERIVED, NOT FROM A THREAD: those three edge-metric rules are applied only inside _evaluate_matched_graph", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "edge_attrs = _evaluate_matched_graph(graph, gt_graph)\n ...\n    div = evaluate_divisions(\n        graph, gt_graph, scale=scale, max_distance=max_distance,\n    )", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CODE-DERIVED, NOT FROM A THREAD: those three edge-metric rules are applied only inside _evaluate_matched_graph, which returns a dataframe. evaluate() then passes the RAW, unfiltered graph to evaluate_divisions(). So the division term never sees the one-timepoint filter or the out-degree cap - a fork built from arbitrary-Dt edges is still a fork to the division metric. This is the surviving structural seam worth testing locally."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1059, "key": "swp/2cc7928d: CODE-DERIVED: the patched TP test accepts a daughter match at the GRANDCHILD level, and pred_lineages is built", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "pred_lineages = [{child, *pred_graph.successors(child)} for child in pred_graph.successors(pred_div)]", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CODE-DERIVED: the patched TP test accepts a daughter match at the GRANDCHILD level, and pred_lineages is built as {child} union successors(child). So a second daughter arc can be routed through an intermediate node rather than attached directly to the fork. Combined with the finding above, this is the one hub-node-adjacent variant the patch does not obviously close - UNLIKE the dead version it needs REAL matched daughters, so it is a routing trick, not fabricated signal. Test it against the patched scorer before trusting it."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1060, "key": "swp/da317e32: CODE-DERIVED: a predicted fork is only a division FALSE POSITIVE when local GT evidence exists - the fork itse", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "evaluable_forks = {\n        pred_id for pred_id in pred_forks\n        if pred_id in pred_to_gt and gt_graph.out_degree(pred_to_gt[pred_id]) >= 1\n    }", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CODE-DERIVED: a predicted fork is only a division FALSE POSITIVE when local GT evidence exists - the fork itself must match (within 7 um) a GT node with out-degree >= 1, or its child/grandchild branches must land in two different GT components, or its branches must be locally merged. With GT ~2.8% annotated, roughly 35 of every 36 forks are invisible to the metric, so a fork proposer needs only ~3% GLOBAL precision to break even."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1061, "key": "swp/4fa4018b: THE BINDING CONSTRAINT IS ABSOLUTE FORK COUNT, NOT PRECISION. Division FPs enter the denominator 1:1 against t", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "For me division_jaccard never rises above 0.0004, at any weight tested. Even at 0.5, where the ILP does find 1 real division (div_tp=1), it does so alongside 3260 false ones. div_j = tp/(tp+fp+fn) ≈ 1/3260 - swamped to near-zero by the FP volume.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE BINDING CONSTRAINT IS ABSOLUTE FORK COUNT, NOT PRECISION. Division FPs enter the denominator 1:1 against the ~151 GT divisions, so J_div = TP/(N_GT_div + FP). One competitor's ILP at division weight 0.5 found 1 real division alongside 3,260 false ones, giving div_j ~ 1/3260. Reaching div_j ~= 0.22 with ~40 TPs needs total FP ~= 31, i.e. a whole-corpus fork budget in the low thousands - not the ~100k an unconstrained ILP emits."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1062, "key": "swp/0db092d3: That same post states the consequence bluntly: weight-tuning ALONE cannot produce a real division contribution", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "For me division_jaccard never rises above 0.0004, at any weight tested. Even at 0.5, where the ILP does find 1 real division (div_tp=1), it does so alongside 3260 false ones. div_j = tp/(tp+fp+fn) ~ 1/3260 - swamped to near-zero by the FP volume.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "That same post states the consequence bluntly: weight-tuning ALONE cannot produce a real division contribution - it only chooses between suppressing everything and drowning a few real forks in thousands of fake ones. Division recovery has to come from a different signal, not from a knob on the existing ILP."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1063, "key": "swp/78f0e4ff: MEASURED division_jaccard #1: 0.22, obtained by a paired submission with the division step switched off. LB 0.", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "normal: LB 0.929\ndivisions off: LB 0.907\ndifference 0.022 = 0.1 × division_jaccard\nso my division_jaccard ≈ 0.22", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED division_jaccard #1: 0.22, obtained by a paired submission with the division step switched off. LB 0.929 normal vs 0.907 divisions-off, difference 0.022 = 0.1 * division_jaccard."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1064, "key": "swp/daa553ac: CAVEAT on that 0.22: the subtraction only isolates the division term if edge_jaccard stayed constant. Parent->", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "the subtraction only gives you the true division_jaccard if edge_jaccard stayed exactly constant between the \"normal\" and \"divisions off\" runs. But parent→daughter edges are part of the edge count too ... your 0.022 delta would be splitting between two effects", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CAVEAT on that 0.22: the subtraction only isolates the division term if edge_jaccard stayed constant. Parent->daughter edges also count as edges, so dropping a daughter link creates an edge FN too and the 0.022 delta splits across both terms - making 0.22 a bound rather than a value. The official repo's evaluate_datasets() returns both terms separately and settles it locally for free."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1065, "key": "swp/f7967c7b: The subtraction method is nevertheless sound in principle: a fork-free submission scores adj_edge_jaccard with", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "summarise() drops the division term entirely when a submission contains no divisions at all (score = edge_jaccard if not has_divisions). So a fork-free submission returns your pure adjusted edge Jaccard, and the division term follows by subtraction — one submission for an exact split.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The subtraction method is nevertheless sound in principle: a fork-free submission scores adj_edge_jaccard with the division term contributing exactly zero, so one paired submission does give the split."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1066, "key": "swp/2b184092: CORRECTION to that: the has_divisions branch fires only when div_tp+div_fp+div_fn == 0, and unrecovered GT div", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "has_divisions = (div_tp + div_fp + div_fn) > 0\n    division_jaccard = _jaccard(div_tp, div_fp, div_fn) if has_divisions else float(\"nan\")", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CORRECTION to that: the has_divisions branch fires only when div_tp+div_fp+div_fn == 0, and unrecovered GT divisions are counted as FN, so on any real split with divisions present the branch never fires. A fork-free submission gets division_jaccard = 0 rather than a dropped term. The arithmetic conclusion is identical; the stated mechanism is not."}
