{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1088, "key": "swp/7d31c037: The author discloses two limitations that matter for the division term specifically: the division rate is infl", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The division rate is deliberately inflated (4.07% of nodes vs ~0.26% in reality). A model starved of examples never learns mitosis. Re-weight your loss by the real rate if you need calibrated priors. ... Nucleus texture and contrast are the weakest axes.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The author discloses two limitations that matter for the division term specifically: the division rate is inflated ~16x (4.07% of nodes vs ~0.26% real), and nucleus texture and contrast are the weakest axes of distribution match. The value is the labels, not the photorealism."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1089, "key": "swp/e5780e0a: The generator reproduces the evaluator's exact XY downsample - a stride vol[:, ::4, ::4] rather than a block m", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The official pipeline downsamples XY by 4 with a stride (vol[:, ::4, ::4]), not a block mean. ... A classical DoG detector recovers ~0.89 of the synthetic nuclei in a moderately dense field, ~0.76 when crowded; on the real annotated nuclei it recovers 0.91–0.94.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The generator reproduces the evaluator's exact XY downsample - a stride vol[:, ::4, ::4] rather than a block mean - and reports DoG detectability of 0.89/0.76 on synthetic versus 0.91-0.94 on real annotated nuclei, i.e. calibrated slightly HARDER than the real data, which is the opposite of the usual synthetic-data failure mode."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1090, "key": "swp/d156adca: An unanswered objection to the synthetic set: the real train corpus is two embryos that differ enormously (ann", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "our real training set turns out to come from only 2 actual embryos, and they differ a lot  annotation sparsity of roughly 1% vs 9%, and division counts of 26 vs 125 across their samples", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "An unanswered objection to the synthetic set: the real train corpus is two embryos that differ enormously (annotation sparsity ~1% vs ~9%, division counts 26 vs 125), and nobody established whether the generator models that heterogeneity or draws from one idealised regime. The author did not reply."}
