{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1094, "key": "swp/54a9e173: SHAKEUP DRIVER #2, measured: movie-to-movie variability is enormous - plus/minus 0.14, 18% coefficient of vari", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Directionally, yes, but the public LB is more optimistic by almost 10%. That being said, movie-to-movie variability is large: ±0.14, with 18% coefficient of variation. Worst movie scores 0.460 and the best is at 0.984. The public split might have more of the easy movies compared to my hold out set.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "SHAKEUP DRIVER #2, measured: movie-to-movie variability is enormous - plus/minus 0.14, 18% coefficient of variation, worst movie 0.460 and best 0.984 - and the public LB runs ~10% more optimistic than held-out CV, consistent with the public split holding easier movies."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1095, "key": "swp/06d4d3fe: SHAKEUP DRIVER #3: most public baselines train on ALL available training data, so any CV built on top of them ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "most of the baseline notebooks use the model trained on all available training data, leading to leakage. My suggestion would be to use two separate models : one trained on only one embryo, the other used for validation", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "SHAKEUP DRIVER #3: most public baselines train on ALL available training data, so any CV built on top of them leaks. The suggested fix is a two-model protocol - one embryo for training and the other for validation, plus a both-embryo model for submission."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1096, "key": "swp/e9444b03: SHAKEUP DRIVER #4, the stated reason people expect one: the top of the leaderboard is separated by less than 0", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "I noticed the top of the leaderboard often has difference less than 0.001 points. In areas like division jaccard where theres only a few samples, it's really easy to overfit. I think this might cause a huge shakeup as even parameter tuning could cause 0.001 changes.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "SHAKEUP DRIVER #4, the stated reason people expect one: the top of the leaderboard is separated by less than 0.001, and division_jaccard rests on very few events, so ordinary parameter tuning moves teams by more than the gaps between them."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1097, "key": "swp/70bdd44b: SHAKEUP DRIVER #5: a very large share of the leaderboard is post-processing one identical set of public baseli", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Because a massive portion of the leaderboard is essentially post-processing the exact same set of baseline detections, doesn't this guarantee a massive Private LB shake-up?", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "SHAKEUP DRIVER #5: a very large share of the leaderboard is post-processing one identical set of public baseline detections, often by shelling out to a zipped GitHub repo or running on pre-computed .geff tracks rather than doing native inference - a monoculture that guarantees correlated private-LB movement."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1098, "key": "swp/8787cf73: The public field self-diagnoses as a monoculture: 0.92-0.933 teams report configs 'nearly identical knob for k", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "the public notebooks are all basically the same stack. learned unet then node transformer then ILP. i checked the configs on a few of the higher ones and theyre nearly identical to mine knob for knob. ... but the top 10 is 0.945 plus. thats way outside that cluster", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The public field self-diagnoses as a monoculture: 0.92-0.933 teams report configs 'nearly identical knob for knob' on the same unet -> node transformer -> ILP stack, with the top 10 at 0.945+ described as structurally different rather than better tuned."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1099, "key": "swp/f2ead692: A controlled ablation shows how little the conventional levers give: clean single-seed 0.908, every detection/", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Clean single-seed pipeline: 0.908\nDetection/ILP parameter probes around the best configuration: all 0.908\nTwo independent seeds, 50/50 detection-logit blend: 0.910\nEdge Top-K / feature-TTA branch: 0.885–0.886\n\nThe two-seed blend reduced emitted nodes by about 1.7% and improved the score", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A controlled ablation shows how little the conventional levers give: clean single-seed 0.908, every detection/ILP parameter probe also 0.908, a two-seed 50/50 logit blend 0.910, and an edge-TopK/feature-TTA branch actively worse at 0.885-0.886. Notably the two-seed blend helped by REDUCING emitted nodes ~1.7%, which is the node-count multiplier at work rather than better linking."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1100, "key": "swp/f3efe212: Detection is the highest-leverage layer, and a pure rule-based pipeline proved it: multi-scale DoG with scale-", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Detection was the biggest lever. Just taking DoG at multiple scales and using the scale-space max jumped LB from 0.786 → 0.826 (+0.040). In contrast, division edges hurt (0.784 → 0.778) and gap closing was roughly neutral.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Detection is the highest-leverage layer, and a pure rule-based pipeline proved it: multi-scale DoG with scale-space max took LB 0.786 -> 0.826 (+0.040) with no learning at all, reaching 7th/344 at the time. Gap closing was neutral and division edges HURT (0.784 -> 0.778)."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1101, "key": "swp/a5d481a1: CONSENSUS IS WRONG ABOUT WHY DIVISIONS HURT. The most-cited evidence that divisions are dangerous is that -0.0", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A predicted edge with a source node that matches a GT node that is connected to another target node. [is an FP]  ///  [716952] division edges hurt (0.784 → 0.778)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CONSENSUS IS WRONG ABOUT WHY DIVISIONS HURT. The most-cited evidence that divisions are dangerous is that -0.006 ablation. But division FPs only count in the ~2.8% annotated region, which cannot produce a loss that size. The cost is on the EDGE term: the second arc becomes an edge FP wherever its source matches an annotated GT node linked elsewhere, plus any added nodes pay the node-count penalty. Divisions should be added by RE-LINKING existing detections, never by adding nodes."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1102, "key": "swp/564aaceb: The field's own recommended ordering is detection -> linking -> division, with detector misses compounding: ed", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "detection -> linking -> division. detection should come first, once detection is solid, it's easier to improve others.  ///  Edge recall ≈ node recall² × conditional linking accuracy. This means detector misses are especially expensive.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The field's own recommended ordering is detection -> linking -> division, with detector misses compounding: edge recall is approximately node recall SQUARED times conditional linking accuracy, so a detector miss costs twice."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1103, "key": "swp/215c7346: Fine-tuning the public detector on a 7-film sparse split made things strictly WORSE - node recall and N_pred/N", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Fine-tuning the public detector on a 7-film sparse split made it worse, not better: node recall and N_pred/N_est fell on every film and adj_J dropped 0.0161, while a dense-label branch collapsed to zero nodes on one film.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Fine-tuning the public detector on a 7-film sparse split made things strictly WORSE - node recall and N_pred/N_est fell on every film, adj_edge_jaccard dropped 0.0161, and a dense-label branch collapsed to zero nodes on one film. More epochs on sparse annotations is not the lever."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1104, "key": "swp/1bbf3ef1: GROUND TRUTH NOISE #1: consecutive volumes are sometimes byte-identical (subtracting t and t+1 gives exactly z", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "1) some frames are frozen. i.e subraction of vol at t and t+1 gives exactly zero\n2) there are suddent jumps in time (global movement of whole volume)\n\ni.e. the time step are not constant, maybe due to limitations of acquisition equipment", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "GROUND TRUTH NOISE #1: consecutive volumes are sometimes byte-identical (subtracting t and t+1 gives exactly zero) and there are sudden global jumps of the whole volume, so the effective time step is not constant."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1105, "key": "swp/c6f1ca79: GROUND TRUTH NOISE #2, with a reproducible case that directly threatens the 7 um matching radius: in 6bba_fc51", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "np.array_equal(volume[81], volume[82]) is True ... With voxel scale (1.625, 0.40625, 0.40625) um, this is an 8.90 um displacement. ... Since the matching radius is 7 um, copying a detection across the duplicated frame may fail to match the second GT node.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 77, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "sweep-forum", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "GROUND TRUTH NOISE #2, with a reproducible case that directly threatens the 7 um matching radius: in 6bba_fc516dc6 frames t=81 and t=82 are byte-identical yet the GT edge moves 8.90 um. Copying a detection across a duplicated frame can therefore FAIL to match the second GT node even when the image says nothing changed."}
