{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1151, "key": "code/efebb687: Fork count alone does not set the exploit's gain: kaiwalyaatulraut's 0.965 and outwrest's 0.950 inject the ide", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "HACK     130301  128239     64     64  -1000     24   1401  kaiwalyaatulraut_biohub-competition-solution\nHACK     124807  119991     64     64  -1000     24   1401  outwrest_metric-hack-minimal-baseline-tta-2gpu", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Fork count alone does not set the exploit's gain: kaiwalyaatulraut's 0.965 and outwrest's 0.950 inject the identical 64 nodes and 24 forks, so the 0.015 between them comes from the underlying honest pipeline, not the hack."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1152, "key": "code/c90aed4d: The official 0.143 starter fails for a specific measurable reason: it thresholds at the 90th intensity percent", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "DOWNSAMPLE = 4\nPERCENTILE = 90\nMAX_LINK_DISTANCE = 15.0  # um\n...\n        ds = vol[::DOWNSAMPLE, ::DOWNSAMPLE, ::DOWNSAMPLE]\n\n        smoothed = uniform_filter(ds.astype(np.float32), size=3)\n        threshold = np.percentile(smoothed, PERCENTILE)\n        binary = smoothed > threshold", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The official 0.143 starter fails for a specific measurable reason: it thresholds at the 90th intensity percentile and labels connected components, flooding the graph with detections, and links at a 15 um gate that is roughly four times the observed p95 cell displacement."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1153, "key": "code/7af2995c: The starter also subsamples Z by the same factor as XY despite 4x anisotropic voxels, destroying the isotropic", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "ds = vol[::DOWNSAMPLE, ::DOWNSAMPLE, ::DOWNSAMPLE]\n...\n| voxels anisotropic (Z 4x X/Y) | XY-pool x4 -> isotropic grid; **all distances in um** |", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The starter also subsamples Z by the same factor as XY despite 4x anisotropic voxels, destroying the isotropic geometry that every successful pipeline restores by XY-pooling only."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1154, "key": "code/990dc718: The classical tier's governing insight is that edge recall scales as the SQUARE of node recall because an edge", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "| edge recall ~ node recall^2 | prioritise *reliable* detections, then link robustly |\n| cells move only **p95 ~ 4 um/frame** | tight link gate `MAX_LINK_DIST~8 um` (benchmark used 15 - too loose) |\n| neighbours are **>=12 um apart** | NMS radius `~4 um` is safe (won't merge real cells) |", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The classical tier's governing insight is that edge recall scales as the SQUARE of node recall because an edge TP needs both endpoints matched, which is why every serious pipeline trades recall for detection reliability."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1155, "key": "code/d16fd73d: PROMISING AND ABSENT FROM THE TOP TIER: per-sample node-count calibration. The adjusted edge Jaccard penalises", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "**Per-sample count calibration.** The metric penalises over-predicting the *total* number of nodes, and the true cell count differs a lot between embryos (a few cells/frame for some, ~12 for others). A single global threshold therefore over-shoots on sparse embryos and under-shoots on dense ones.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "PROMISING AND ABSENT FROM THE TOP TIER: per-sample node-count calibration. The adjusted edge Jaccard penalises (N_pred - n_total)/n_total, the test set ships no estimated_number_of_nodes, and a 0.728 notebook learns an image-statistic to cell-count regressor per movie - while the whole 0.940-0.947 family uses one global detection threshold of 0.965 for every embryo."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1156, "key": "code/6b9601df: PROMISING AND ABSENT FROM THE TOP TIER: Difference-of-Gaussians band-pass detection to defeat the depth/illumi", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "**Difference-of-Gaussians (DoG) band-pass detection.** A single global threshold misses dim nuclei under a depth/illumination gradient (deep cells sit near the background median). DoG subtracts the slowly-varying background so each peak is judged **locally** - on a synthetic gradient movie this lifted recall 0.38 -> 0.79 and **deep-cell recall 0.00 -> 0.88**.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "PROMISING AND ABSENT FROM THE TOP TIER: Difference-of-Gaussians band-pass detection to defeat the depth/illumination gradient, reported to lift deep-cell recall from 0.00 to 0.88 on a synthetic gradient movie, whereas the support-pack detector only percentile-normalises."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1157, "key": "code/74275a43: The two-pass tight-then-relaxed Hungarian relink that the 0.947 family uses at 6.0/10.0 um was independently d", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "**Two-pass um-gated Hungarian linking.** Pass 1 commits only **confident** short links (tight ~6 um gate on a half-velocity-predicted position), which prevents a fast distractor from *stealing* a match; pass 2 re-links the few leftovers at the full ~10 um gate.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The two-pass tight-then-relaxed Hungarian relink that the 0.947 family uses at 6.0/10.0 um was independently derived in the CPU classical tier at the same gates, with the stated rationale that pass 1 prevents a fast distractor from stealing a match."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1158, "key": "code/63b0ee0e: PROMISING AND ABSENT FROM THE TOP TIER: Trackastra, a pretrained graph transformer that won the ISBI 2024 Cell", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "**Tracking** - [Trackastra](https://github.com/weigertlab/trackastra) (ECCV 2024),\nwinner of the ISBI 2024 Cell Tracking Challenge. A graph transformer that predicts\nassociation weights between all candidate cell pairs in a sliding temporal window and\nsolves the assignment globally - no hand-tuned distance gates, divisions handled natively.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "PROMISING AND ABSENT FROM THE TOP TIER: Trackastra, a pretrained graph transformer that won the ISBI 2024 Cell Tracking Challenge and handles divisions natively, is used by exactly one public notebook (0.616) where it is bottlenecked by classical DoG detection - never paired with the support-pack learned detector."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1159, "key": "code/010fb2a3: The 0.616 author names the exact structural reason the 0.947 family's division_jaccard is stuck at 0.23: frame", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "**Why not Hungarian/ILP baseline?** Frame-pair Hungarian matching requires a hand-tuned\nmax-distance gate and cannot handle divisions. Trackastra learns both from CTC data.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 0.616 author names the exact structural reason the 0.947 family's division_jaccard is stuck at 0.23: frame-pair Hungarian matching cannot handle divisions at all, and that is the final association stage the top tier uses."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1160, "key": "code/248f9bf0: The 0.912 to 0.940 step (+0.028, the largest single jump below the exploit) is one env-var edit: turn on bidir", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "ADDED at 0.940: ['BIDIRECTIONAL_EDGE_WEIGHT', 'BIDIRECTIONAL_FUSION_MODE', 'ILP_DIVISION_WEIGHT', 'SAFE_DIV_DIVERGE_UM', 'SAFE_DIV_SISTER_SYMMETRY_TAU', 'SHORT_TRACK_RESCUE_*']\nCHANGED: {'SAFE_DIV_MAX_UM': ('4.66', '9.0'), 'SAFE_DIV_SISTER_MAX_UM': ('8.5', '14.0'), 'ADAPTIVE_SHORT_TRACK_RESCUE': ('0', '1'), 'DET_THRESHOLD': ('0.96875', '0.965')}", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 0.912 to 0.940 step (+0.028, the largest single jump below the exploit) is one env-var edit: turn on bidirectional harmonic fusion, enable adaptive short-track rescue, widen the safe-division gates from 4.66/8.5 to 9.0/14.0 um, enable the DeepCenter safe-division veto, enable gap2 recovery, and loosen detection 0.96875 to 0.965."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1161, "key": "code/406af97e: The DeepCenter center-prior veto is deliberately run from a 2-epoch checkpoint in the 0.940+ tier, having been", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "CHANGED: {'DEEPCENTER_EXPECTED_EPOCH': ('500', '2'), 'DEEPCENTER_CHECKPOINT': ('/kaggle/input/biohub-deepcenter-unet3d-center-prior-v1/weights/full_frame_center/checkpoint_last.pt', 'str(')}", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The DeepCenter center-prior veto is deliberately run from a 2-epoch checkpoint in the 0.940+ tier, having been switched away from the 500-epoch checkpoint_last that the 0.912 configuration used - the barely-trained prior makes a better veto than the converged one."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1162, "key": "code/421c6aeb: CANONICAL FAILURE MODE: adding sophistication on top of an over-detecting detector makes the score WORSE. The ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "DOWNSAMPLE = 4          # spatial downsample factor applied before segmentation\nPERCENTILE = 90         # fallback threshold percentile if Otsu misbehaves\nMAX_LINK_DISTANCE = 15.0     # micrometers, hard gate for a normal frame-to-frame link\nDIVISION_SEARCH_RADIUS = 18.0  # micrometers, gate for a *second* (daughter) edge from a parent", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CANONICAL FAILURE MODE: adding sophistication on top of an over-detecting detector makes the score WORSE. The 0.131 notebook adds watershed splitting, feature-gated Hungarian, divisions and gap closing to the 0.143 starter but keeps PERCENTILE=90, so every added mechanism multiplies an already-flooded node set and it scores below the naive baseline it upgrades."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1163, "key": "code/84ad97fa: FAILURE MODE: division gates in the sub-0.90 tier are set at 18-20 um, four to five times the observed p95 cel", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "DIVISION_SEARCH_RADIUS = 18.0  # micrometers, gate for a *second* (daughter) edge from a parent\nGAP_MAX_DISTANCE = 20.0      # micrometers, relaxed gate for gap-closing (t -> t+2) links\n...\n| cells move only **p95 ~ 4 um/frame** | tight link gate `MAX_LINK_DIST~8 um`", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FAILURE MODE: division gates in the sub-0.90 tier are set at 18-20 um, four to five times the observed p95 cell displacement of about 4 um per frame, so every 'division' found is a distractor pairing and the division term goes negative on net."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1164, "key": "code/efe9a51b: The bottom of the leaderboard is largely verbatim forks of the official starter: a 0.143 notebook reproduces P", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "DOWNSAMPLE\n= 4\nPERCENTILE\n= 90\nMAX_LINK_DISTANCE\n= 15.0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The bottom of the leaderboard is largely verbatim forks of the official starter: a 0.143 notebook reproduces PERCENTILE=90, DOWNSAMPLE=4 and MAX_LINK_DISTANCE=15.0 unchanged, matching the starter's score exactly."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1165, "key": "code/bad4c098: MEASURED EXPLOIT VALUE: the hack's own author documents +0.0815 on a local replica of the host metric (0.8388 ", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "# OPTIONAL FINAL CELL - division-term augmentation (metric hack)\n# Append as the LAST cell of the M001 kernel, AFTER submission.csv is written.\n# Validated offline on host_metric replica: VAL 0.8388 -> 0.9203 (+0.0815).\n# This is a METRIC EXPLOIT (fake out-of-volume / negative-time hub + forks that\n# game division_jaccard). Public score; does NOT commit to final-2. Patchable.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED EXPLOIT VALUE: the hack's own author documents +0.0815 on a local replica of the host metric (0.8388 to 0.9203), i.e. it moves division_jaccard by roughly 0.82 - so the exploit captures almost the entire 0.1 division term and is worth four times the whole 0.912-to-0.947 honest climb."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1166, "key": "code/8ed5a62f: The canonical exploit recipe is exactly two constants - MAX_COMPONENTS=1400 hub-to-root edges and FORKS=5 chai", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "MAX_COMPONENTS, FORKS = 1400, 5\n...\n# idempotency guard: if already augmented (any negative-time node), do nothing\nif ((_S.row_type == \"node\") & (_S.t < 0)).any():\n    print(\"already augmented - skipping\")", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The canonical exploit recipe is exactly two constants - MAX_COMPONENTS=1400 hub-to-root edges and FORKS=5 chained synthetic forks per dataset - plus an idempotency guard keyed on any negative-time node, which is why three independent authors' outputs all show precisely 24 forks and a 1401 hub out-degree."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1167, "key": "code/934c8ca5: The synthetic forks are chained rather than parallel - each fork's continuation node becomes the next fork's p", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "        for i in range(FORKS):\n            d, c, co = range(nxt, nxt + 3); nxt += 3; tt = -999 + 2 * i\n            new += [_row(ds, \"node\", d, tt, -10000, -10000, -10000),\n                    _row(ds, \"node\", c, tt + 1, -10000, -10000, -10000),\n                    _row(ds, \"node\", co, tt + 1, -10001, -10000, -10000),\n                    _row(ds, \"edge\", s=prev, tg=d), _row(ds, \"edge\", s=d, tg=c),\n", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The synthetic forks are chained rather than parallel - each fork's continuation node becomes the next fork's parent, at t = -999 + 2i - so a single hub plus five forks yields a connected chain that the division scorer's weakly-connected-component test cannot separate from the real graph."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1168, "key": "code/d9e476f7: Hack authors themselves treat the exploit as public-leaderboard-only and expect it to be patched, explicitly s", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "# This is a METRIC EXPLOIT (fake out-of-volume / negative-time hub + forks that\n# game division_jaccard). Public score; does NOT commit to final-2. Patchable.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Hack authors themselves treat the exploit as public-leaderboard-only and expect it to be patched, explicitly stating it does not commit to their final two submissions - so the >=0.95 band should be read as a signalling exercise, not as a private-LB threat."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1169, "key": "code/4fc0bbe4: UPDATED EXPLOIT CENSUS over 43 of the 203 scored notebooks: 11 are contaminated - all 10 of the 10 notebooks a", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "tier           total  examined  hacked\nA >=0.95          10        10      10\nB 0.94-0.95       34        13       0\nC 0.90-0.94       79         2       0\nD 0.80-0.90       65         8       1\nE <0.80           15        10       0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "UPDATED EXPLOIT CENSUS over 43 of the 203 scored notebooks: 11 are contaminated - all 10 of the 10 notebooks at >=0.95 (a complete census of that tier), plus one 0.884 notebook that carries the same copy-paste cell; 0 of 13 examined in 0.94-0.95 and 0 of 10 examined below 0.80."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1170, "key": "code/d45a4291: SHARPEST DIAGNOSIS IN THE PUBLIC SET: with the support pack's default division_weight=1.0 and edge_weight=-1.0", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "**Divisions nearly absent.** Only 1-6 division events surface across a full 100-frame video of early zebrafish development - a stage where cells divide constantly. Looking at the ILP objective: with `division_weight=1.0` and `edge_weight=-1.0`, a split (one parent -> two daughters, two edge rewards) accrues the same net cost as a plain continuation.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "SHARPEST DIAGNOSIS IN THE PUBLIC SET: with the support pack's default division_weight=1.0 and edge_weight=-1.0 a split earns exactly the same net ILP objective as a plain continuation, so the solver commits to a division only when the edge classifier is simultaneously confident about BOTH daughter links - which is why only 1-6 divisions surface per 100-frame movie."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1171, "key": "code/dacac2fc: That diagnosis directly indicts the top tier's setting: the 0.940-0.947 family raises ILP_DIVISION_WEIGHT to 1", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "CV_DET_THRESHOLDS = [0.99, 0.95, 0.90]\nCV_DIV_WEIGHTS    = [1.0,  0.5]\n...\nDIVISION_WEIGHT\"] = \"0.9\"\n...\nos.environ[\"BIOHUB_ILP_DIVISION_WEIGHT\"] = \"1.2\"", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "That diagnosis directly indicts the top tier's setting: the 0.940-0.947 family raises ILP_DIVISION_WEIGHT to 1.2, making a split strictly MORE expensive than a continuation, while the 0.810 and 0.898 notebooks are the only ones that lower it (to 0.5 and 0.9) to buy divisions."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1172, "key": "code/2291de97: The 0.810 notebook's own CV harness is mis-calibrated by a bug: its node-matching gate uses np.sqrt(diff**2).s", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "D = np.sqrt(\n            ((p_t[[\"z\",\"y\",\"x\"]].values * SCALE_UM)[:, None] -\n             (g_t[[\"z\",\"y\",\"x\"]].values * SCALE_UM)[None]) ** 2\n        ).sum(2)\n        cost = np.where(D <= GATE_UM, D, 1e9)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 78, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "code-scored", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 0.810 notebook's own CV harness is mis-calibrated by a bug: its node-matching gate uses np.sqrt(diff**2).sum(axis=2), which is the Manhattan distance, not the Euclidean distance the official scorer uses, so its 7 um gate is systematically too permissive and its selected hyperparameters are chosen against the wrong metric."}
