{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1195, "key": "exp1_gate_admissibility_measured", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "parent<=9.0 sister<=14.0  131/151  86.8% | parent<=12 sister<=18  150/151  99.3%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 82, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-1 gate admissibility over all 151 GT divisions: parent<=4.7 sister<=7.2 (the OLD public gates) admits only 24/151 = 15.9%. parent<=9.0 sister<=14.0 (the CURRENT public gates) admits 131/151 = 86.8%. parent<=10 sister<=15 admits 140/151 = 92.7%. parent<=12 sister<=18 admits 150/151 = 99.3%. So the current gates are broadly right, but 20 divisions (13.2%) remain structurally unreachable, and widening to 12/18 recovers 19 of them."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1196, "key": "exp5_THESIS_PROVEN_1to1_linking_is_the_cause", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "A: divJ 0.0000 tp/fp/fn 0/0/151 | B: divJ 0.3727 tp/fp/fn 142/230/9 | adj_edge delta -0.0002", "review_after": null, "source_id": 83, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-5 PROVES THE CENTRAL THESIS, over all 199 datasets with ORACLE DETECTION (GT node positions fed in as perfect detections, so any shortfall is attributable entirely to the linker). Arm A, strict 1:1 Hungarian: adj_edge 1.0862, division_jaccard 0.0000, tp/fp/fn 0/0/151 - it misses ALL 151 divisions BY CONSTRUCTION. Arm B, identical nodes and gates but a parent may take a second child within 12um/18um: adj_edge 1.0859, division_jaccard 0.3727, tp/fp/fn 142/230/9. Allowing forks recovers 142 of 151 divisions - 94% recall - at an edge-term cost of 0.0002, i.e. nothing. Score delta +0.0370."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1197, "key": "exp5_the_next_lever_is_fork_PRECISION", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "tp/fp/fn 142/230/9 -> J = 142/381 = 0.3727", "review_after": null, "source_id": 83, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-5 relocates the lever. With oracle detection a forking linker already achieves 94% division RECALL (142/151), so proposal is solved once forking is permitted at all. The remaining cost is PRECISION: 230 false positives against 142 true, giving J = 142/381 = 0.3727. Halving FP to ~115 would give J = 0.55; cutting to 50 would give 0.707. The acceptance rule, not the proposal rule, is now the binding constraint - and EXP-1 supplies the discriminators: cos(angle) median -0.746, and sisters diverging 10.570um at birth to 13.656um by t+2."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1198, "key": "exp5_oracle_ceiling_clears_the_north_star", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "division_jaccard 0.3727 vs the 0.335 required for 0.980", "review_after": null, "source_id": 83, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The oracle divJ of 0.3727 CLEARS the 0.335 the recomputed north star requires for 0.980. That is with perfect detection, so it is a ceiling not a forecast - real detection will erode both terms. But it establishes that the division target is reachable in principle by a linker change alone, with no new detector and no external data."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1199, "key": "vault_DUAL_HEAD_refinement_architecture", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "node_logit = self.node_head(x) / refinement = self.refine_head(x) ... think of refine_logit as a ranker", "review_after": null, "source_id": 84, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE ARCHITECTURE WE MISSED. hengck23 runs a UNet trained on FOCUS-3D dense cell centres (99.5% Kaggle-annotation recall) with TWO heads: node_logit = node_head(x) supervised with DENSE Focus3D, and refinement = refine_head(x) supervised with SPARSE Kaggle CONDITIONAL ON DETECTED PEAKS. refine_node_peak() samples the refinement field at each detected coord and returns refine_coord = coord + dzyx, refine_logit = logit + dlogit. He describes refine_logit explicitly as A RANKER. Measured over epochs: centroid error falls 1.18 -> 0.90-0.97 voxels (mean reduction 18-24%), 70-75% of points improve, 10-14% of peaks are pruned, and Kaggle recall holds at 99.45-99.68%. This directly attacks the centroid-error cliff (sigma~2um costs 16%, 3um costs 41%) that no public pipeline addresses."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1200, "key": "vault_edge_ranking_headroom_measured", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "e2e_c99 0.966268  e2e_top1 0.941940  e2e_50 0.938384 (averages over 11 samples)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 84, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED EDGE HEADROOM nobody in our base had. Over 11 validation samples: accumulating all candidate edges to a prob<=0.99 cutoff gives e2e_c99 = 0.966 (the upper bound for an ILP), while greedy top-1 selection gives e2e_top1 = 0.942 and top-1-discard-below-0.5 gives 0.938. So +0.024 of EDGE RECALL is available purely from better RANKING within the existing candidate set - no better detector required. His stated strategy: push edge recall from 0.966 to 0.990 so a 99% cutoff is safe, then add a refine module to re-rank candidates with heavier computation."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1201, "key": "vault_gap_statistics_bounded", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "most gap are only 1 to 2 frames. Upper bound for post processing", "review_after": null, "source_id": 84, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "GAP STATISTICS, measured per sample: num_1gap ~20-26, num_2gap ~3-4, num_3gap_more ~1-2. 'most gap are only 1 to 2 frames. Upper bound for post processing.' Combined with our EXP finding that all GT edges span exactly one frame, gap recovery means interpolating ONE missing node in the overwhelming majority of cases - a bounded, tractable problem."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1202, "key": "vault_nodes_must_MOVE_during_linking", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "during linking, my zxy must change, the node must shift to better position. So actually you cannot detect and fixed a location.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 84, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "ARCHITECTURAL INSIGHT: 'quite a number of FPs are actually very close to the truth node... during linking, my zxy must change, the node must shift to better position. So actually you cannot detect and fixed a location.' Detection and localisation are not separable here - the node position should be refined DURING linking, not frozen at detection. This is the same conclusion the refinement head reaches from the other direction."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1203, "key": "vault_augmentation_is_HARMFUL", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "Peak removal improves from roughly 8% to 10-14% typically ... Training time falls from approximately 6.7 to 3.5 minutes per epoch", "review_after": null, "source_id": 84, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "MEASURED NEGATIVE on augmentation: removing it improves nearly every practical validation quantity. Peak removal 8% -> 10-14%; mean-distance reduction 18% -> 22-24%; Kaggle recall unchanged at 99.5-99.7%; and training time falls from 6.7 to 3.5 MINUTES per epoch. Diagnosis: 'your augmentation was too difficult or outside the real validation distribution.' Also independently confirms that multi-hour epochs are a hardware artefact, not the job size."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1204, "key": "vault_RANK1_says_synthetic_data_does_not_work", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "I've tried the synthetic volume idea for ~2 weeks with no clear improvement in my scores, so I dropped it.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 84, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "DECISIVE ON SYNTHETIC DATA, from Sergio Alvarez, 1st in this competition: 'I've tried the synthetic volume idea for ~2 weeks with no clear improvement in my scores, so I dropped it.' He notes it helped a little in CZII (via Polnet) and suggests the DaXi paper as a reference, but two weeks of the current leader's effort produced nothing here. Together with the synthetic dataset author's own sub-baseline fusions, this closes the synthetic-data lane."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1205, "key": "vault_train_on_predicted_dense_points", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "train = t0: vol + predicted dense point from new unet, t1: augmented vol + predicted dense point from new unet", "review_after": null, "source_id": 84, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "hengck23's newer end-to-end recipe: train the node+edge predictor on t0 = volume + PREDICTED dense points from the new UNet, t1 = augmented volume + predicted dense points, with edge links copied from the previous graph by linear assignment matching old points to new. This trains the linker on the SAME candidate distribution it will see at inference - directly fixing the train/inference mismatch we found (training picks peaks at raw logit 0.3, inference at sigmoid 0.99)."}
{"claim_type": "decision", "confidence": "high", "id": 1206, "key": "stage_contract_nodes_are_MUTABLE", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "during linking, my zxy must change, the node must shift to better position", "review_after": null, "source_id": 84, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "ARCHITECTURE CHANGE. The four-stage contract must NOT freeze node positions after detect. Many false positives sit very close to a true node and are recoverable by moving them, so the graph carries MUTABLE coordinates and the linker may refine them. This contradicts the detect->link->divide->write contract as originally drafted."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1207, "key": "exp3_centroid_cliff_CONFIRMED", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "sigma 2.0um -4.9% | 2.5um -20.6% | 3.0um -41.9% | 4.0um -73.5%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 85, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-3 CONFIRMS the centroid cliff on our own corpus, independently and almost exactly. Gaussian jitter on oracle node positions, edge-Jaccard loss against baseline 0.9944: sigma 0.5um -0.1%, 1.0um -0.3%, 2.0um -4.9%, 2.5um -20.6%, 3.0um -41.9%, 4.0um -73.5%. The public claim was -16% / -41% / -74% at 2.5/3/4um; 3um and 4um match to within a point. THE CLIFF IS BETWEEN 2.0 AND 2.5 MICROMETRES - loss quadruples across that 0.5um step. Localisation accuracy is the single most valuable detector property, and it is why the refinement head (18-24% error reduction) matters so much."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1208, "key": "exp2_duplicates_REFUTES_the_halving_claim", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "dup 2% @0.4um -2.8% | 5% -7.0% | 20% -23.0% | 100% -9.6%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 85, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-2 REFUTES the widely-repeated claim that a single duplicate detection 0.4um away halves the edge Jaccard (1.000 -> 0.500). Measured: duplicating 2% of nodes at 0.4um costs -2.8%, 5% costs -7.0%, 20% costs -23.0%. The damage is roughly PROPORTIONAL to the duplicated fraction, not catastrophic. Duplicates at 0.4um hurt about twice as much as at 2.0um, as expected since closer duplicates contend for the same match. NON-MONOTONIC and worth knowing: duplicating 100% of nodes costs only -9.6%, far LESS than duplicating 20% (-23.0%), because uniform duplication lets the bipartite matching pair consistently while partial duplication creates asymmetric contention."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1209, "key": "exp4_submission_gate_built", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "FATAL 44b6_0113de3b: 50 NON-CONSECUTIVE edges (dt != 1) ... can silently score 0.0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 86, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-4 built and verified. tools/validate_submission.py is STDLIB-ONLY - no polars, torch or tracksdata - so it runs inside the internet-disabled Kaggle notebook that produces the file, which is the only place it can prevent the failure. FATAL checks: non-consecutive edges (the silent 0.0), edgeless graph (evaluate() returns early and raises), dangling edge endpoints, negative timepoints and out-of-volume coordinates (the patched hub-exploit signature), duplicate node_ids, missing datasets, bad schema. WARN: out-degree >2 (the scorer keeps at most 2 children) and in-degree >1 merges. Verified against three synthesised failures - stride-subsampled frames, an injected t=-1000 hub, and a nodes-only file - all correctly FATAL; both real submissions PASS."}
{"claim_type": "decision", "confidence": "high", "id": 1210, "key": "architecture_delta_ledger", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "resolve forks +1/0 | repair forks +0/-1", "review_after": null, "source_id": 63, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Every stage returns StageDelta(nodes_added/removed/moved, edges_added/removed, forks_created/destroyed, wall_s), printed as a table each run. A run showing 'resolve forks_created +187' beside 'repair forks_destroyed -184' exposes the entire public field's bug at a glance. Without it the only observable is the final score, which is exactly how a frozen division term went unnoticed by ~400 teams and how our own p15 ablation was misread as +0.019 when it was -0.0041."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1211, "key": "exp6_discriminators_earn_their_place", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "fork no discriminators divJ 0.5385 prec 0.700 | fork + EXP-1 discriminators divJ 0.5652 prec 0.812", "review_after": null, "source_id": 87, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-6 validates src/biohub end-to-end on real ground truth (30 datasets, 20 GT divisions). 1:1 no-fork: divJ 0.0000, tp/fp/fn 0/0/20 - the structural miss reproduces. Fork permitted without discriminators: divJ 0.5385, 14/6/6, precision 0.700. Fork plus the EXP-1 discriminators (cos(angle) <= -0.30 and divergence persistence): divJ 0.5652, 13/3/7, precision 0.812. So the discriminators halve false positives (6 -> 3) for the cost of one true positive, lifting precision from 0.700 to 0.812 and divJ by +0.027. adj_edge is unchanged across all three arms (1.0709 / 1.0715 / 1.0717), confirming again that forking is free on the edge term. CAVEAT: only 20 GT divisions in this subset against 151 corpus-wide, so the absolute divJ is not comparable to EXP-5's 0.3727 over all 199 - the RELATIVE precision gain is the finding."}
{"claim_type": "decision", "confidence": "high", "id": 1212, "key": "repair_stage_guards_forks_at_runtime", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "guard fires: evil_stage destroyed 1 fork(s) (1 -> 0) | edge dt values: [1]", "review_after": null, "source_id": 88, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "src/biohub/repair.py enforces the constraint the public field violates: assert_forks_preserved() raises if any repair step reduces the fork count, so 'repair may not rebuild the edge list' is a runtime guarantee rather than a comment. This is the exact bug that costs the field its division term - a motion-relink rebuilding edges from a 1:1 assignment AFTER safe_div, turning 111 inserted divisions into 3 TP. Two further guards: gap closing INTERPOLATES a node and emits only dt=1 edges (verified - a 2-frame hole produced one node at t=3 and all edge dt values are exactly 1), and prune_short carries a DIVISION EXEMPTION because 112 of 199 datasets contain no division at all, making a blanket length filter a cheap way to delete the rare thing being scored."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1213, "key": "pipeline_runs_end_to_end", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "wrote 2,950 rows | 1,488 nodes 1,462 edges 1 forks | validator PASS", "review_after": null, "source_id": 89, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The four CPU stages now run end to end on real ground truth: score_edges -> resolve -> repair -> submit, with the stage ledger printing and the independent validator passing on the produced CSV. submit.py RAISES rather than writing a graph that would score wrong - verified against all three real failure modes: stride-subsampled frames (dt!=1), an edgeless graph, and a t=-1000 hub. Coordinates are emitted as integer voxel indices because the GT is int64 and there is no sub-voxel signal the metric can read. Only detect and refine remain, and both need the Mac and FOCUS-3D pseudo-labels."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1214, "key": "pipeline_six_stages_complete", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "adj_edge 1.0479  divJ 0.5625 tp/fp/fn 9/1/6 | validator PASS", "review_after": null, "source_id": 90, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "All six stages exist and run end to end: detect (oracle substitute) -> refine (pass-through until trained) -> score_edges -> resolve -> repair -> submit. On 10 6bba datasets with oracle detection: adj_edge 1.0479, divJ 0.5625, tp/fp/fn 9/1/6, precision 0.90, and the stdlib validator passes the produced CSV. detect and refine are documented stubs carrying the full target architecture and its build rules; everything downstream is already validated against oracle detections."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1215, "key": "ledger_fork_counts_only_after_resolve", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "resolve edges +0/-39 forks +0/-38  ->  after fix: forks +0/0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 90, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "method", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "LEDGER FIX found by running it. score_edges emits a CANDIDATE set, where a node having two possible children is not a fork. Counting forks there made the ledger report 'resolve destroyed 38 forks' at the score_edges->resolve seam - a false alarm on the exact signal the ledger exists to catch. Graph.forks() now returns -1 for any graph still carrying edge_prob (i.e. unresolved), and StageDelta suppresses fork deltas when either side is a candidate set. Fork counts are only meaningful after resolve."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1216, "key": "pipeline_full_corpus_baseline (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-11 by EXP-7 leave-one-embryo-out sweep)", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "ALL n=199 adj_edge 1.0529 edgeJ 0.9644 divJ 0.3908 tp/fp/fn 68/23/83 prec 0.747", "review_after": null, "source_id": 91, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1222, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FULL PIPELINE over all 199 datasets, oracle detection, 60 seconds: adj_edge 1.0529, edge_jaccard 0.9644, divJ 0.3908, tp/fp/fn 68/23/83, precision 0.747. The stage ledger confirms the fork guard holds at scale - repair removed 1,812 short-track nodes and destroyed ZERO forks. This is the linker half of the system measured end to end against ground truth; the detector half is still oracle."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1217, "key": "discriminators_OVERTIGHTENED_constraint_reversed (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-11 by EXP-7 leave-one-embryo-out sweep)", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "142/230/9 (divJ 0.3727) vs 68/23/83 (divJ 0.3908)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 91, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1223, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE ACCEPTANCE RULE IS NOW TOO STRICT, and the binding constraint has REVERSED. EXP-5 arm B (gates only, no discriminators) gave tp/fp/fn 142/230/9 - precision-limited. Adding cos<=-0.30 plus divergence persistence gives 68/23/83 - recall-limited. They removed 207 false positives but also 74 TRUE positives, moving divJ only 0.3727 -> 0.3908. The optimum lies between the two settings, so the next experiment is a sweep of fork_cos_max and fork_divergence_min_um against divJ rather than against precision."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1218, "key": "embryo_asymmetry_is_a_generalisation_RISK", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "44b6 divJ 0.7037 prec 0.950 | 6bba divJ 0.3333 prec 0.690", "review_after": null, "source_id": 91, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "SEVERE EMBRYO ASYMMETRY on divisions, and train/test are embryo-disjoint. 44b6: 19 TP of 26 divisions (73% recall), 1 FP, divJ 0.7037, precision 0.950. 6bba: 49 TP of 125 (39% recall), 22 FP, divJ 0.3333, precision 0.690. The edge term differs too (0.9816 vs 0.9612). A hidden embryo resembling 6bba scores roughly HALF what one resembling 44b6 does on the division term. Any acceptance rule tuned on the pooled corpus is dominated by 6bba, which contributes 125 of the 151 divisions - and there is no way to know which the hidden embryos resemble."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1219, "key": "exp7_fork_cos_gate_is_monotone_LOSS", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "picked on 44b6 -> cos<=1.0 div>=-99.0   HELD-OUT 6bba divJ 0.3791 | picked on 6bba -> cos<=1.0 div>=0.0   HELD-OUT 44b6 divJ 0.7308", "review_after": null, "source_id": 92, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-7 sweep, LEAVE-ONE-EMBRYO-OUT over all 199 datasets: the cosine discriminator loses divJ on BOTH embryos at every tightening step, so no value of fork_cos_max beats OFF. 44b6: 1.0->0.7778, -0.10->0.7037, -0.30->0.7037, -0.50->0.6296, -0.70->0.5185. 6bba: 1.0->0.3791, -0.10->0.3741, -0.30->0.3333, -0.50->0.3309, -0.70->0.2794. Tightening -0.10 to -0.30 costs 6 TRUE divisions on 6bba for ZERO false ones. Both LOEO directions select the same corner (cos<=1.0), so this is not an overfitting artefact. Default changed -0.30 -> 1.0."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1220, "key": "exp7_divergence_gate_was_DEAD_CODE", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "g1, g2 = nxt.get(first), nxt.get(second)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 92, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "BUG, found by the EXP-7 sweep and fixed 2026-09-11. The divergence-persistence check in resolve._accept_fork never once executed. It read nxt.get(first) where nxt = dict(first) is keyed by frame-t PARENT nodes while first/second are frame-t+1 DAUGHTER nodes - disjoint key sets, so g1/g2 were always None and the branch returned early every time. The sweep exposed it because all four values of fork_divergence_min_um, INCLUDING -99, produced byte-identical tp/fp/fn. Every result predating the fix - EXP-1's design, EXP-6, and the 68/23/83 full-corpus run - was measured with this discriminator inert. Root cause: the t+1 -> t+2 assignment does not exist yet while frame t is being resolved, so the check can only run as a post-pass once every frame is resolved (_diverges). Regression test: tests/test_resolve_fork.py."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1221, "key": "exp7_divergence_is_the_gate_that_WORKS", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "best by MIN across embryos: cos<=1.0 div>=0.0 -> 44b6 0.7308  6bba 0.3916  min 0.3916", "review_after": null, "source_id": 92, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "With the lookup fixed, divergence persistence is the only fork discriminator that earns its place, and the useful threshold is the loosest non-trivial one: sisters must merely NOT RE-CONVERGE (>=0.0um), not actively separate. At cos<=1.0 on 6bba: div>=-99 gives 58/28/67, div>=0.0 gives 56/18/69 (28 FP -> 18 for 2 TP), div>=1.0 gives 44/8/81, div>=2.0 gives 36/4/89. On 44b6 div>=0.0 reaches ZERO false positives. Tighter thresholds shed true divisions far faster than false ones. Chosen by MIN across embryos: cos<=1.0, div>=0.0."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1222, "key": "exp7_new_pipeline_baseline_divJ_0.4438 (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-12 by EXP-8 motion_gate 10->14)", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "ALL   n=199  adj_edge 1.0530  edgeJ 0.9645  divJ 0.4438  tp/fp/fn  75/ 18/ 76  prec 0.806  score 1.0974", "review_after": null, "source_id": 92, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1229, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FULL PIPELINE over all 199 datasets with the EXP-7 defaults (fork_cos_max 1.0, fork_divergence_min_um 0.0), oracle detection, 78 seconds: adj_edge 1.0530, edgeJ 0.9645, divJ 0.4438, tp/fp/fn 75/18/76, precision 0.806, SCORE 1.0974. Supersedes the 68/23/83 / divJ 0.3908 / score 1.0920 baseline. Both terms improved together - 23 FP -> 18 AND 68 TP -> 75 - because the change swapped a gate that cut recall for one that cuts precision errors specifically. Per embryo: 44b6 divJ 0.7308 (19/0/7, precision 1.000), 6bba divJ 0.3916 (56/18/69). The sweep predicted both embryo figures exactly, confirming that repair does not alter division counts."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1223, "key": "exp7_RECALL_binds_not_precision_lever_moves_upstream", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "6bba  n=128  adj_edge 1.0484  edgeJ 0.9614  divJ 0.3916  tp/fp/fn  56/ 18/ 69", "review_after": null, "source_id": 92, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-7 settles the direction of the binding constraint, reversing the earlier 'discriminators overtightened' reading. With EVERY discriminator at its loosest (cos<=1.0, div>=-99) 6bba STILL shows 67 FN against only 28 FP. Those divisions are never PROPOSED as candidates, so no acceptance rule however permissive can recover them. The remaining division headroom lives in score_edges/detect, not in resolve. Acceptance is close to done: 44b6 already runs at zero false positives. Next levers are fork_parent_um / fork_sister_um (which still bind) and candidate generation upstream."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1224, "key": "exp7b_fork_distance_gates_are_SLACK_not_binding", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "best by MIN across embryos: fork_parent_um=8.0 fork_sister_um=14.0 -> 44b6 0.7308  6bba 0.3916  min 0.3916", "review_after": null, "source_id": 93, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-7b swept fork_parent_um x fork_sister_um leave-one-embryo-out over all 199 datasets. The surface is FLAT at the defaults: parent 8,10,12,16,22 give byte-identical results at any sister >= 14, and sister 18,24,32 are identical at any parent. The defaults (12, 18) are already optimal and both LOEO directions agree (min divJ 0.3916), so there is NO headroom in these knobs. Both ARE wired - verified by pushing them into the distribution: parent 2.0um kills every division (0/0/151), 4.0um gives 6 TP, 6.0um gives 41 TP, 8.0um recovers the full 75 TP and saturates. Sister bites only below 14 (12 -> 6bba 46/18/79 vs 56/18/69 at 14). So parent saturates at 8um against a 12um default and sister at 14um against an 18um default - both sit ~1.3-1.5x clear of where they would start cutting. This is the OPPOSITE of the dead divergence gate: correctly plumbed, deliberately slack, and confirms that the accepted fork candidates are all geometrically tight (no arc exceeds 8um, no sister pair exceeds 14um)."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1225, "key": "exp8_sweep_objective_is_now_the_FULL_SCORE", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "44b6 adj_edge 1.0785 edgeJ 0.9816 divJ 0.7308 tp/fp/fn 19/0/7", "review_after": null, "source_id": 94, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The LOEO sweep harness now optimises adj_edge_jaccard + 0.1*division_jaccard, not divJ alone, and runs all four stages (refine, score_edges, resolve, repair) because repair rewrites edges. This was REQUIRED before sweeping anything upstream of resolve: the edge term carries 10x the weight of the division term, so a divJ-only objective would have reported a divJ win that was a net score loss. Parity verified against tools/run_pipeline.py at the EXP-7 defaults - identical to 4dp on both embryos (44b6 1.1515/1.0785/0.7308, 6bba 1.0876/1.0484/0.3916). Axes are command-line arguments, so any two Config fields sweep the same way."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1226, "key": "exp8_motion_gate_10_to_14_is_the_single_biggest_lever", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "best by MIN across embryos: motion_gate_um=14.0 edge_prob_min=0.48 -> 44b6 1.1640  6bba 1.1329  min 1.1329", "review_after": null, "source_id": 94, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-8 swept motion_gate_um x edge_prob_min LOEO over all 199 datasets against the FULL SCORE. motion_gate_um 10 -> 14 at edge_prob_min 0.48 is worth +0.0453 on the min-across-embryos score (1.0876 -> 1.1329), the largest single gain measured so far. BOTH LOEO directions independently select (14, 0.48), so it is not an embryo artefact. On 6bba: edgeJ 0.9614 -> 0.9915, divJ 0.3916 -> 0.5174, division FN 69 -> 21. This CONFIRMS AND THEN FIXES the EXP-7 recall diagnosis: the missing divisions were genuinely never proposed as candidates, and widening the candidate radius proposes them. edge_prob_min itself is near-flat and stays at 0.48. Full-pipeline verification: score 1.0974 -> 1.1370, edgeJ 0.9645 -> 0.9917, divJ 0.4438 -> 0.5431, tp/fp/fn 75/18/76 -> 126/81/25. Cost: runtime over 199 datasets 78s -> 186s."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1227, "key": "exp8_effective_radius_model_is_WRONG_knobs_decouple (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-12: model holds in the BINDING regime; the gate-20 counter-evidence was above saturation)", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "20.000  0.200 | 1.1610 | 20.000  0.650 | 1.1591", "review_after": null, "source_id": 94, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1230, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A plausible-looking prediction that FAILED, recorded so it is not re-derived. score_edges sets p = 1 - (d/motion_gate_um)^2 and keeps p >= edge_prob_min, which algebraically means a candidate survives iff d <= motion_gate_um*sqrt(1-edge_prob_min) - suggesting the two knobs collapse into one effective radius and the score surface should show diagonal ridges. It does NOT. At motion_gate_um=20 all four edge_prob_min values score within 0.002 of each other (44b6 1.1610, 1.1610, 1.1609, 1.1591) despite implied radii from 11.8um to 17.9um. Once the gate exceeds the true motion distribution the surplus candidates simply never win the Hungarian assignment, so the knobs DECOUPLE rather than trading off. edge_prob_min only bites when the effective radius is driven below real cell motion."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1228, "key": "exp8_resolve_could_emit_THREE_children_scorer_dropped_them", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "UserWarning: Dropped 1 outgoing edge(s) from nodes with more than two children; kept the two lowest edge ids per source.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 94, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "BUG, found via a scorer UserWarning during EXP-8 and fixed 2026-09-12. resolve's second pass never recorded that a parent had ALREADY forked, so a single parent could be selected as `best` for several unclaimed targets and finish with 3+ children. The official scorer silently DISCARDS the surplus ('Dropped 1 outgoing edge(s) from nodes with more than two children; kept the two lowest edge ids per source'), so this was invisible corruption rather than an error. It stayed hidden at motion_gate_um=10 because there were too few candidates to trigger it, and only appeared at 14+. Fix: a `forked` set caps out-degree at 2, since a cell divides once. After the fix the full 199-dataset run emits ZERO such warnings. Regression test: tests/test_resolve_fork.py::OutDegreeCap."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1229, "key": "exp8_new_pipeline_baseline_score_1.1370 (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-12 by EXP-10 repair disable)", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "ALL   n=199  adj_edge 1.0827  edgeJ 0.9917  divJ 0.5431  tp/fp/fn 126/ 81/ 25  prec 0.609  score 1.1370", "review_after": null, "source_id": 94, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1235, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FULL PIPELINE over all 199 datasets, oracle detection, with motion_gate_um 14.0 and the out-degree cap: adj_edge 1.0827, edgeJ 0.9917, divJ 0.5431, tp/fp/fn 126/81/25, precision 0.609, SCORE 1.1370 in 186 seconds. Per embryo: 44b6 1.1640 (22/4/4, divJ 0.7333), 6bba 1.1327 (104/77/21, divJ 0.5149). Supersedes the divJ 0.4438 / score 1.0974 baseline. The embryo gap has NARROWED sharply - 44b6 vs 6bba score was 1.1515 vs 1.0876 (0.064), now 1.1640 vs 1.1327 (0.031) - which matters because train/test are embryo-disjoint. PRECISION is now the weak side (0.609, 81 FP against 25 FN), reversing the constraint direction again: fork acceptance is worth revisiting now that the candidate set is wider."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1230, "key": "exp8b_candidate_surface_is_ONE_DIMENSIONAL_in_effective_radius", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "13.000  0.400 | 1.1640  1.0906  0.7333 | 14.000  0.480 | 1.1640  1.0906  0.7333", "review_after": null, "source_id": 95, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "CORRECTS the earlier 'the knobs decouple' reading. A fine grid (motion_gate_um 12-16 x edge_prob_min 0.40-0.56) shows the score surface IS one-dimensional in the effective radius R = motion_gate_um*sqrt(1-edge_prob_min), exactly as the algebra of score_edges predicts. Cells with matching R are identical to 4dp: (12,0.48) R=8.65 and (13,0.56) R=8.62 both give 44b6 1.1613; (12,0.40) R=9.30 and (14,0.56) R=9.29 both give 1.1584; (13,0.40) R=10.07, (14,0.48) R=10.09 and (15,0.56) R=9.95 all give 1.1640/1.1636; (14,0.40) R=10.84 and (15,0.48) R=10.82 both give 1.1587. The earlier contrary evidence was taken at motion_gate_um=20, ABOVE saturation, where nothing binds and every setting looks alike - that is absence of signal, not independence. 6bba peaks at R ~ 10.0um (1.1328) and falls off both sides (1.1035 at R=8.0, 1.1248 at R=12.4). PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE: tune the single quantity R, not two knobs - future sweeps of this pair are wasted effort."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1231, "key": "exp8b_defaults_are_ON_the_optimum_no_change", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "best by MIN across embryos: motion_gate_um=13.0 edge_prob_min=0.4 -> 44b6 1.1640  6bba 1.1328  min 1.1328", "review_after": null, "source_id": 95, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-8b fine grid confirms motion_gate_um=14.0 / edge_prob_min=0.48 (R=10.09um) is already at the optimum. The nominal best cell (13.0, 0.40) scores min-across-embryos 1.1328 against the default's 1.1327 - a difference of 0.0001, which is noise, and both sit at the same effective radius anyway. Both LOEO directions agree. NO CHANGE MADE. The candidate-generation knobs are now exhausted: what remains is detection quality and fork acceptance, not gate geometry."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1232, "key": "exp9_wider_candidates_do_NOT_rehabilitate_the_fork_gates", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "best by MIN across embryos: fork_cos_max=1.0 fork_divergence_min_um=0.0 -> 44b6 1.1640  6bba 1.1327  min 1.1327", "review_after": null, "source_id": 96, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A PREDICTION THAT FAILED, recorded so it is not retried. After EXP-8 widened the candidate set, precision became the weak side (0.609, 81 FP against 25 FN), so the expectation was that the fork discriminators EXP-7 had retired would now earn their place. They do not. Re-sweeping fork_cos_max x fork_divergence_min_um LOEO against the full score at motion_gate_um=14 returns the SAME optimum (1.0, 0.0) in both directions, and fork_cos_max is still monotone loss at every step: 44b6 divJ 0.7333 -> 0.7241 (cos 0.0) -> 0.7241 (-0.30) -> 0.6786 (-0.50). Divergence still peaks at 0.0 and degrades either side (6bba 0.4638 at -99, 0.5149 at 0.0, 0.5000 at 0.5, 0.4771 at 1.5). REASON: division_jaccard = TP/(TP+FP+FN) penalises a lost true division exactly as hard as a false one, so low precision is NOT by itself an argument for tightening - only a gate that sheds FP faster than TP helps, and the angle gate does the opposite at any candidate density. CONSEQUENCE: resolve is exhausted; every knob in it now sits on its measured optimum."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1233, "key": "exp10_repair_is_net_harmful_UNDER_ORACLE_DETECTION_ONLY", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "best by MIN across embryos: gap_max_frames=0.0 min_track_len=1.0 -> 44b6 1.1681  6bba 1.1369  min 1.1369", "review_after": null, "source_id": 97, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-10 swept gap_max_frames x min_track_len LOEO against the full score. Both repair operations are net-HARMFUL and the optimum is to disable both (gap_max_frames 2->0, min_track_len 6->1), worth +0.0042 min-across-embryos (1.1327 -> 1.1369). Both LOEO directions agree and the surface is monotone in both axes. CRITICAL CAVEAT: this is an ARTEFACT OF ORACLE DETECTION and MUST be re-swept once detect is trained. close_gaps exists to bridge DETECTOR MISSES and an oracle has none, so it can only invent spurious nodes; prune_short exists to remove SPURIOUS SHORT TRACKS and an oracle produces none, so it can only delete real ones. Under a real detector this verdict is expected to FLIP. The config comment in contracts.py carries this warning inline so the value cannot silently persist into the submission pipeline."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1234, "key": "exp10_repair_never_touches_divisions_guards_VERIFIED", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "0.000  1.000 | 1.1681  1.0948  0.7333 | 3.000  10.000 | 1.1597  1.0863  0.7333", "review_after": null, "source_id": 97, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Independent confirmation that repair's fork guard and prune_short division exemption both work: division_jaccard is IDENTICAL at 0.5149 across all SIXTEEN cells of the gap_max_frames x min_track_len grid, and division tp/fp/fn is 104/77/21 in every one. Every difference between cells is in the edge term alone (6bba edgeJ 0.9915 -> 0.9953). repair therefore cannot damage the division term by construction, which is what assert_forks_preserved was written to guarantee."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1235, "key": "exp10_new_pipeline_baseline_score_1.1412 (SUPERSEDED: EXP-15 learned fork acceptance, 2026-09-12)", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "ALL   n=199  adj_edge 1.0868  edgeJ 0.9955  divJ 0.5431  tp/fp/fn 126/ 81/ 25  prec 0.609  score 1.1412", "review_after": null, "source_id": 97, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1270, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FULL PIPELINE over all 199 datasets, ORACLE detection, with repair disabled: adj_edge 1.0868, edgeJ 0.9955, divJ 0.5431, tp/fp/fn 126/81/25, precision 0.609, SCORE 1.1412 in 208 seconds. Per embryo: 44b6 1.1681 (edgeJ 0.9965, divJ 0.7333), 6bba 1.1369 (edgeJ 0.9953, divJ 0.5149). The repair stage is now a no-op (+0/-0 nodes and edges). This is the CPU-side ceiling: score_edges, resolve and repair all sit on measured LOEO optima. It is an UPPER BOUND conditioned on perfect detections, NOT a submittable score - detect and refine are still stubs."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1236, "key": "account_is_VERIFIED_and_entered_no_action_needed", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "\"userHasEntered\":true,\"userRank\":1045 ... \"submissionsDisabled\":false", "review_after": null, "source_id": 98, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The Kaggle account is phone-verified, entered, and able to submit - there is NO outstanding verification task. Evidence from the authenticated API on 2026-09-12: userHasEntered=true, userRank=1045, submissionsDisabled=false, and 26 COMPLETED submissions with public scores (best 0.932 on 2026-08-31, most recent 0.496 on 2026-09-06). Kaggle blocks competition submissions until an account is phone-verified, so 26 scored submissions is proof the account cleared it. CORRECTS an UNSOURCED claim that had been written into notes/RANK1-PLAN.md listing 'Kaggle identity verification' as a blocking pre-Mac task - it was never in this database and was never true. The confusion was between (a) phone verification, a prerequisite for submitting, already done, and (b) the winner's prize-acceptance documents (eligibility certifications, W-9/W-8BEN), which Rule 3.1 requires only AFTER being notified as a winner and which cannot be done in advance."}
