{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1257, "key": "exp11_annotation_density_is_7x_ASYMMETRIC_between_embryos", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "TOTAL annotated 133,318 of estimated 4,725,117 = 2.8215% | 44b6 0.7712% | 6bba 5.3710%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 109, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE central constraint on detector training, measured across all 199 datasets by comparing geff node counts against the host's own estimated_number_of_nodes. Overall 133,318 annotated of 4,725,117 estimated = 2.82%. But it is NOT uniform: 44b6 is 20,197 / 2,618,970 = 0.77% while 6bba is 113,121 / 2,106,147 = 5.37% - a SEVEN-FOLD difference. Per dataset the range is 0.13% (44b6_18ced818) to 20.21% (6bba_ef7b4f7e), a 155x spread; p10 0.41%, p90 15.12%. CONSEQUENCES: (1) dense supervision is impossible - treating unannotated voxels as background teaches the detector to suppress 97% of real cells, which detect.py already names as the documented cause of loss blowing up at ~10 epochs; (2) 85% of all supervision comes from 6bba, so a detector trained pooled is effectively a 6bba detector and the embryo-disjoint test is a real risk; (3) the 44b6 arm of every LOEO result rests on 20k nodes and 26 divisions against 6bba's 113k and 125, so the 44b6 direction is the NOISIER one and should not be treated as equally evidenced."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1258, "key": "exp11_public_test_dir_is_4_COPIES_of_train_hidden_set_is_swapped_in", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "Train and test sets are embryo-disjoint - no embryo appears in both.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 109, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The downloaded bundle's test/ directory is a DEVELOPMENT STUB, not the test set: 4 .zarr volumes, 0 .geff, and all 4 names also appear in train/. Host documentation states it verbatim: 'test/ - Example test samples (copies from train). Contains .zarr image volumes only - no ground truth is provided. When a notebook is submitted for rerun, a new hidden test set is swapped in. The size of the hidden test set is approximately the same size as the training dataset.' So the hidden test set is ~199 datasets ~ 82GB, which is what the 12h kernel must process. The same page confirms the split assumption every LOEO decision rests on: 'Train and test sets are embryo-disjoint - no embryo appears in both.' The bundle holds 24,886 files / 87.6GB: 199 train .zarr + 199 train .geff + 4 test .zarr."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1259, "key": "exp11_detector_is_NOT_compute_limited_at_any_sane_width", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "64     3   25,551,940        584.5        11631     0.22     0.65", "review_after": null, "source_id": 109, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-11 measured params and FLOPs with torch's own FlopCounterMode on the architecture detect.py specifies (Upsample+Conv3d decoders, bias=True, (1,4,4) stem, depth 3). Inference over the whole hidden test set (199 datasets x 100 frames = 19,900 volumes of 64x256x256) against a 12h kernels-only budget, at a realistic 15 TFLOPS effective for 3D convs on a T4: base_c=16 1.60M params 36.7 GFLOP/vol 0.01h; c=32 6.39M 146 GFLOP 0.05h; c=48 14.4M 329 GFLOP 0.12h; c=64 25.6M 585 GFLOP 0.22h. Even c=64 is THIRTEEN MINUTES of GPU compute against 720 minutes. The (1,4,4) stem is why - it drops the working grid to 64x64x64, 16x fewer voxels. Training memory is equally slack: fp32 activations at batch 1 are 0.20GB (c=16) to 0.79GB (c=64) forward, ~2.4GB with gradients at c=64, against 48GB unified. Peak extraction is not a bottleneck either (scipy maximum_filter 64^3 size 7^3 = 16.6ms/frame = 5.5 min single-core for the whole test set). CONCLUSION: the kernel budget will be dominated by zarr I/O and decompression, not by the network. Model width should be chosen on DATA (133k labelled cells), never on compute."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1260, "key": "HOST_CONFIRMS_zebrahub_external_data_is_ALLOWED", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "Yes you are free to use the data and all resources in Zebrahub for this competition! There is no overlap with the test set.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 110, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "RESOLVED, and it is the single most actionable thing on the forum given that supervision is our binding constraint. A HOST (Thibgolds) answered the external-data question on 2026-08-13: 'Hi! Yes you are free to use the data and all resources in Zebrahub for this competition! There is no overlap with the test set.' Zebrahub is the Royer lab's public zebrafish light-sheet dataset - the SAME organism, the same imaging modality, and densely tracked. Our own corpus is 2.82% annotated (0.77% on 44b6), so external dense labels attack the exact constraint that model size cannot. The previous DB entry for this topic was the unresolved stub 'check host answer'; this supersedes that. NOTE the host's 'no overlap with the test set' also removes the leakage objection."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1261, "key": "hengck23_focus3d_to_kaggle_OFFSET_two_head_recipe", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "have two heads: one to predict Focus3D zyx, another head dzyx, then Kagge zyx = Focus3D +dzyx", "review_after": null, "source_id": 111, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "detector", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A concrete recipe from the strongest public contributor (hengck23, 2026-09-05) that refines the architecture detect.py already assumes. Focus3D's centroid convention DIFFERS from Kaggle's, and rather than fight the offset you learn it: '1) Just pretrain with Focus3D annotation 2) find matches (hit) of Focus3D and Kaggle zyx to compute diff dzyx 3) have two heads: one to predict Focus3D zyx, another head dzyx, then Kaggle zyx = Focus3D + dzyx'. He adds: 'might as well let diff head predict values in um (instead of quantized 64x64x64 coord)'. This maps exactly onto our node_head (dense, pseudo-label supervised) + refine_head (sparse, Kaggle supervised) split, and tells us the refine head should regress PHYSICAL micrometres, not grid coordinates. He also reports a trained unet3d on focus3d+kaggle centroids at kaggle.com/code/hengck23/cell-point-detector and claims 'i basically solved the cell detection problem'."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1262, "key": "hengck23_reports_6s_per_volume_CONTRADICTS_our_headroom_estimate", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "(6 sec per volume on one T4 gpu)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 111, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A DISCREPANCY to resolve before trusting the 19x kernel headroom from EXP-12. hengck23 reports his unet3d detector runs at '(6 sec per volume on one T4 gpu)' with 'input 64x64x64 (one volume)'. Our EXP-11 estimate is FLOP-based (base_c=64 -> 0.22h for all 19,900 frames at an assumed 15 TFLOPS effective). If 6s is really per 64^3 crop then a 64x256x256 frame is ~16 such crops and the full test set would be ~33h - over the 12h limit and ~150x our estimate. The likeliest explanation is that his figure is unoptimised wall-clock including I/O, model load and post-processing rather than steady-state batched inference, but OUR NUMBER IS THEORETICAL AND HIS IS MEASURED. Do not plan ensembling or TTA on the 19x headroom until a real batched forward pass is timed on actual GPU hardware."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1263, "key": "gt_contains_extreme_jumps_up_to_37_voxels_in_z", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "min       -37.000000    -13.000000     -9.750000", "review_after": null, "source_id": 112, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Ground truth contains motion far outside our candidate gate, and the community reads it as an imaging artefact rather than biology. hengck23's displacement census over 63,751 GT links reports dz_sub min -37.0 voxels (= -60um at the 1.625um z spacing), dy_sub min -13.0, dx_sub min -9.75, against means near zero and std ~1. Our tuned effective candidate radius is ~10um (EXP-8b), so these edges are unreachable BY CONSTRUCTION. Timmy Juicehouse attributes them to 'a fault in the imaging system causing repeated frames or dropped frames', reasoning that continuous development cannot produce 'perfectly repeated frames where subtraction yields exactly 0'. Tom counters 'Some cells are natural long jumpers'. These are rare tail events, so widening the gate to catch them would cost far more in false candidates than it recovers - but it bounds the achievable edge Jaccard and explains part of the residual FN."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1264, "key": "zebrahub_ACQUIRED_105x_more_divisions_than_kaggle", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "ZSNS003: 4,057,611 rows | tracks 169,108 | DIVISIONS: 15,868 | parents with >2 children: 0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 113, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "data", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Zebrahub is downloaded and characterised, and it dwarfs our corpus. The public bucket public.czbiohub.org/royerlab/zebrahub/imaging/single-objective/ holds 5 light-sheet timelapses (ZSNS001, ZSNS001_tail, ZSNS003, ZSNS004, ZSNS005) as .ome.zarr plus a *_tracks.csv per dataset in EXACTLY our format: track_id,t,z,y,x,parent_track_id (-1 = track start; a division is one parent claimed by two children). ZSNS003 alone: 4,057,611 annotated nodes, 169,108 tracks, 15,868 divisions, 515 timepoints, 4,382-11,771 cells per frame. Our ENTIRE Kaggle corpus has 133,318 nodes and 151 divisions - so one Zebrahub dataset is 30x the nodes and 105x the divisions. Tracks are only ~2.3GB for all five. ZERO parents have more than two children, which independently validates the out-degree cap added in EXP-8. RESOLUTION MATCHES: Zebrahub level 0 is z 1.24um, y/x 0.439um against Kaggle's 1.625/0.40625 - within 1.08x in xy and 1.31x in z. Level 0 is ~1TB per dataset uncompressed, but level 2 ([515,1,65,483,510]) is ~16GB and its Z depth of 65 nearly matches Kaggle's 64."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1265, "key": "zebrahub_INDEPENDENTLY_CONFIRMS_exp7_and_exp7b", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "fork_cos_max= -0.3: rejects 9.6% | fork_parent_um=12: rejects 0.0% | fork_sister_um=18: rejects 0.0%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 113, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The Zebrahub division geometry, measured over 76,217 clean divisions (30,202 in ZSNS003 + 46,015 in ZSNS005), independently corroborates both fork conclusions reached on Kaggle's 151. COSINE: Zebrahub median -0.856 / -0.899 (IQR -0.944..-0.671, -0.963..-0.760) against Kaggle EXP-1's -0.746. Applying our gates to real Zebrahub divisions, fork_cos_max=-0.30 would reject 4.1-9.6% of them - closely matching the 6 TP lost from 125 (~5%) that the Kaggle sweep measured when tightening -0.10 to -0.30. Since that tightening removed ZERO false positives on Kaggle, EXP-7's decision to disable the angle gate stands on two independent corpora. DISTANCE GATES: fork_parent_um=12 rejects 0.0-0.1% and fork_sister_um=18 rejects 0.0-0.1% of real Zebrahub divisions, confirming EXP-7b's 'wired but slack' verdict. Zebrahub parent arcs are median 4.6um (p99 8.3-9.0) and sister separations median 7.4um (p99 14.5), which is exactly why the parent gate saturated at 8um in EXP-7b."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1266, "key": "RISK_divergence_gate_rejects_38pct_of_zebrahub_true_divisions", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "divergence>= 0.0: rejects  38.0% of real divisions | KAGGLE median 1.724 vs ZEBRAHUB 1.315, ratio 1.31x", "review_after": null, "source_id": 113, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A GENERALISATION RISK on the fork_divergence_min_um=0.0 default set in EXP-7. On Zebrahub it would reject 38.0-38.6% of REAL divisions, because sister cells frequently CONVERGE one frame after birth: divergence median is only +0.18/+0.10um with p25 NEGATIVE (-0.35, -0.33). That contradicts EXP-1's Kaggle reading that 'sisters keep diverging, 10.570um at birth -> 13.656um by t+2'. It is NOT explained by temporal sampling: per-frame displacement is median 1.724um on Kaggle vs 1.315um on Zebrahub, only 1.31x apart. DECISION: KEEP 0.0 for now. Instrumenting the real pipeline shows the gate is active on 95.7% of the 253 candidate forks and rejects 19.0% of them, and that rejection removes 11 false divisions for 4 true ones on Kaggle - a net gain on the metric we are actually scored on, measured in-domain. The Zebrahub figure describes a different distribution. But if the hidden test embryo behaves like Zebrahub rather than like our two, this gate is the most fragile default we hold."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1267, "key": "exp14_error_atlas_attributes_every_missed_division", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "RECOVERED 126 83.4% | NOT_PROPOSED 15 9.9% | FORK_REJECTED/diverge 8 5.3% | LOST_IN_ASSIGNMENT 2 1.3%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 115, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE ERROR ATLAS replaces scalar grid-search with per-item causal attribution. For each of the 151 ground-truth divisions it names the stage that lost it, exactly rather than by distance matching, because under oracle detection node index i IS ground-truth node i. Result over all 199 datasets: RECOVERED 126 (83.4%, which matches the pipeline's 126 TP exactly and so validates the atlas), NOT_PROPOSED 15 (9.9%, score_edges never offered a daughter edge as a candidate), FORK_REJECTED/diverge 8 (5.3%, the divergence gate refused it), LOST_IN_ASSIGNMENT 2 (1.3%), REPAIR_DESTROYED 0. Per embryo: 44b6 22 recovered / 3 diverge / 1 not-proposed; 6bba 104 recovered / 14 not-proposed / 5 diverge / 2 assignment. TWO CONSEQUENCES. (1) The divergence gate costs 8 real divisions, independently confirming the Zebrahub warning that it is our most fragile default -- it is kept only because EXP-9 measured it as net positive on the metric. (2) REPAIR_DESTROYED 0 independently confirms EXP-10's finding that divJ was identical across all 16 repair grid cells. The largest remaining bucket is candidate generation, not acceptance."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1268, "key": "exp14_false_divisions_are_INVENTED_not_unannotated", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "GT_CONTINUES_second_child_invented 50 96.2% | GT_SUCCESSOR_UNANNOTATED 1 1.9% | geometrically indistinguishable from a real division: 21/52 = 40.4%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 116, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The atlas now attributes FALSE divisions, and the structural answer kills a comfortable hypothesis. 96.2% are GT_CONTINUES_second_child_invented: the parent genuinely continues as ONE track in ground truth and we invented a second child. Only 1 of 52 was GT_SUCCESSOR_UNANNOTATED, so the idea that our false divisions are mostly REAL divisions nobody annotated is WRONG - they are linking errors and therefore fixable. Geometrically, 40.4% sit inside the p1..p99 envelope of 76,217 Zebrahub divisions and 59.6% do not; of those outside, 25 of 31 violate PARENT ARC. True forks have arc p50 6.91um (p75 8.22) against false forks p50 8.92um (p25 8.34) - a real offset."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1269, "key": "exp15_learned_fork_acceptance_beats_every_threshold", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "held-out 6bba: before tp 104 fp 77 divJ 0.5149 | after tp 95 fp 31 divJ 0.6090 delta +0.0941", "review_after": null, "source_id": 117, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-15 proves the true/false fork signal is REAL but NOT AXIS-ALIGNED, which is why ten threshold sweeps all came out flat or negative. Parent arc separates in distribution, yet a cut at 8.5um that removes 73% of false forks also removes 25% of true ones, and divJ weights them equally: 126/203=0.621 becomes 98/165=0.594, WORSE. The sweeps were right. A classifier reaches what no threshold can: best single-feature AUC is 0.806 IN-SAMPLE (arc_max), while a 7-feature logistic model reaches AUC 0.832 HELD-OUT, min across embryos. Gradient boosting overfits at n=207 (0.728). At a leave-one-embryo-out operating point, with the threshold chosen on the OTHER embryo and applied blind: 44b6 divJ 0.7333->0.7692 (+0.0359), 6bba 0.5149->0.6090 (+0.0941). Shipped as src/biohub/fork_model.json - 7 features, 8 numbers, no sklearn at runtime. Coefficients are physically sensible: arc_max -0.767, divergence +0.866, cos -0.516."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1270, "key": "exp15_new_pipeline_baseline_score_1.1499 (SUPERSEDED: EXP-16 LOEO-tuned threshold, 2026-09-12)", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "ALL   n=199  adj_edge 1.0870  edgeJ 0.9957  divJ 0.6284  tp/fp/fn 115/ 32/ 36  prec 0.782  score 1.1499", "review_after": null, "source_id": 118, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1277, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FULL PIPELINE over all 199 datasets, oracle detection, with learned fork acceptance at fork_accept_p=0.40: adj_edge 1.0870, edgeJ 0.9957, divJ 0.6284, tp/fp/fn 115/32/36, precision 0.782, SCORE 1.1499. Per embryo 44b6 1.1688 (divJ 0.7407, 20/1/6) and 6bba 1.1465 (divJ 0.6090, 95/31/30); min across embryos 1.1369 -> 1.1465. Supersedes the divJ 0.5431 / score 1.1412 baseline. False divisions fall 81 -> 32 for 11 true ones. NOTE the shipped model is trained on BOTH embryos, so this pooled number is optimistic; the honest generalisation estimate is the LOEO result (+0.0359 / +0.0941 on divJ). 6bba's shipped divJ of 0.6090 exactly matches its LOEO-predicted value, which is reassuring."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1271, "key": "env_override_typo_was_SILENT_now_fails_loudly", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "unrecognised BIOHUB_* override(s): BIOHUB_fork_accept_p", "review_after": null, "source_id": 119, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A trap that cost a whole threshold sweep and would have cost more. _env() upper-cases the field name, so a lower-case override like BIOHUB_fork_accept_p=0.4 sets NOTHING and reports nothing - the sweep returned four identical rows and looked like a genuine null result. contracts.check_env() now raises on any BIOHUB_* variable matching no field, and lists the valid names. run_pipeline calls it at startup. A config override that is silently ignored is strictly worse than one that crashes."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1272, "key": "exp17_lost_edges_are_UNRECOVERABLE_velocity_linking_is_dead", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "typical step (edges we got right) n=128445 p50=  1.82 | what the LOST truth demanded n= 438 p50= 12.19 | truth OUTSIDE our candidate radius 374 85.4%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 120, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "linking", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-17 reproduces, on our own system and corpus, a negative result posted by a 42nd-place competitor on the Focus3D thread 2026-09-12. Under oracle detection we lose only 438 of 128,883 GT edges (0.34%), and those losses are NOT a model failure - the truth is locally implausible. Typical correct step is p50 1.82um (p90 4.08, p99 7.98); the lost truth demands p50 12.19um (p90 17.22, p99 51.98), i.e. 6.7x the normal step and beyond the p99 of correct edges. 85.4% of lost edges lie OUTSIDE our candidate radius entirely. In contested cases the truth is the LONGER jump 92.3% of the time, median margin +5.74um against it. Their independent numbers match ours in shape: both parents ~1.5um from what they linked against a 1.16um/frame step, truth asking ~9um. CONSEQUENCES: (1) velocity extrapolation and Kalman linking are DEAD for us - they reported the true child picked in 0 of 381 cases and 0 of 271 swaps, and our geometry says the same; (2) these edges are bounded by GROUND-TRUTH quality, tying to our recorded fact that Kaggle GT contains jumps to -60um the forum reads as dropped or repeated frames; (3) edge Jaccard 0.9957 under oracle detection is therefore close to the ceiling the annotation permits."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1273, "key": "competitor_dense_finetune_HURTS_when_encoder_moves", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "dense labels, detection head only 33 0.0716 0.9429 -0.0033 | dense labels, head plus encoder 1,496,353 0.0107 0.9085 -0.0377", "review_after": null, "source_id": 121, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "detector", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A 42nd-place competitor fine-tuned on dense Focus3D labels and measured the cost over five held-out films with the official scorer, against an honest baseline of 0.9462 (not their fielded 0.9557 - their checkpoint enters through a weights override that also swaps the primary link model). Detection head only, 33 params: held-out detector loss 0.1139 -> 0.0716, score -0.0033. Head PLUS encoder, 1,496,353 params: loss -> 0.0107, a TEN-FOLD better detector, yet score -0.0377. Their diagnosis: 'my edge transformer is frozen and reads that encoder... Extra detections are nearly free; moving the representation underneath the linker is what hurts.' THIS DOES NOT TRANSFER TO US, AND THE REASON MATTERS: our linker is purely geometric - score_edges computes p from Euclidean distance and resolve is a Hungarian assignment over coordinates. Nothing downstream of detect reads encoder features, so moving the encoder cannot break our linker. Their -0.0377 is a coupling failure specific to a frozen-transformer-on-encoder design; it is evidence FOR our stage separation, not a warning against dense pretraining."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1274, "key": "competitor_confirms_dzyx_bias_must_be_LEARNED_per_node", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "over 15,693 matched pairs the mean residual is dz -0.57, dy -0.24, dx -0.20 um, at 48, 31 and 26 standard errors from zero, against a median match distance of 1.50", "review_after": null, "source_id": 122, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "detector", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Independent confirmation of hengck23's Focus3D->Kaggle offset recipe, with the detail that kills the cheap version. Over 15,693 matched pairs the centroid bias is dz -0.57, dy -0.24, dx -0.20 um at 48, 31 and 26 standard errors from zero, against a median match distance of only 1.50um - the bias is a third of the matching tolerance and overwhelmingly significant. But correcting it with a CONSTANT loses 0.0031, 'since matching is an assignment on relative distance', and it is EMBRYO DEPENDENT: near zero in z for one of their embryos and -0.61 for the other. So our refine head must regress a PER-NODE offset in micrometres, never a global constant, and must not assume the offset transfers across embryos - sharp for us, since our two embryos already differ 2x in intensity and 7x in annotation density."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "medium", "id": 1275, "key": "competitor_one_sixth_of_contested_links_is_a_SELECTION_failure", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "My link model prefers the true child in 44 of 290 contested cases, 15.2 per cent. So a sixth is a selection failure your structured loss could fix with current features, worth about +0.005 edge jaccard here, and the rest needs the node and link models to actually move.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 123, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "linking", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The one POSITIVE finding in an otherwise negative report, and it bounds what better global optimisation can buy. On 290 contested links their model already ranks the true child first in 44 cases (15.2%) yet the solver still chooses wrong - a pure SELECTION failure fixable with current features, worth about +0.005 edge Jaccard. The other ~85% need the underlying node and link models to improve. Read with EXP-17: our own lost edges demand a median 12.19um jump against a 1.82um typical step, so most of our residual is the unrecoverable kind, not the selectable kind. Their +0.005 ceiling is the right order of magnitude for what a smarter assignment can win, and it is small."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1276, "key": "exp16_LOEO_tuned_fork_accept_p_honest_gain_is_0.0059", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "picked p=0.25 on 44b6 -> HELD-OUT 6bba score 1.1428 (divJ 0.5723) | picked p=0.35 on 6bba -> HELD-OUT 44b6 score 1.1661 (divJ 0.7143) | MIN across the two directions: 1.1428", "review_after": null, "source_id": 124, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-16 tunes fork_accept_p properly: BOTH the model and the threshold are refitted per fold, because EXP-15 shipped a model trained on both embryos and then picked its operating point from a pooled sweep - two separate leaks. Protocol: train on embryo A, pick the threshold on A, report blind on B. Result: p=0.25 chosen on 44b6 gives held-out 6bba 1.1428 (baseline 1.1369, +0.0059); p=0.35 chosen on 6bba gives held-out 44b6 1.1661 (baseline 1.1681, MINUS 0.0020). MIN across directions 1.1428, so the HONEST gain on the min is +0.0059, NOT the +0.0087 the pooled sweep reported. TWO THINGS WORTH KEEPING. (1) The model HELPS the weaker embryo and slightly HURTS the stronger one - and since the hidden test embryo could be either, that asymmetry is a risk, not a rounding error. (2) The 44b6 fold model is fitted on only 26 forks, so that direction is thin evidence. Shipped fork_accept_p=0.30, the mean of the two independently chosen folds, rather than the 0.40 a contaminated pooled sweep preferred."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1277, "key": "exp16_new_pipeline_baseline_score_1.1492", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "ALL   n=199  adj_edge 1.0870  edgeJ 0.9957  divJ 0.6218  tp/fp/fn 120/ 42/ 31  prec 0.741  score 1.1492", "review_after": null, "source_id": 125, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FULL PIPELINE over all 199 datasets, oracle detection, at the LOEO-derived fork_accept_p=0.30: adj_edge 1.0870, edgeJ 0.9957, divJ 0.6218, tp/fp/fn 120/42/31, precision 0.741, SCORE 1.1492. Per embryo 44b6 1.1672 (divJ 0.7241, 21/3/5) and 6bba 1.1460 (divJ 0.6037, 99/39/26). Slightly below the 1.1499 at p=0.40, and that is deliberate: 0.40 was chosen by a pooled sweep using a model that had seen both embryos, while 0.30 is the mean of two independent LOEO folds. The honest generalisation estimate is the LOEO min of 1.1428, not either pooled figure."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1278, "key": "exp18_THE_ORACLE_SCORE_IS_A_DENSITY_ARTEFACT", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "1.0 -0.990 0.9966 1.0948 0.7200 1.1668 | 1.5 -0.985 0.9711 1.0662 0.0889 1.0750 | 12.0 -0.879 0.4819 0.5218 0.0063 0.5224", "review_after": null, "source_id": 126, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1289, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE MOST IMPORTANT FINDING OF THE SESSION, and it reframes everything measured before it. Our 1.1492 is conditioned on oracle detection, which supplies ONLY the 2.82% of cells that are annotated - 6.7 cells per frame. The host estimates 237 cells per frame. A real detector therefore operates at 35x the node density our entire linker was tuned on. EXP-18 adds unannotated-cell-like detections to an oracle run and links them normally: at 1.5x nodes the score falls 1.1668 -> 1.0750, and DIVISION JACCARD COLLAPSES 0.7200 -> 0.0889. At 12x, score 0.5224 and edgeJ 0.4819. The mechanism is NOT the node-count term: total_node_ratio moves only -0.990 -> -0.879 because n_total is ~35x our count, so the multiplier is a near-constant +9% bonus we cannot leave and cannot exploit further. The damage is LINKING CONTENTION - extra nodes win targets in the Hungarian assignment that belong to real cells, and fork acceptance is destroyed first. CONSEQUENCES: (1) 1.1492 is NOT a ceiling we approach from below, it is an artefact of knowing which cells are annotated, and no detector can know that; (2) every threshold tuned this session - motion_gate_um, fork_accept_p, the fork model, repair - was fitted at 6.7 cells/frame and must be re-validated at realistic density; (3) the public plateau's shape (edgeJ ~0.926, divJ ~0.231) is what real density looks like, and it is the honest reference, not our oracle number."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1279, "key": "node_count_multiplier_is_a_CONSTANT_not_a_lever", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "adj_edge_jaccard = max(0.0, edge_jaccard * (1 - ADJUSTMENT_ALPHA * total_node_ratio),) | ADJUSTMENT_ALPHA: float = 0.1", "review_after": null, "source_id": 127, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Settles what the node-count adjustment is worth to US, as opposed to in theory. The scorer computes total_node_ratio = (N_pred - n_total)/n_total and J_adj = max(0, J*(1 - 0.1*ratio)) with NO ceiling, so under-predicting nodes is rewarded without limit. But n_total is the host's estimate of TRUE cell count, ~35x the annotated count, so any realistic solution sits at ratio between -0.99 and -0.85 and receives a near-constant multiplier of 1.085 to 1.099. Measured across a 12x sweep of predicted node counts the multiplier moved only 1.099 -> 1.088. So the +9% is real, it is why our adj_edge 1.0870 exceeds our edgeJ 0.9957, and it is NOT a lever: no realistic change in detection count moves it meaningfully. Effort spent gaming node counts is wasted; the term to optimise is edge Jaccard, which the same experiment shows is destroyed by exactly the extra detections that would move the node count."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1280, "key": "review_pacmap_target_and_decoder", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "Each Gaussian kernel’s amplitude and standard deviation is equal to the proximity to the nearest neighbor", "review_after": null, "source_id": 128, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "PAC-MAP's target amplitude encodes nearest-neighbour distance, not inverse distance; width uses an estimated nuclear radius. Author code points2prob.py queries k=2 neighbours in physical coordinates and passes dist[:,1] as target values. It also uses a proximity-aware decoder. A target-only change with the current fixed-threshold decoder is not a faithful PAC-MAP implementation. Weak-label pretraining plus manual fine-tuning does not establish support for arbitrary missing Kaggle annotations: missing neighbours inflate target distances. Test on dense pseudo-centres with a defined uncertainty mask and matched decoder. The authors now cite the 2025 Computers in Biology and Medicine publication, DOI 10.1016/j.compbiomed.2024.109561."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1281, "key": "review_zebrahub_label_provenance", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "Ultrack, the cell tracking tool used.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 129, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "The dataset provider identifies Ultrack as the tool producing the released tracking data. Treat these lineages as algorithm-generated reference labels unless curation is separately documented. Agreement is not proof of biological truth or evidence of beating Ultrack. Use spatial crops with intact trajectories, native physical scales and matched time intervals; measure nearest-neighbour spacing and motion, not just cells/frame. Whole-embryo cell count cannot establish local crop density. The local host-response archive allows Zebrahub and states no test overlap, but this does not establish label correctness or exclude overlap with training material."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1282, "key": "review_ultrack_joint_hypotheses", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "By evaluating multiple candidate segmentations and employing temporal consistency", "review_after": null, "source_id": 130, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "Ultrack jointly considers segmentation hypotheses and temporal consistency. This supports testing whether irreversible detection and Hungarian decisions cause errors; it does not require replacing the entire pipeline immediately. A baseline comparison must use identical detections and evaluation regions, and cannot score Ultrack against its own generated labels as an independent accuracy test. The Nature paper landing page/PDF could not be retrieved in this review; implementation claims were checked against the authors' repository and the linked ECCV algorithm paper."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1283, "key": "review_linajea_sparse_supervision", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "We only train on the mean squared error loss within the training mask.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 131, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "Malin-Mayor et al., Automated reconstruction of whole-embryo cell lineages by learning from sparse annotations (DOI 10.1038/s41587-022-01427-7), explicitly train cell-centre predictions on local masks around sparse annotations. Their backward movement vectors let both daughters point to one parent. This is a more direct sparse-supervision precedent than PAC-MAP. It disproves the categorical claim that sparse annotations cannot supervise detection, but does not demonstrate Biohub gains. Compare a masked Gaussian baseline before adding dense pseudo-label dependence or a second refinement head. Background predictions remain unconstrained and require filtering; mask radius must avoid neighbouring nuclei. The paper also uses augmentation, so a universal ban on augmentation is not supported."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1284, "key": "review_trackastra_3d_checkpoint", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "\"dimensionality\": [2, 3]", "review_after": null, "source_id": 132, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "The authors' checkpoint registry lists ctc as supporting 2D and 3D; the usual general_2d example is not the appropriate 3D checkpoint. Trackastra links segmented objects, so centroid-only integration needs an explicit feature/mask adapter and validation. Candidate for a paired linker baseline after that adapter exists. Freeze the checkpoint, code and training-data ancestry before use; Cell Tracking Challenge success does not imply a Biohub score."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1285, "key": "review_colab_budget_is_estimate", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "Paid users whose compute unit balance is exhausted will revert to the free-of-charge tier policies", "review_after": null, "source_id": 133, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "Colab hardware availability and runtime limits are dynamic. The proposed units-to-A100-hours conversion is a planning estimate, not a guaranteed allocation. Record the actual offered GPU, memory, unit burn rate, checkpoint resume behavior and measured step time before a budgeted run. There is no technical reason to wait for a Mac to perform CPU validation or prepare CUDA training, but peak throughput specifications do not establish this workload's platform ranking."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1286, "key": "review_a100_peak_not_throughput", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "312 TFLOPS", "review_after": null, "source_id": 134, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "NVIDIA specifies A100 dense BF16 Tensor Core peak at 312 TFLOPS. This is a peak hardware figure, not measured 3D-convolution training throughput. Two T4s do not automatically combine memory or accelerate a single-process pipeline. Benchmark actual batch/precision/model shape and I/O before forecasting training or the Kaggle rerun."}
{"claim_type": "claim", "confidence": "high", "id": 1287, "key": "review_current_submission_requirements", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "Internet access disabled", "review_after": null, "source_id": 135, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "The current competition overview requires notebook submissions, a file named submission.csv, disabled internet, and a maximum 12-hour CPU or GPU run. Public external data is allowed. Package weights, code and offline dependencies together and validate complete dataset coverage. A local graph roundtrip does not establish Kaggle acceptance, hidden-data runtime or GPU parity."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1288, "key": "review_metric_density", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "\"multiplier_at_estimated_density\": 1.0", "review_after": null, "source_id": 136, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "The host-count multiplier is 1.1 - 0.1*N_pred/N_total, clipped at zero on adjusted edge score. At estimated density it is 1.0; sparse-oracle bonus is not constant across realistic predictions. Metadata totals are 133318 annotated and 4725117 estimated; their pooled ratio implies illustrative multiplier 1.097178524891553, not the weighted aggregate score. Counts and ratios vary by embryo."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1289, "key": "review_exp18_scope", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "\"first_60_embryos\": {\n      \"44b6\": 60\n    }", "review_after": null, "source_id": 136, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "EXP-18 independently scatters extra points rather than generating coherent tracks, samples only 44b6 in its first 60 sorted datasets, permits out-of-bounds positions, seeds with process-dependent hash(), and uses the both-embryo model. Precise published drops are historical unreplicated claims; do not use them as a causal estimate of realistic density. Revalidate all oracle-tuned thresholds, but retain oracle results as scoped diagnostics."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1290, "key": "review_readiness_probes", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "\"name\": \"graph_csv_reload_official_scorer_roundtrip\"", "review_after": null, "source_id": 136, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "Eight CPU diagnostic probes: seven readiness expectations fail (plateaus, stem-grid NMS, NaN rejection, negative endpoint rejection, empty CSV rejection, forks competing against continuations, coherent EXP-18 tracks). One synthetic graph-to-CSV-to-reloaded-official-scorer roundtrip passes. This is not image-to-submission or Kaggle validation. Full results and hashes in checks.json."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1291, "key": "review_notebook_scope", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "base,0.9259611085554402,0.23076923076923078,0.9490380316323633,3,1,9,63,182965,5535,137,79,0,8,468.88628602027893,{}", "review_after": null, "source_id": 137, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "The archived decomposition belongs to eight-sample local CV with adjusted_edge_jaccard 0.9259611085554402 and division_jaccard 0.23076923076923078. It is not hidden leaderboard component disclosure and cannot establish an edge ceiling for the field. Prior notebook/rescore census is historical, not independently refreshed here."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1292, "key": "review_image_pipeline_missing", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "raise NotImplementedError(\"wire node_head + peak extraction here\")", "review_after": null, "source_id": 138, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "detect() is unimplemented even when supplied a model. run_pipeline.py uses detect_oracle; refinement without a model is a pass-through. An image-to-submission package is not demonstrated."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1293, "key": "review_fold_ancestry", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "\"embryos\": [\n      \"44b6\",\n      \"6bba\"\n    ]", "review_after": null, "source_id": 139, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "The default shipped fork model lists both embryos as training members. Generic runner and EXP-7 do not enforce exclusion; EXP-16 supplies fold paths, but model loading silently returns probability one on a missing model. Require checked hashes, membership and fail-closed loading."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1294, "key": "review_cache_provenance", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "neg_thr = float(np.percentile(np.concatenate(peaks), NEG_PCT)) if peaks else np.inf", "review_after": null, "source_id": 140, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "Existence-only cache reuse can preserve truncated-frame or outdated targets. If sampled frames contain no labelled peaks, neg_thr becomes infinity and every finite voxel is supervised as background. A cache must record content hashes, frame coverage, target configuration and fold membership; missing labels must remain unknown."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1295, "key": "review_exp17_pairing", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "worse = (lost_d[:m] > kept_d[:m]).mean()", "review_after": null, "source_id": 141, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "lost_d contains every lost edge but kept_d only losses with alternatives. Prefix slicing does not align identities; final comparative percentages and margins are invalid if an unpaired loss precedes a paired one. Use paired tuples and preserve the old result as invalid for that comparison."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1296, "key": "review_kb_validity_laundering", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "values(?,?,?,?,?,?,?,?,'active','VALID')", "review_after": null, "source_id": 142, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "kb.record stamps every insertion VALID and measured even for unverified claims; kb.ask does not display/filter validity. This bypasses the database contract. Review writes used kb.conn with explicit source methods, types and validity; no fabricated provenance was assigned to historical measurements."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1297, "key": "review_registry_can_restore_superseded", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "quote=excluded.quote,status=excluded.status,validity=excluded.validity,", "review_after": null, "source_id": 143, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "Registry ingestion conflict updates overwrite status and validity from the source registry. A curated DB supersession can be silently reactivated on refresh. Preserve local adjudications or require an explicit reconciliation before status changes."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1298, "key": "review_existing_tests", "observed_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "quote": "Ran 20 tests in 6.232s", "review_after": null, "source_id": 144, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "review-2026-09-12", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": "Verified against cited code, primary source or bounded review diagnostic; no model-quality promotion.", "value": "All 20 existing unittest tests pass in the pre-existing score-venv. System Python lacks numpy; reference pytest suite was not run because pytest is absent. Existing green tests do not cover the failing readiness diagnostics. An edgeless-scorer diagnostic timed out under a 90-second cap; after excluding it the remaining diagnostic script completed. No GPU, model training or Kaggle run occurred."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1299, "key": "PUBLIC_CC0_TRAINED_WEIGHTS_EXIST_we_need_not_train_a_detector", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "datasetRefs=['pilkwang/biohub-deepcenter-unet3d-center-prior-v1', 'pilkwang/biohub-temporal-unet3d-seed314159-v1', 'pilkwang/biohub-tracking-support-pack-50ep-v1'] | CC0: Public Domain", "review_after": null, "source_id": 145, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "STRATEGIC UNLOCK. Every clean 0.947 kernel (sjlee101/biohub-lf-dctta, flexonafft/biohub-harmonic-fusion, sjlee101/biohub-hf-sister18) attaches the SAME three public Kaggle datasets, all licensed CC0 Public Domain: a TRAINED DeepCenter UNet3D detector (71MB), a trained temporal UNet3D (350MB), and the tracking support pack (350MB). So the problem we framed as 'detect and refine are stubs, we cannot submit' does NOT require training anything - competitive detector weights are public and free. The 0.947 is reachable by forking, not by building. This makes the earlier estimate of 'a first trained detector lands 0.85-0.93' the wrong frame entirely: the floor is the public 0.947, and the question is what we add ON TOP. Their notebooks run 6,200-7,700s against a 43,200s budget, GPU on, internet off, so there is ~5x headroom for anything we add."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1300, "key": "their_division_gates_VALIDATE_ours_but_divergence_is_theirs_to_lose", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "os.environ[\"BIOHUB_SAFE_DIV_MAX_UM\"] = \"9.0\" | os.environ[\"BIOHUB_SAFE_DIV_SISTER_MAX_UM\"] = \"14.0\" | os.environ[\"BIOHUB_SAFE_DIV_DIVERGE_UM\"] = \"2.25\" | SAFE_DIV_GLOBAL_FRAC_CAP = 0.004", "review_after": null, "source_id": 146, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 0.947 family gates divisions with FOUR hand-tuned thresholds that map one-to-one onto ours, and two of them independently confirm our sweeps. Their SAFE_DIV_MAX_UM=9.0 against our measured parent-arc saturation at 8um (EXP-7b); their SAFE_DIV_SISTER_MAX_UM=14.0 against our measured sister saturation at EXACTLY 14um (EXP-7b). Two independent tuning efforts landing on the same saturation points is strong mutual validation. BUT their SAFE_DIV_DIVERGE_UM=2.25 with REQUIRE_DIVERGENCE=1, and our Zebrahub census over 76,217 real divisions (EXP-13) says a divergence threshold >=2.0 rejects 89.6% of them. They also cap divisions at SAFE_DIV_GLOBAL_FRAC_CAP=0.004 of nodes. That conservatism is consistent with their published divJ of 0.231, and it is the specific place EXP-15 showed thresholds are beatable: a learned 7-feature acceptance model reached held-out AUC 0.832 against 0.806 for the best single threshold. CAVEAT: their conservatism may be CORRECT at real density, which our oracle-conditioned experiments cannot judge - that is exactly what a fork-and-measure run settles."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1301, "key": "THE_0947_DIVISION_SELECTOR_IS_A_TWO_TERM_HAND_WEIGHTED_SUM", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "score = parent_dist + 0.15 * sister_dist | proposals.sort(key=lambda item: item[0]) | global_cap = max(1, int(round(max(1, len(edges)) * SAFE_DIV_GLOBAL_FRAC_CAP)))", "review_after": null, "source_id": 147, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE INSERTION POINT, found by reading the 0.947 kernel. Their add_safe_divisions_postlink is structurally IDENTICAL to our resolve second pass - single-child sources, unclaimed t+1 candidates, parent and sister distance gates, a divergence check - arrived at independently. They have two things we lack: a mutual-nearest-neighbour requirement, and a RANKED proposal list truncated by a frame cap and a global cap. And the ranking key is a single line: 'score = parent_dist + 0.15 * sister_dist', sorted ascending. A two-term hand-weighted sum decides which divisions survive the cap. On our own labelled forks those two features rank at AUC 0.806 (parent_dist = our arc_max) and 0.551 (sister_dist, barely above chance), while our EXP-15 seven-feature logistic model reaches 0.832 HELD-OUT across embryos. BECAUSE THE CAP BINDS, ranking quality converts directly into division Jaccard at a fixed emission count. Their global cap of 0.004 of edges is NOT conservative - it matches the true biological rate of 0.00391 measured on Zebrahub - so this is a SELECTION problem, not a recall problem. At divJ 0.231 only 37.5% of their emitted divisions are correct; 57.1% would give divJ 0.40 (+0.017 of score) and 63.1% would give 0.461 (+0.023, the current rank-1 margin)."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1302, "key": "FOCUS3D_AND_SSL_HAVE_NOT_BEATEN_THE_FREE_0947", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "hengck23 r250@0.9460 -> r284@0.9460 -> r377@0.9460 subs 26 | Lê Quang Cảnh r304@0.9460 -> r49@0.9480 -> r41@0.9500 subs 51 | Sergio Alvarez r1@0.9700 -> r1@0.9700 -> r1@0.9700 subs 64", "review_after": null, "source_id": 148, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Empirical answer to whether the FOCUS-3D and self-supervised routes can beat the free public 0.947: NOBODY HAS DONE IT. hengck23 authored the FOCUS-3D adaptation recipe and the SSL proposals (V-JEPA 2.1, 3D optical flow) and wrote 'i basically solved the cell detection problem'; he sits at 0.9460 - BELOW the public plateau anyone can fork - and his rank fell 250 -> 284 -> 377 across our three snapshots while his score did not move. Le Quang Canh, the competitor who actually MEASURED the dense fine-tune, reached 0.9500 (rank 41) partly by discovering that dense fine-tuning HURT his score by 0.0377 when the encoder moved under a frozen linker. So two serious, independent, publicly documented detector efforts have produced 0.946 and 0.950 against a forkable 0.947. This is not proof the route cannot work, but it is strong evidence that better detection is NOT where the 0.023 gap to rank 1 lives, and it argues against spending our remaining time training detectors. Rank 1 (Sergio Alvarez) has been immovable at 0.9700 across all three snapshots with 64 submissions and 2 members."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1303, "key": "exp19_OUR_RANKER_BEATS_THEIRS_and_their_sister_term_HURTS", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "0.947 kernel: -(arc + 0.15*sister) 0.776 0.943 0.753 | arc_max alone 0.806 0.955 0.786 | held-out 6bba: OURS 0.832 THEIRS 0.753 delta +0.079 | held-out 6bba: top-104 OURS 81.7% correct THEIRS 73.1% delta +8.7%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 149, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-19 scores the 0.947 kernel's exact division ranking rule against ours on the same 207 labelled forks, leave-one-embryo-out. TWO RESULTS. (1) THEIR RULE IS WORSE THAN ITS OWN BEST FEATURE: 'parent_dist + 0.15*sister_dist' ranks at pooled AUC 0.776, while parent_dist ALONE ranks 0.806. Sister distance is AUC 0.551 - essentially noise - and the 0.15 weight drags the ranking down. Deleting one term improves their own selector at zero risk. (2) OUR LEARNED RANKER BEATS IT HELD-OUT: 0.955 vs 0.943 on 44b6 and 0.832 vs 0.753 on 6bba (+0.079). At their operating point - top-K where K is the number of true forks, which is how a binding cap behaves - we get 81.7% correct against their 73.1% on 6bba, +8.7 points of precision. TRANSLATION AT A BINDING CAP: their published divJ of 0.231 implies 37.5% of emitted divisions are correct; +8.7pp gives divJ 0.301 and +0.0070 of score, while the smaller relative reading (x1.118) gives divJ 0.265 and +0.0034. So the honest expected range for this one-line change is +0.003 to +0.007. CAVEAT: our forks come from oracle detection at 6.7 cells/frame against their ~237, so this compares ranking quality on identical data, not their behaviour at their density."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1304, "key": "KAGGLE_P100_BREAKS_THE_0947_PIPELINE_pin_the_T4 (SUPERSEDED 2026-09-12: the recorded FIX was wrong; the API ignores acceleratorType)", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB with CUDA capability sm_60 is not compatible with the current PyTorch installation. The current PyTorch install supports CUDA capabilities sm_70 sm_75 sm_80 sm_86 sm_90 sm_100 sm_120. | torch.AcceleratorError: CUDA error: no kernel image is available for execution on the device", "review_after": null, "source_id": 150, "status": "superseded", "superseded_by": 1306, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "A LIVE SUBMISSION HAZARD, found by forking the 0.947 kernel. Our byte-identical fork FAILED on its first run, and the cause is environmental rather than any flaw in the notebook: Kaggle allocated a Tesla P100 (compute capability sm_60) and the pinned PyTorch in that Docker image supports only sm_70 and above, so every CUDA call dies with 'no kernel image is available for execution on the device'. The original kernel declares machineShape NvidiaTeslaT4 and the notebook's own metadata carries accelerator=nvidiaTeslaT4, but pushing through the API with enableGpu=true ALONE does not preserve that - Kaggle then assigns whatever GPU it likes, and a P100 is fatal. FIX: pass acceleratorType='nvidiaTeslaT4' in the kernels/push body; it is accepted and produced version 2. CONSEQUENCE FOR US: any final submission must pin the T4 explicitly, or a P100 draw silently destroys a run we cannot resubmit. This also means any competitor whose kernel lands on a P100 right now fails outright, which is consistent with our recorded fact that Kaggle dropped P100 from GPU CI on 2026-09-05 while still allocating them for notebook runs."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1305, "key": "their_pipeline_RUNS_LOCALLY_against_our_ground_truth", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "model_config: {'unet_out_channels': 32, 'unet_layers': [32, 64, 128], 'downsample': [1, 4, 4], 'window_size': 2, 'pool_kernel_um': 5.0} | epoch: 402 best_score: 0.9834918738001537", "review_after": null, "source_id": 151, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The 0.947 family's pipeline is now runnable on this laptop against OUR 199 labelled datasets, which is what makes the division work measurable without burning 5 submissions a day at 2h each. Route: set BIOHUB_DATA_DIR to data/images/train (which already holds 199 .zarr and 199 .geff side by side, exactly their expected layout), PYTHONPATH to repo/src and repo/scripts, and symlink repo/weights to the pack's weights directory. Their scripts/predict_unet_transformer.py takes --split, --slice and --evaluate and falls back to CPU cleanly when no CUDA is present. TWO TRAPS: (1) the weights file is edge_predictor_best.pth, a bare state dict - checkpoint_last.pth is a full training bundle with model_state_dict nested inside and fails load_state_dict outright; (2) it needs DATASET_PATH/dataset_splits.json in the form [{'train': [...], 'test': [...]}], which we wrote to encode OUR leave-one-embryo-out folds: fold 0 holds out the 71 44b6 datasets, fold 1 the 128 6bba. ARCHITECTURE CONFIRMATION: their model_config is unet_layers [32,64,128] with downsample [1,4,4] - the same (1,4,4) stem and comparable width our EXP-11 sizing arrived at independently, and their checkpoint is 402 epochs at best_score 0.9835."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1306, "key": "P100_CANNOT_BE_AVOIDED_VIA_THE_PUSH_API_only_the_UI", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "CUDA device: Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB | Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB with CUDA capability sm_60 is not compatible with the current PyTorch installation.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 152, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "FOLLOW-UP that corrects the fix recorded for the P100 hazard. Passing acceleratorType='nvidiaTeslaT4' in the kernels/push body IS accepted by the API - it returned versionNumber 2 without error - but it does NOT change the allocation: version 2 drew a Tesla P100 again and failed identically. Kaggle's push API appears to expose no GPU selection, and it discards the notebook's own metadata.kaggle.accelerator field on push even when the .ipynb is uploaded byte-identically with accelerator=nvidiaTeslaT4. The only reliable route found is the web UI: open the kernel and set Accelerator to GPU T4 x2. CONSEQUENCE: our final submission cannot be pushed purely through the API and trusted - the accelerator must be verified in the UI, because a P100 draw is fatal and a final submission cannot be resubmitted. Unknown fields in the push body are silently ignored rather than rejected, so 'the API accepted it' is not evidence that a setting took effect."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1307, "key": "MEASUREMENT_LOOP_CLOSED_their_pipeline_scores_on_our_GT", "observed_at": "2026-09-12", "quote": "n=3  edgeJ 0.9261  adj_edge 0.9275 divJ 0.0000  div tp/fp/fn 0/3/0  node_recall 1.0000  SCORE 0.9275", "review_after": null, "source_id": 153, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE MEASUREMENT LOOP IS CLOSED. The 0.947 family's own model now runs on this laptop and scores against our 199 labelled datasets, so division changes can be measured offline instead of costing a 2-hour Kaggle run out of 5 per day. First result on 3 held-out 44b6 datasets: edgeJ 0.9261, adj_edge 0.9275, node_recall 1.0000, divJ 0.0000 (0 TP, 3 FP - these three have no GT divisions), score 0.9275. THE EDGE JACCARD OF 0.9261 MATCHES THE PUBLISHED 0.926 DECOMPOSITION ALMOST EXACTLY, which is strong evidence we are reproducing their real behaviour rather than something adjacent. NODE RECALL IS 1.0000: their detector finds every ground-truth node, so detection recall is NOT their limit - the edge term is. IMPORTANT SCOPE: this is the SUPPORT-PACK pipeline; add_safe_divisions_postlink lives in the notebook, not the repo, so 0.9275 is their base model BEFORE division post-processing, and 0.926 + 0.1*0.231 = 0.947 is exactly how their published decomposition assembles. That makes this the cleanest insertion point available: their linked graph, our division selection. TWO LOCAL PATCHES were required and are documented in-file with .orig kept: evaluate.py now calls open_dataset with load_image=False and device='cpu', because it reads only ds.scale and ds.tracks yet the default pins the full (100,64,256,256) volume to CUDA - which both crashes on a CPU-only box and OOM-killed this 16GB machine mid-run."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1308, "key": "HOW_TO_SUBMIT_a_kernels_only_entry_from_the_CLI", "observed_at": "2026-09-13", "quote": "kaggle competitions submit biohub-cell-tracking-during-development -k aryaarun07/biohub-repro-947 -v 3 -f submission.csv -m '...' -> 4 submissions remaining today.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 154, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE SUBMISSION ROUTE, established end to end. This competition is isKernelsSubmissionsOnly, so a run alone does nothing - the kernel output must be submitted explicitly. The REST endpoint /api/v1/competitions/submissions/submit/{comp} is the FILE path and rejects a kernel reference with 'BlobFileTokens must be specified'; every internal RPC guess under competitions.CompetitionService (CreateKernelSubmission, SubmitKernel, CreateSubmission, SubmitToCompetition, CreateCodeSubmission) returns 404. The working route is the CLI, which has code-competition flags the REST API does not expose: 'kaggle competitions submit <comp> -k <user>/<kernel> -v <version> -f submission.csv -m <message>', authenticated with KAGGLE_API_TOKEN set to the access token. It returned '4 submissions remaining today' and created submission 27, status pending. SCORING CAN TAKE UP TO 8 HOURS on this competition, so never block on polling the result. The full chain is: push with machineShape=NvidiaTeslaT4 (acceleratorType is accepted but ignored, and a P100 draw is fatal), wait for status=complete, confirm submission.csv is among the outputs, then submit by kernel reference."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1309, "key": "OFFLINE_DIVISION_SWEEP_HARNESS_built", "observed_at": "2026-09-13", "quote": "theirs (arc + 0.15*sister) divisions_added=205 edgeJ 0.9310 divJ nan tp/fp/fn 0/0/0 SCORE 0.9324 | prediction graphs: 3", "review_after": null, "source_id": 155, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Built the harness that makes division selection measurable without spending submissions. tools/div_sweep.py is a faithful reimplementation of the 0.947 kernel's add_safe_divisions_postlink with the RANKER made pluggable - same gates (max_um 9.0, sister_max_um 14.0, existing_child 10.0, symmetry_tau 0.6, diverge_um 2.25, require_divergence, require_mutual_nn) and the same frame and global caps (0.0076, 0.00375). The DeepCenter image veto is deliberately omitted: it needs the volumes and it GATES candidates rather than ORDERING them, so it is orthogonal to the ranking question. tools/exp20_div_sweep_local.py loads their prediction .geff, strips to one child per source, re-adds divisions under a given ranker and scores against our GT. VERIFIED END TO END on the three graphs produced locally: 205 divisions added, edgeJ 0.9310, score 0.9324. It cannot yet DISCRIMINATE rankers because those three 44b6 datasets contain no ground-truth divisions - divJ is nan with tp/fp/fn 0/0/0. Only 87 of 199 datasets carry a GT division at all, and they hold 151 between them, so the sweep needs the full corpus. One fairness fix applied: strip_divisions now keeps the HIGHEST edge_prob child (ties by shorter distance) rather than whichever edge came first, so the common starting graph does not depend on file order."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1310, "key": "THE_THRESHOLD_SURFACE_IS_MINED_OUT_only_the_RANKER_is_untouched", "observed_at": "2026-09-13", "quote": "54 distinct keys; 4 differ | BIOHUB_SAFE_DIV_SISTER_MAX_UM 14.0 14.0 18.0 | BIOHUB_DEEPCENTER_TTA 1 1 - | BIOHUB_SECONDARY_EDGE_FEATURE_TTA 1 1 -", "review_after": null, "source_id": 156, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Diffing the three clean 0.947-family kernels we hold (sjlee101/biohub-lf-dctta 0.947, flexonafft/biohub-harmonic-fusion 0.947, sjlee101/biohub-hf-sister18 0.946) shows they differ in only FOUR of 54 explicit settings. Fifty are identical consensus. TWO CONSEQUENCES. (1) THE THRESHOLD SURFACE IS MINED OUT: many people have converged on the same 50 values, so further threshold tuning is low-yield - which is exactly why the RANKING RULE, which none of them varies, is the right target. (2) OUR OWN sister value was already tested at real density and lost: hf-sister18 sets SAFE_DIV_SISTER_MAX_UM=18.0 - the value EXP-7b derived as our saturation point - and scores 0.946 against 14.0's 0.947. That is a direct empirical answer to a question our oracle-density sweeps could not settle, and it says 14.0 is right at real density. The other three differing keys are TTA switches (DEEPCENTER_TTA, SECONDARY_EDGE_FEATURE_TTA at weight 0.75), which the 0.946 variant has off - weak evidence that the TTA is worth about a point in the third decimal."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1311, "key": "exp21_THEIR_GATES_REJECT_75PCT_OF_REAL_DIVISIONS", "observed_at": "2026-09-13", "quote": "KAGGLE ground truth, IN DOMAIN: 151 divisions | SAFE_DIV_SISTER_SYMMETRY_TAU = 0.6 rejects 37.7% | SAFE_DIV_DIVERGE_UM = 2.25 rejects 48.3% | ALL GATES INCLUDING DIVERGENCE rejects 74.8%", "review_after": null, "source_id": 157, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "EXP-21 prices every gate in the 0.947 division selector by the only currency that matters: what fraction of REAL divisions it discards. Measured IN DOMAIN on Kaggle's own 151 ground-truth divisions, their gates collectively reject 74.8% of them BEFORE any ranking happens. Individually: SISTER_SYMMETRY_TAU 0.6 rejects 37.7%, DIVERGE_UM 2.25 rejects 48.3%, MAX_UM 9.0 rejects 19.9%, SISTER_MAX_UM 14.0 rejects 12.6%, EXISTING_CHILD_MAX_UM 10.0 rejects 0.7%. The same audit over 761,849 Zebrahub divisions (all five films, once the differing schemas were normalised - ZSNS001_tail names the column ParentTrackID and types it as text) gives 87-88%, and 54.9% for symmetry alone, so the direction is corroborated out of domain even though the magnitudes differ. THIS CORRECTS OUR OWN STRATEGY. We had inferred from the global cap matching the biological rate (0.00375 vs 0.00391) that this was a SELECTION problem and that reranking was the lever. If the gates admit only ~25% of real divisions, the cap probably never binds, their divJ of 0.231 is closer to pure RECALL at high precision, and no reranking can recover what the gates already discarded. RECALL, VIA THE GATES, IS THE LEVER - and the two worst offenders are exactly the two nobody in the 0.947 family varies. Both are env-settable: BIOHUB_SAFE_DIV_SISTER_SYMMETRY_TAU and BIOHUB_SAFE_DIV_DIVERGE_UM."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1312, "key": "exp21b_THE_COMBINED_PLAY_loosen_gates_THEN_rank", "observed_at": "2026-09-13", "quote": "current gates (74.8% rejected) admits 25.2% p=1.0->divJ 0.252 | drop divergence only (48.3% -> 0) admits 48.7% p=1.0->divJ 0.487 p=0.8->divJ 0.355", "review_after": null, "source_id": 158, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The two division findings COMPOSE rather than compete, and the arithmetic says so. If their divJ is recall-limited, their current gates at PERFECT precision would yield divJ 0.252 - almost exactly the 0.231 they publish, which is strong evidence the recall reading is right and precision is already near 1. Dropping the divergence gate alone raises the admitted fraction 25.2% -> 48.7%, worth divJ 0.487 at p=1.0, 0.355 at p=0.8 (+0.012 of score), 0.245 at p=0.6. Dropping symmetry alone gives 0.404 / 0.299 / 0.209. BUT PRECISION WILL NOT HOLD when you admit twice the candidates, and that is precisely where the ranker and the cap earn their place: loosen the gates to recover recall, rank the larger pool well (EXP-19: our learned ranker beats their two-term rule by +0.079 AUC held out), and let the global cap enforce precision. A ranker is worth most when there is a surplus of candidates to order - which their gates currently prevent from existing. SEQUENCE THAT FOLLOWS: loosen, then rank, then let the cap bind - and measure all three offline on their own graphs rather than spending a submission per variant."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1313, "key": "THEIR_OWN_DIAGNOSTICS_CONFIRM_25PCT_DIVISION_RECALL", "observed_at": "2026-09-13", "quote": "config tight55 adjusted_edge_jaccard 0.928 division_jaccard 0.2308 div_tp 3 div_fp 1 div_fn 9 | wrong_association_edges 0 | missed_gt_nodes 63 spurious_pred_nodes 182971 edges_recovered 5539 edges_fragmented 133 edges_lost_to_detection 79", "review_after": null, "source_id": 159, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "division", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE STRONGEST EVIDENCE IN THE CAMPAIGN, and it came free from our own repro run's diagnostics. Their kernel runs an in-kernel post-process sweep over 8 HELD-OUT TRAIN stems (4x 44b6, 4x 6bba) and reports a full division confusion per config. In ALL EIGHT configs: div_tp 3, div_fp 1, div_fn 9, divJ 3/13 = 0.2308. That is division RECALL of 25.0% at precision 75%. EXP-21 predicted, from Kaggle ground-truth GEOMETRY alone and with no access to their pipeline, that their gates admit 25.2% of real divisions. Their actual measured recall is 25.0% - two independent methods agreeing to 0.2 percentage points. CONSEQUENCES. (1) Their division term is overwhelmingly RECALL-limited: 9 false negatives against 1 false positive. (2) THE GLOBAL CAP NEVER BINDS - they emit 4 divisions where 0.00375 of edges would allow far more - so our earlier 'selection problem' framing was wrong and reranking is NOT the first lever; loosening the gates is. (3) divJ is PINNED at 0.2308 across all 8 configs of their own sweep, so every knob they tune moves only the edge term, exactly as our earlier registry fact claimed. (4) wrong_association_edges = 0: their linker makes ZERO wrong associations on these stems, so edge loss is fragmentation (133) and detection (79), not bad linking - which independently corroborates EXP-17's finding that the lost edges are unrecoverable rather than mis-selected. (5) spurious_pred_nodes 182,971 against missed_gt_nodes 63: they flood nodes almost for free, because an edge between two unmatched nodes cannot be charged as a false positive."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 1314, "key": "WE_KNOW_THEIR_EXACT_VALIDATION_SET_and_proxy", "observed_at": "2026-09-13", "quote": "held_out_stems: 44b6_12dfb391, 44b6_267148e4, 44b6_2a2eff9f, 44b6_341df25f, 6bba_062c8d37, 6bba_07e24132, 6bba_085bf656, 6bba_09961292 | base_proxy 0.9490380316323633 -> selected tight55 0.9510954147638787 {'MOTION_RELINK_TIGHT_UM': 5.5}", "review_after": null, "source_id": 160, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "ops", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Our repro run leaked their tuning setup, and it is directly reusable. The 0.947 kernel tunes itself IN-KERNEL against a fixed set of 8 held-out TRAIN stems - 4x 44b6 and 4x 6bba - computing a proxy score and selecting the best of 8 candidate configs. On our run it chose 'tight55' (MOTION_RELINK_TIGHT_UM 5.5), lifting the proxy from 0.94904 to 0.95110. THE PROXY TRACKS THE LEADERBOARD: their base proxy of 0.9490 against a published 0.947 is within 0.002, so it is a usable offline objective and we hold the ground truth for all 8 stems locally. TWO CAUTIONS. Their 8 stems contain only 12 GT divisions between them (div_tp 3 + div_fn 9), so tuning DIVISION behaviour on that set is far too noisy - division work needs the full corpus, where 87 of 199 datasets carry 151 divisions. And the set is balanced 4/4 across embryos rather than leave-one-embryo-out, so it cannot detect the embryo-transfer failure that our LOEO discipline is designed to catch."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 1315, "key": "WHY_NOBODY_IMPROVES_DIVISIONS_they_cannot_MEASURE_them", "observed_at": "2026-09-13", "quote": "TOTAL across their 8 held-out stems: 12 GT divisions | div_tp 3 div_fp 1 div_fn 9 | division_jaccard 0.2308 in all 8 configs", "review_after": null, "source_id": 161, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "VALID", "validity_reason": null, "value": "THE EXPLANATION FOR THE WHOLE PLATEAU, and it is our opening. The 0.947 kernel tunes itself against 8 held-out train stems that contain TWELVE ground-truth divisions between them - verified by counting our own GT: 1,1,1,1,1,2,1,4. With 12 divisions and integer confusion counts, division_jaccard is quantised brutally: they sit at 3/13 = 0.2308 and the next reachable value is 4/13 = 0.3077. ANY DIVISION IMPROVEMENT SMALLER THAN A WHOLE EXTRA TRUE POSITIVE IS INVISIBLE TO THEIR TUNING LOOP. That is exactly what their sweep shows - divJ is pinned at 0.2308 across all eight configs while adjusted_edge_jaccard moves 0.9260 to 0.9280 - and it explains why the entire public field is frozen at the same division score and why every knob anyone tunes moves only the edge term. It is not that divisions are hard to improve; it is that nobody can SEE an improvement. OUR EDGE IS MEASUREMENT: we score divisions on 151 across 199 datasets, leave-one-embryo-out, with an offline harness that costs seconds per variant. They measure on 12, balanced 4/4 across embryos rather than LOEO, once per 2-hour kernel run."}
