{"choice": "Hybrid: build an honest detector+linker, then TUNE the detection threshold on the node-count ratio as a legitimate calibrated parameter. Do not ship a pure hack.", "id": 1, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Do we ride the node-count 'metric hack' to the top of the public LB?", "rationale": "The adjustment is part of the published metric and calibrating N_pred is legitimate tuning. But a submission whose ONLY source of score is metric gaming is exposed by the winner obligations (training+inference code, reproducible methodology, recorded call) and by rules 3.148 (unfair playing practices). The host has already patched one exploit mid-competition and rescored. Pure-hack notebooks are also 7-minute runs with no model to defend.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Upload weights as a Kaggle Dataset/Model; notebook runs with internet off and loads them.", "id": 2, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Train on Mac, infer on Kaggle - how do weights cross?", "rationale": "onlyAllowKernelSubmissions=True and internet is disabled in the notebook, so the Mac can never touch the submission path directly.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "FOCUS-3D pseudo-labels first. Then royerlab's BSD-3 torchscript weights. NOT the synthetic set, NOT Zebrahub tracks, NOT CTC.", "id": 3, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Which external data do we add?", "rationale": "FOCUS-3D is the only external source with a MEASURED result on this task - a competitor trained with zero Kaggle annotation and got edge recall 0.84-0.99 (mean 0.93) on held-out annotated samples, training 200 epochs without the overfitting that kills sparse-label training at 10. royerlab publish unet-simview.pt and unet-daxi.pt under BSD-3, free and unused. The 18.5GB synthetic set is CC0 and legal but its OWN AUTHOR measured all three fusions BELOW plain DoG-max (0.82/0.75/0.827 vs 0.836), all its lineage supervision is in 6-frame 64^3 crops, and its full-resolution volumes carry centroids with no graph. Zebrahub is host-approved but its tracks are Ultrack pseudo-labels with systematic errors ESPECIALLY around divisions - the exact thing we would be training - and no Zebrahub volume matches the Kaggle voxel scale. CTC forbids cloning, which an internet-disabled rerun requires.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "SOLO. No recruitment. Merge deadline 2026-09-22 passes unused.", "id": 4, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Team size - do we recruit?", "rationale": "User's decision, 2026-09-11. Consistent with the evidence: nine of the top ten teams have 2 members, submissions are per-team at 5/day so headcount buys no extra attempts, and the real gap is iteration (top-20 average 93 submissions against our 4), which a second person does not fix inside 11 days.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "POLICY fixed now, choice deferred to 28 Sep: one highest-public-LB run, and one lowest-variance run with the fewest post-process stages. Never two variants of the same configuration.", "id": 5, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Which 2 final submissions do we select?", "rationale": "Public LB is 29% of test and ~400 teams sit inside 0.002, so public rank is close to noise. Ties break on earliest submission, meaning a score-neutral resubmission at the plateau strictly loses rank. The prior campaign's offline proxy repeatedly failed to predict LB DIRECTION (+0.0009 offline vs +0.003 actual; +0.002 offline vs -0.014 actual), so neither offline CV nor public LB alone is trustworthy - splitting the two selections across that disagreement is the only hedge available.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Division detection. Drive division_jaccard from ~0 toward 0.25+ on top of a solid edge baseline.", "id": 6, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "What is the primary honest lever to rank 1?", "rationale": "Gap/weight arithmetic puts the implied rank-1 division_jaccard at ~0.235, matching a reported 0.22. Divisions are ~1 link in 853 so this is a rare-event detection problem, which is exactly what the community 18.5GB synthetic set with 165k labelled divisions is for. Prior campaigns never closed this.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "YES for CPU work at a ~6% throttle tax; NO for training.", "id": 7, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Is the HP laptop usable as a second machine during the sprint?", "rationale": "Measured over 12.1h of sustained real load: 5.79% of wall time throttled, against 0.11% in the earlier light 3h session. Still holding 3,976 MHz at 69 C, well above the 1.8 GHz base. So the handoff's 'throttled to 1.4 GHz' premise is wrong, but so was the optimistic 0.11% reading - that was a 61-second test. It earns its keep on data prep, local scoring and packaging while the Mac and Colab do the GPU work.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Kaggle T4 x2 for long training; the Mac for data prep, ablations, evaluation and packaging - run both at once.", "id": 8, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Where do the long training runs actually go?", "rationale": "M5 Pro is ~33-35 TFLOPS dense FP16 (est.) vs a single T4's 65, so Kaggle's T4x2 is ~4x the Mac for raw throughput, and bandwidth is at parity (307 vs 300 GB/s). The Mac's real edge is 48GB unified with no copies and no 12h wall. Plan patch size for the 16GB T4, not the 48GB Mac, or inference will not fit.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Upsample + Conv3d. Never ConvTranspose3d.", "id": 9, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Detector decoder architecture?", "rationale": "ConvTranspose3d on MPS rejects fp16/bf16 and crashes autocast; fix PR still open. Costs nothing in model quality and removes the only hard MPS blocker.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "NO. One framework: PyTorch, device selected at runtime (mps / cuda / cpu).", "id": 10, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Do we adopt MLX for the Mac GPU, alongside CUDA for Kaggle?", "rationale": "Apple states PyTorch already reaches the M5 Neural Accelerators under the hood, so MLX is NOT required to use the 20-core GPU. MLX cannot produce a submission (Kaggle has no Metal); torch 2.14 erased its one real advantage by taking conv3d to 97% of matmul peak; an open MLX issue has it 10-20% SLOWER than PyTorch on M5 bf16 GEMM; and a dual stack costs two implementations plus numerical-agreement checks in an 18-day runway. The sprint handoff had already decided this independently.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Colab A100 for heavy training; Kaggle T4x2 for free parallel runs and submission-identical numerics; Mac for everything with no marginal cost.", "id": 11, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Which machine does the heavy training?", "rationale": "The Mac is the slowest of the three for raw training. Colab A100 is ~10x it. The Mac's value is zero-cost unlimited iteration, 48GB, and no disconnects - so it owns data prep, the classical baseline, metric work, ablations, evaluation and packaging.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "No. Only the detector is GPU-bound. Linking is combinatorial (bipartite matching / ILP) and CPU-bound, and the podium gap is the division term, which is a rare-event modelling problem.", "id": 12, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Is GPU compute actually our binding constraint?", "rationale": "Adding A100 hours cannot fix a model that does not detect divisions. Budget compute to the detector and spend thinking time on divisions.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Re-gate the division fork proposer: SAFE_DIV_MAX_UM 4.7->10, SAFE_DIV_SISTER_MAX_UM 7.2->15, and replace the ranking score with one DECREASING in divisionness (-cos_angle + lambda*divergence persistence).", "id": 13, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "What is the single highest-value action in the sprint?", "rationale": "Measured: current gates admit only 10 of 89 metric-reachable divisions; widened they admit 81. fork_at_matched_mother is literally 0 today. CPU-only, hours, no retrain, no GPU, no new data. The entire public field carries the same constants, which is why the 0.9465 plateau has divJ~0 - so this is both the gap AND a differentiator nobody else has fixed.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Yes - archived (read-only), not deleted. Its measured burn rates and parity-failure lessons are extracted into the base.", "id": 14, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Retire biohub-colab-relay now that the official Colab CLI works?", "rationale": "The CLI supersedes every function the relay performed and is maintained by Google. Deleting would destroy the 5 parity-failure notebooks and the only measured unit data on this account, for zero benefit - archiving already stops it being used. The one capability lost is per-job unit accounting, which the CLI does not provide.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "One contract in AGENTS.md. Both write to db/biohub_base.db under its rules; NEITHER writes to ~/.claude/. Serialise writes: git pull + sync_source_rows.py check before starting, export + commit + push after.", "id": 15, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "How do two agents share the knowledge base without corrupting it?", "rationale": "SQLite is single-writer even in WAL mode, and the irreplaceable rows live only in db/source_rows.jsonl because the .db is gitignored. `check` is the gate that catches an unexported peer. The ~/.claude boundary holds because that store is machine-agnostic and Claude-session-scoped, while project knowledge belongs in the repo.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "A100 only, for jobs that need >16GB or are bandwidth-bound. Never L4. Never T4 on Colab - use Kaggle's free T4 x2 instead.", "id": 16, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Which Colab GPU do we actually buy?", "rationale": "L4 costs 2.4x a T4 for identical 300 GB/s bandwidth and only 8GB more VRAM - the worst point on the curve. A100's advantage is not FLOPS/unit (it is the WORST at 20.8) but 1555 GB/s and 40GB, which is what 3D convolution training is bound by, plus wall-clock: 13x faster means 13x more experiments in an 18-day runway.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "0.980, which requires division_jaccard = 0.335.", "id": 17, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "What is the north star score?", "rationale": "That is 57% of the measured post-processing-only ceiling of 0.589, reachable without a single new detection - only by emitting the second mother-daughter edge at mothers whose daughters are already detected and matched. Today's rank 1 (0.970) implies divJ ~0.235, so 0.980 clears the current field by a margin rather than tying it.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Replace the division fork RANKING with one decreasing in divisionness (-cos_angle + lambda*divergence-persistence). Keep the public stack's already-wide gates (9/14) and its cap; neither is binding.", "id": 18, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Revised: what is the single highest-value change?", "rationale": "Supersedes the earlier 'widen the gates' framing, which was true of the 0.918 stack. The current 0.947 public stack already widened them but still ranks cheapest-first, so a 3.2x fork budget is spent on the tightest - and therefore wrongest - candidates. The ordering defect survived into the current public lineage and nobody has fixed it.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Port the honest edge pipeline (adj_edge ~0.945; weights public in pilkwang/biohub-tracking-support-pack-50ep-v1) and build a GENUINE division predictor to divJ 0.25-0.35.", "id": 19, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Final target, decomposed: what must we actually build?", "rationale": "The hack is division-only, so the 0.947->0.970 spread is entirely the division term and 0.1 is its hard ceiling. Honest edge 0.945 + divJ 0.25 ties rank 1; +0.335 reaches 0.980. The measured post-processing-only ceiling is 0.589. Genuine division detection - not better linking - is the whole remaining headroom.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Attack BOTH terms. Port the 0.947 recipe as the edge baseline (it is one codebase with BIOHUB_* env vars), then work divisions - where the whole field's tuning is frozen - via fork PROPOSAL capacity, not filtering.", "id": 20, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Final strategy, after the real decomposition", "rationale": "The plateau decomposes as edge 0.926 + divJ 0.231, not edge 0.945 + divJ 0. Headroom is near-equal (+0.074 / +0.077) so neither term alone reaches 0.980. Divisions are the better marginal lever because every public post-process sweep leaves divJ pinned while moving only edges, and because the public cascade can only subtract forks, never propose them: 111 safe divisions across 8 embryos yield 3 TP. Trackastra forks natively and has never been paired with the support-pack detector.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "NO - do not prune. Whole-track cuts are neutral at best (+0.001 at 10%) and scattered cuts are catastrophic (-0.163 at 10%).", "id": 21, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Does a 44.7% node cut really score neutral? (node-count multiplier)", "rationale": "Measured sweep on the patched scorer. We already predict 0.905 of T_true, so the multiplier has at most +0.09 relative left and edge loss overtakes it past ~10%. The competitor's 44.7%-neutral result does not reproduce here.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "RESOLVED: test ships no .geff at all. T_true must be ESTIMATED from image statistics, not read.", "id": 22, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "Does the TEST set ship estimated_number_of_nodes?", "rationale": "Direct inspection of the archive: 408 test members, 0 .geff. estimated_number_of_nodes lives only in train geff metadata.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Dual-head UNet: node_head supervised DENSELY on FOCUS-3D pseudo-centres, refine_head supervised SPARSELY on Kaggle conditional on detected peaks. No augmentation. Train the linker on PREDICTED points, not GT points.", "id": 23, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "What detector do we build?", "rationale": "Sparse labels are the root of the field's training instability - a detection loss on single hard voxels teaches every unannotated real cell to be a hard negative, which is why three people report loss blowing up at ~10 epochs. Dense supervision for detection and sparse only for refinement sidesteps it: measured 18-24% centroid-error reduction, 10-14% peak prune, recall held at 99.5%, and 200 epochs without overfitting. It lands on the two most expensive failure modes in the metric - the sigma~2um centroid cliff and duplicate detections, where one duplicate at 0.4um halves the edge Jaccard. Augmentation measured harmful and doubles epoch time.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Six stages over a mutable Graph: detect -> refine -> score_edges -> resolve -> repair -> write. Every stage returns a StageDelta. One frozen env-overridable Config. Device resolved in one function. Scoring is a stage and always reports adj_edge and divJ separately.", "id": 24, "made_at": "2026-09-11", "question": "What is the system architecture?", "rationale": "Every seam is justified by a measurement rather than taste. detect/refine split because refinement is gated on a >=15% centroid-error reduction (EXP-3: the cliff between 2.0 and 2.5um quadruples loss) and because supervision differs - dense for detection, sparse for refinement. score_edges/resolve split because the candidate set holds 0.966 edge recall while greedy top-1 gets 0.942, so fusing them makes +0.024 unreachable. resolve must fork because EXP-5 showed a 1:1 assignment misses all 151 divisions BY CONSTRUCTION while forking recovers 142 for an edge cost of 0.0002. repair runs after resolve and may not delete forks - the public stack's defining bug. The StageDelta ledger makes that bug visible in one line rather than only through the final score.", "status": "decided", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Re-sweep once detect is trained. Values are set to the EXP-10 optimum (gap_max_frames=0, min_track_len=1) for now, with an inline warning in contracts.py.", "id": 25, "made_at": "2026-09-12", "question": "Should repair (close_gaps / prune_short) stay disabled in the submission pipeline?", "rationale": "EXP-10 measured both ops as net-harmful (+0.0042 to disable) but ONLY under oracle detection, where there are no detector misses to bridge and no spurious short tracks to prune. BOTH premises reverse under a real detector, so the measured optimum is not transferable to the submission pipeline. This decision stays OPEN until detect is trained and the sweep is repeated on real detections.", "status": "open", "tbl": "decision"}
{"choice": "Hold; validation and packaging gates remain open.", "id": 26, "made_at": "2026-09-12T20:13:05.725018+00:00", "question": "May the campaign launch paid model compute after this review?", "rationale": "Follow notes/FULL-REVIEW-2026-09-12.md. Correct density benchmark; enforce provenance; prove image-to-CSV and offline Kaggle path before a bounded device pilot.", "status": "open", "tbl": "decision"}
